“Most women Liam keeps around are docile little things, happy to be led. But you?” I let my gaze drift, just for a second. “You like to bite back. Even when you shouldn’t.”

A slow, involuntary shiver ran through her. I smiled, “That makes you worth watching.”
I let Caroline stew in silence for a few moments, letting the weight of her mistake settle into her limbs, letting the whispers still crawl up the back of her neck.

The orchestra played on, oblivious to the small scandal unraveling between crystal glasses and champagne-stained smiles. The party carried forward, conversation blooming over hors d’oeuvres and cigars, but Caroline? She almost looked lost.

When I reached for her hand, she didn’t resist.

“Come along, princess,” I murmured, my voice silk and thorns as I led her onto the dance floor. “It’s easier to talk this way.”

She followed, reluctant but pliant, her fingers resting stiffly in mine. “I should be dancing with Liam,” she said, but the challenge was weak, unconvincing.

I smirked. “And yet… he’s not here to defend your honor.”

My gaze drifted lazily across the room, slow and unhurried, like I had all the time in the world to notice the obvious. “That’s odd,” I murmured, scanning once, then again with exaggerated calm. “He was just over there, wasn’t he?”

A pause. I let my eyes keep moving—past the bar, the staircase, the French doors leading out to the patio. Nowhere urgent. Nowhere public.

Brows lifted slightly, voice lower now.

“Huh. Not seeing him.”

I gave it another beat, like I might spot something—anything—before letting the silence stretch too long.

“Maybe he slipped away.” A shrug, almost careless. “You know how these nights go. Too many rooms, too many pretty distractions.”

Then, as if it had just occurred to me, I glanced to the far side of the room—where Liam stood, poised and composed, deep in conversation with Senator Harrington.

I smiled, slow and cutting.

“Ah. Never mind. There he is. Playing statesman.” My eyes flicked back to her.
“Like a good little heir.” Her fingers tensed in mine, but she didn’t argue.

“Don’t worry, I’m sure he’ll come to your rescue when you need it — oh wait.” It was too easy to tease a glare from her.

“And then he runs off,” she avoided my eyes. There it was: resentment? Disappointment? Fear?

The music dictated our movements, the rhythm slow, deliberate—something measured, like all things in this world. I led, and she followed, even if I could feel her resistance simmering just beneath the surface. She didn’t want to owe me anything, not even a dance, which made this all the more fun.

I let the silence stretch, making her wait for it, making her feel the weight of my attention. And then, finally, I leaned in.

“Shall I entertain you, princess?” I murmured, just beside her ear, watching the way the words sent a shiver down her spine. “Since I seem to be the only one willing to show you around this little den of predators.”

She rolled her eyes. “By entertain, you mean gossip?”

I huffed a quiet laugh, adjusting my grip at her waist. “It’s not gossip if it’s true.”

Her lips parted slightly, hesitation flickering across her face, but she stayed silent. Listening, leaning in ever so slightly.

I tilted my head just enough to glance at James Harrington Jr.—Jamie, leaning against the bar, all crisp lines and effortless charisma, talking to one of the Devereaux daughters.

“Jamie,” I mused, just loud enough for her to hear. “Golden boy. The good son. Future Congressman, future Senator, future President if his father has his way. He smiles like he already knows the election results.”

Caroline glanced toward him.

“His biggest flaw?” I continued smoothly. “He thinks he’s invincible. And that makes him careless. Ask anyone who’s ever spent a weekend in the Hamptons with him, and you’ll hear stories of substances that never make it into the press.” I spun her effortlessly, pulling her back in before whispering, “But don’t worry, princess—his father’s donations always keep his record clean and any victims behind closed doors, where they belong.” The breath hitched in her throat—soft, barely audible, but there. She was quick to mask it, smoothing out her expression, but I caught it. That moment of weakness. The slip. Liam had taught her well, but not well enough. She could still be startled

She didn’t like how much I knew. I hope she wondered about just how much I knew about her.

I let the smirk linger as I shifted my gaze toward Lucien Devereaux, standing near the balcony doors, wine glass in hand, his presence commanding as ever.

“And then we have Lucien,” I murmured, keeping my voice smooth, amused. “Foreign. Charming. Absolutely lethal when it comes to women.”

Caroline hesitated before glancing toward him.

“He’s been engaged three times,” I continued, leading her effortlessly through another turn. “Broke off every single one. You’d think he gets bored, but really, he just likes to watch women unravel. He drips romance until they fall in love, and then he disappears just to see how hard they’ll beg.” Her breath caught—just for a second, just long enough for me to notice. She was absorbing my words, turning them over in that sharp little mind of hers.

She knew why I was telling her this. She knew exactly what I was insinuating. I smirked, it was almost too easy.
I shifted my gaze toward Beckett Montgomery, who had a hand on the waist of some European socialite, his smile all easy charm and practiced ease.

“And Beckett,” I murmured, watching as Caroline followed my line of sight. “Ah, Beckett. The good ol’ boy. Former Army Ranger, current heir to the Montgomery dynasty.” I let my lips curl. “He flirts because it’s expected of him, not because he actually gives a damn. Every move is practiced, every romance pre-approved by his parents.”

Caroline exhaled slowly. “And what’s your opinion of all this?”

I chuckled. “I don’t have opinions, Greer.” I dipped her slightly, just enough to make her pulse stutter. “I just know things.”

She swallowed.

“But these are only rumors,” I added, letting the words slip through my teeth like smoke. She knew I was lying, and she also knew I wasn’t.

We continued to move, the music swelling around us, our steps smooth, seamless. She was too easy to move, too easy to bend. Petite, delicate—designed for men like Liam, men who liked to carve their names into soft things. And yet, there was something underneath. A spine that hadn’t quite been broken. If I had met her first, I wondered how quickly I could break her, not in half steps, but fully and unmistakably.

She was learning, or maybe thinking. Knowing her, probably overthinking, which meant it was time to start talking.

“Relax,” I murmured, my grip adjusting slightly at her waist. “This is supposed to be enjoyable.”

Her gaze flicked up to mine, did I detect a spark of playfulness? “Dancing with you isn’t exactly on my Christmas wish list, Ethan.” A breath away from a laugh. I couldn’t tell if she was more annoyed at him or at me; I’d mark that as progress.

“Shame.” I smirked, spinning her into the next turn. “Liam could’ve at least warned you I’m better at this than he is. You, however, are an absolute treat of a dance partner. Did you learn this in the backwoods of Pennsylvania or did Liam give you a crash course?”

Caroline exhaled sharply through her nose, “I’m not some hick, Cooper.” “Never said you were, darling.”
“Want to hear more?” I mused. “Maybe about your new favorite person?”

The edge of her jaw tensed—not enough for anyone else to notice, but enough for me. She was annoyed. Irritated. Trying not to let it show. I liked that. It meant she still had some fight left in her. Liam must have enjoyed that part, too. No one liked breaking something that had already been shattered, “She’s pathetic.”

“That she is.” “And desperate.”
“Mmm.” I hummed in agreement. “Though not for the reasons you think.” That got her attention.
Her grip on my shoulder tightened slightly, her curiosity flickering past her irritation. “What do you mean?”
I smirked, dipping my head slightly as I leaned in, as if what I was about to say was something intimate.

“Her name is Celeste Dupont.” Caroline blinked. “French?”

“Swiss,” I corrected. “Old money. Good breeding. Entitled as hell.”

She didn’t react—at least, not outwardly. But I caught the slight pause, the way her grip on my shoulder flexed just enough to betray her thoughts.

“It was never serious,” I continued, voice smooth, almost lazy. “Not to Liam, anyway. What word did he use to use? ‘Convenient?’ Or was it ‘temporary?’ I guess she never got the memo. Possession is a funny thing—some people don’t mind losing until someone else picks up what they left behind.”

That landed. A flicker in Caroline’s expression, a shift so small it might have been missed by anyone who wasn’t looking for it, but I was. Satisfaction. Relief. Something sharp and possessive beneath the surface. There you are, princess.

“I see,” she murmured, her voice too smooth, too composed. I challenged, “Do you?”
Her gaze flickered, her mask slipping just enough to show the truth—she did see. And she liked what she saw. I exhaled a quiet laugh and she relaxed a bit more in my arms. I could work with this. We danced in silence for a beat, her breath evening out, the jealousy dulling into something else. She had just let her guard down and now for some real fun.

“Since we’re on the topic of Barnettes,” I drawled, “I’m curious—what do you make of Michael?”

She blinked up at me. “What about him?”
“You’ve been watching him.” “I have not.”
“Caroline.”

She exhaled, annoyed. “Fine. He’s… interesting.”

“Interesting,” I repeated, amused. “That’s a polite way of saying he’s the only one in this family who gets to enjoy himself.”

Caroline’s lips parted slightly. She wasn’t ready for that. “Liam called him weak,” she said, voice softer.
“Liam isn’t wrong,” I murmured, spinning her into the next turn before pulling her back in. “But weakness and enjoyment aren’t mutually exclusive.”

Caroline hesitated, something almost hesitant flickering across her face.

So, of course, I pushed further.

“Did you ever hear about the Senator’s wife from New York?” I mused. Caroline blinked up at me. “Which one?”
“Exactly.”

She sighed through her nose.

“She was young,” I continued, as if that explained everything. “A second wife. Much prettier than the first. The kind of woman who knew how to stand next to power without outshining it. Except…” I smirked, “she got bored.”

Caroline’s brows furrowed slightly. “And?”
“And Michael is very good at alleviating boredom.”

She inhaled slowly, processing. Waiting for the part that mattered.

“It was discreet at first,” I said, watching her closely. “A lingering touch here. A whispered conversation there. He took her to a hotel in Georgetown once—made sure she arrived in the lobby alone, left alone, separate rooms on the booking. They were careful.”

She pressed her lips together, too tightly to be casual. “Then they weren’t.” Her fingers tightened slightly against my shoulder.
“She called him after a gala,” I continued, my voice low, almost conspiratorial. “Drunk. Irritated. Her husband had ignored her all night. So she told Michael to meet her at her townhouse. Said it was safe. Said no one would know.”

Caroline exhaled. “But someone did.” “Of course someone did.”
She hesitated. “And what happened to her?”

“Oh, she’s still married,” I said, smirking. “To the public, at least. But she spends most of her time in Europe now, under the guise of ‘philanthropic work.’ The Senator was… encouraged to look the other way.”

Caroline looked stunned. “And Michael?” she asked.
“Michael was never a problem. She was.”

We continued to dance, she was a natural following my lead, never mixing up steps and so easy to control.

“Do you want to hear about the time Michael was politely asked to leave the Vatican?” I mused, my grip steady as I led Caroline through another effortless step.

Caroline blinked. “What?”

“Mmm,” I hummed, clearly savoring her reaction. “It was during one of those charity tours he does to keep the press friendly. He got invited to a private event with some of the top- ranking clergy—big deal, extremely formal, all about optics.”

Caroline’s brow furrowed. “And what did he do?”

I smirked. “Apparently, he got bored halfway through and started flirting with the daughter of a foreign diplomat. By the end of the night, they were caught in a restricted section of the archives—very restricted.”

Caroline’s breath hitched. “You’re lying.” “Would I lie to you?”
“What happened?”

“Oh, nothing, of course.” I shrugged. “A generous donation was made, a few key people were… persuaded to forget, and Michael got a firm ‘we would prefer if you didn’t return’ from a member of the clergy.”

Caroline exhaled sharply. “He was banned from the Vatican?”

“We prefer to say discouraged from visiting again,” I corrected. “A minor difference.” Caroline stumbled, but it was easy for me to hide the blunder as we continued dancing. I grinned.
“Careful, princess,” I murmured, tightening my grip at her waist to steady her. “Wouldn’t want you falling over the scandal of it all.”

Her cheeks were turning pink now, but laughed along with me - like old friends reminiscing over some timeless joke.

“Michael is many things,” I continued smoothly, “but dull? Never.”

Caroline exhaled, clearly debating whether or not to ask for more. I could tell she was waiting for it. She wouldn’t ask, of course. Not directly. But I could see it in the way her fingers curled slightly against my shoulder, the way her breath hitched just a little too sharply every time I fed her another piece of the story.

Caroline wanted to know. And I? I wanted to see what she’d do with it.

“Tell me something else,” she murmured, her voice just barely above the music. I smirked. “You’re enjoying this.”
“I’m horrified.” She gave a sly smile back. “And yet.”
She gave a casual shrug, a good attempt at indifference. Someone else may have believed it, but indifference wasn’t real — not in places like this, not between people like us. The only ones who mastered it were the ones who didn’t survive the game long enough to make it matter.

“Alright, princess,” I murmured, spinning her into a slow turn before pulling her back in. “Let’s talk more about the affairs.”

Her cheeks flushed a lovely shade of pink - I could see what Liam saw in her. It was so easy to make her blush and squirm, even months after Liam sunk his claws into her. I wondered what she was like before him; shaking like a leaf, her skin the color of cherry blossoms.

“I’ll give you a fun one,” I mused, twirling her effortlessly. “You remember that pop star who went to ‘rehab for exhaustion’ a few years ago?”

Caroline smirked. “Yes?”

I grinned. “Guess who she was with for a full seventy-two hours before that happened?” Her breath caught. “You’re joking.”
“Oh, Caroline, I wish I were. Michael met her in Ibiza. Three days later, she was being escorted onto a private jet in dark sunglasses and a hoodie, and Michael was getting a very firm phone call from his father.”

“And?”

“And?” I echoed, amused. “She got a PR recovery arc. Michael got a slap on the wrist and an invitation to her comeback tour.”

Caroline’s hazel eyes widened, sparking from the candlelight that danced between champagne glasses and crystal chandeliers, her fingers tightening slightly on his shoulder. “This is the part where you laugh like I told you the funniest joke. Gotta keep up appearances, princess” She giggled charmingly. She had a nice laugh, fake or otherwise.

“You think he’ll ever settle down?” she asked finally, voice quieter. I exhaled a short laugh.
“Michael?” I shook my head, smirking. “Michael isn’t meant for things that last. He lives fast, fucks faster, and wakes up clean because someone else takes care of the mess.”

Her throat worked in silence.

I leaned in slightly, lowering my voice. “He gets all the fun, Greer.” One breath. Shallow. Controlled. “And Liam gets to watch.”
She turned her head sharply toward Liam—not a movement she thought about, but an instinctual reaction.

Now the question was in her head. And the best part? I didn’t even have to be the one to answer it.

“Ask him,” I murmured, brushing my thumb lightly against the back of her hand as I spun her into the final step of the dance. “Ask Liam what it feels like to never get to indulge.”

“Tell me more about Liam.”

She looked up at me through thick lashes, voice smooth. A challenge.

I smirked. Alright, Greer. Let’s see how much you really want to know.

I glanced over her shoulder, scanning the room until I found him—our golden boy, locked into conversation with the people who mattered, completely unaware of what I was about to give her. He should have been watching.

“That’s a dangerous request, princess,” I murmured, leading her through the next step of the waltz.

“I can handle it.” “Can you?”
Her nails pressed just slightly into my shoulder.

“Try me.” Her sun-warmed moss eyes met mine, steady, unwavering. A challenge? Maybe. Or perhaps she hadn’t learned yet that looking someone like me in the eye was an invitation. A dare. And I always played with my food before I ate it.

I chuckled softly, tilting my head.

“Alright,” I mused, lowering my voice just enough to make her lean in. “Let’s talk about the first time Liam realized rules didn’t apply to him.”

Her breath stalled—just barely, just enough that I felt it against my chest. It wasn’t shock. Not really. It was something quieter, something creeping in at the edges. I could feel the way she tensed in my hold, the way her fingers curled just slightly against mine before she caught herself. Did Liam notice these things about her? Did he watch the way she breathed, the way she tried to cover it up?

I exhaled slowly, amused. Of course he did. He had already rewired her, already shaped the way she reacted to power. I was just getting a glimpse of the finished product.

“He wanted something,” I continued smoothly. “A win. A position. A title. Something that someone else had.”

Caroline was still keeping her expression perfectly schooled, but I could feel the tension in the way she held herself now—like she was bracing for impact.

“The problem?” I murmured, brushing my thumb over the back of her hand as I spun her into the next step. “Someone was in his way.”

Her fingers tightened slightly.

“He didn’t like that,” I went on, watching her carefully. “And Liam doesn’t lose.” A slow inhale.
“So what did he do?” Caroline asked, voice measured, as if she didn’t care about the answer. “He made sure they were eliminated,” I said simply.
Her pulse fluttered against my fingers. A quiet betrayal. A response she couldn’t control, no matter how carefully she held herself.

Did she react like that for him? Did she let herself falter under his hands, under his voice? I almost smirked, almost pitied her. Because I already knew the answer. Of course she did.
She had been something before him—something nervous, something unformed, something that hadn’t yet been claimed. But Liam had made sure she didn’t exist like that anymore.
Now? Now, she was his. Pinned, pretty, and perfectly placed beneath the weight of him.

“Not by force, not directly,” I clarified. “That would’ve been messy. And Liam doesn’t like messy.”

Caroline’s lips parted slightly, like she wanted to say something but hadn’t figured out the right words yet.

I didn’t let her.

“Instead,” I continued smoothly, “he found their weaknesses. He fed them to the right people, in the right places, at the right time. And by the time the dust settled, Liam was the only option left.”

Her throat bobbed, a quick, instinctive swallow. A hesitation. Something she didn’t want me to see. But I caught it

“Who was it?” she asked, voice quieter now.

I smirked, “Does it matter?”

Her grip tightened again. She knew it didn’t.

“He must have done something to deserve it,” she said. I chuckled.
“You want there to be a reason, don’t you?” I murmured. “You want me to tell you that Liam only plays this game when he has to. That it’s only for power, only for control, only when he’s backed into a corner.”

Caroline didn’t answer; she knew better.

“But that’s not how it works,” I continued, lowering my voice slightly. “Liam didn’t do it because he had to. He did it because he could.”

She exhaled, slow and measured, like she was trying to regain her footing. Like she thought she could still hold onto something of herself in this conversation.

I watched her closely, let my grip shift just slightly at her waist. She was still so soft. Still fragile in a way she probably hated.

Liam must have liked that about her. The way she still bristled, still tried to push back even when she knew she’d already lost. He must have enjoyed breaking her in, stripping away those sharp edges until they were only for him.

And I? I enjoyed the fact that there was still a little bit left to break, and now, I gave her more
— the realization: Liam wasn’t just ruthless. He was inevitable. He didn’t take power. He was power, and by the time you realized you were playing his game, you had already lost.

“Why are you telling me this?” Caroline asked, voice barely above a whisper.

I smiled. Up close, she was all soft lines and sharp angles—delicate but defined, the high cut of her cheekbones betraying the sweetness of her small frame. The kind of face men liked to ruin. The kind that made them think about pressing their thumbs beneath a pretty jaw just to see what kind of sound she’d make. Liam’s pretty, delicate thing. I had seen delicate things before. They never stayed delicate for long.

“Because you asked.”

She exhaled, still processing, still absorbing, still trying to understand what this meant for her.

“And,” I added, spinning her into one final, perfect turn before pulling her back in, “because I’m in the holiday spirit.”

A pause. Then, I leaned in slightly, just enough to make sure she felt my words settle under her skin. “Consider this a gift, princess.”

She wasn’t breathing properly now.

“If Liam ever gives you a way out,” I murmured, voice smooth, amused, unreadable, “don’t take it.”

Her entire body went still. I watched the way her lips parted, the way her mind worked through the pieces, trying to decipher whether I was helping her or leading her straight into a trap.

Perfect, confusion suited her. “I believe I’m cutting in.” Liam.
Calm. Collected. Perfectly timed. I smirked, releasing her hand.
“She’s all yours, Barnette.” And let Liam pick up the pieces of whatever I had just planted inside her mind.

Caroline still hadn’t resumed breathing properly now. But me? I grinned, stepping back, bowing slightly. “Lovely dance, Caroline.”
Then I walked away.

And let her figure out for herself whether I had given her a warning… or a command.

