I spun her into a slow turn before pulling her back in, controlling the rhythm, keeping my movements effortless—everything she hadn’t been tonight.

“You cannot behave like that in society, Caroline,” I continued, my voice low, sharp. “You are playing in a world that thrives on composure. The second you show weakness, they will rip you apart.”

She flinched, her weakness once again showing. “I know,” she murmured.
“Do you?”

Her steps faltered slightly, another disappointment.

“You embarrassed yourself tonight,” I said smoothly. Not cruel. Just a fact. Then I leaned in slightly, lowering my voice to something colder.
“And more importantly? You embarrassed me.”

Her cheeks flushed a deep, mortified red while a slow breath caught halfway, then eased out without sound.

*“I—” she started, but I didn’t let her finish.

“You think Celeste matters?” I spun her again, forcing her to move, not letting her retreat into her shame. “You think she’s a threat?”

Caroline swallowed hard. She wasn’t crying, but I could feel the emotion tightening her body.

“She’s nothing,” I said, my voice like ice. “But you? You gave her power. You let her dictate your reactions. You let her win.”

