I clicked my tongue, displeased. “Enough,” I murmured.
Her eyes flickered up to mine, wide, uncertain.

“Hold your head up high,” I instructed, my grip tightening at her waist, forcing her to meet my gaze. “You are practically a Barnette. Act like it.”

Her pulse fluttered against my fingertips.

But this time? She obeyed. She had no other choice.

I exhaled through my nose, leading her through the last movements of the dance, grounding her in the correction.

She had failed tonight, but I would make sure she never made that mistake again.

The music shifted, something slower played from the orchestra, but Caroline had already stilled in my arms. Her spine was stiff, jaw tight. She wasn't sulking—no, this was something colder. Something coiled.

Her gaze didn’t quite meet mine, but her voice was steady when it came.

“If you're finished with your monologue and castigation,” she said quietly, “I'd like to excuse myself.”

The words were polite. The tone was not.

I let a beat pass.

“No,” I said. “You’re expected to remain at the party.”

Her eyes lifted then—finally—and there it was: the defiance, the wound, the fire she didn’t know how to aim. Not yet.

“I have no desire to embarrass you further,” she replied, and the smile she gave me wasn’t a smile at all. “But I’m in no mood to stay on the dance floor with you.”

She started to step back. I didn’t stop her.

I held her gaze as she turned, heels clicking against polished marble, silk dress trailing behind her like smoke.

Let her retreat, if she thought it was on her terms, we both knew how this ended. She would need to raise to the occasion, there was no other option.

Chapter 51.



The Senator's POV. In the Library..

I needed a moment away from the crowds. One can only take so many pleasantries and small talk. These Christmas parties were always for the wives and the young - a chance to gossip, to show off subtly. Us men found our comfort in a fine reserve and well time jokes. It was pageantry - Bread and Circuses.

The library was quieter than I expected. The echoes of the evening still lingered beyond threshold—the distant hum of conversation, the rhythmic clinking of glassware as staff worked with quiet efficiency to erase the remnants of the night. But in here, the air was different. Still. Measured. Private.

A fire burned low in the hearth, stretching flickering shadows across dark-paneled walls, the scent of aged whiskey thick in the air. Old habits died hard, at least once a party, I would escape to the sanctuary of leather and linen—where noise gave way to reflection, where the calculations of the evening could be properly measured.

And yet, tonight, I wasn’t the only one here. Caroline Greer sat curled in one of the armchairs, posture composed but not at ease, a champagne flute balanced between her fingers. I suspected sparkling water and not champagne. She wasn’t sulking, nor was she unraveling.

I stood back, studying her before invading the space. She was thinking. Reconstructing herself. Reassessing. I had seen men do the same after political miscalculations. This wasn’t self-pity. This was course correction. I took a slow sip of my drink, assuring myself I wasn’t projecting. No, the way she studied the fire, held herself, composed herself told me enough.

“May I join you?”

She startled slightly, her grip tensing around the glass. But the recovery was immediate—her back straightened, her chin lifted.

“Of course, sir.”

I stepped further inside, taking the chair opposite her with the ease of a man who had spent decades in rooms like this, across from people just like her—people who wanted something.

She held her glass a little tighter. I noted that she understood the weight of this moment.

“I was surprised to find you in here,” I said, tipping my glass slightly in her direction. “Most young women your age would prefer the ballroom.”

“I needed a moment,” she replied carefully.

I studied her, swirling the whiskey in my glass. “A moment to recover, or a moment to reflect?”
She didn’t rush to answer, I could see quiet calculation behind her gaze.

“A bit of both.” Honesty - either because of trust or vulnerability, perhaps both. I exhaled a quiet chuckle.
“Honesty, when used wisely, is valuable in this world.” Her brow lifted slightly. “Used wisely?”
“Everything has a place,” I said simply. “Even the truth.”

She parted her lips slightly, as if about to challenge that, but thought better of it - a sign of good instincts.

I studied her for a moment longer, then let my gaze drift toward the fire.

I chuckled as a memory came back to me, “You remind me of a woman I once met when I was first entering politics.”

A flicker of surprise. “Oh?”

I nodded, tipping my glass slightly. “It was my first year in Washington. A holiday party, much like this one. I watched a senator’s wife—Massachusetts, I believe—throw a glass of champagne at another woman’s face.”

Her brows lifted.

“Quite the scene,” I continued, shaking my head faintly. “The poor woman gasped so loudly, you’d think she’d been shot. Everyone in the room froze. It was the only thing anyone talked about for weeks.”

Caroline hesitated but asked anyway, “And what happened to her?” I shrugged. “Nothing.”
Her fingers curled slightly around the stem of her glass. “Nothing?”

“People don’t forget,” I mused, taking another sip of whiskey. “But they do stop caring.”

I could see her absorbing that, turning it over in her mind. “So, you’re saying I shouldn’t worry?”
I shook my head.

“I’m saying that if you play your role well enough, tonight will be nothing but a footnote.” A pause held between us.
She met my gaze, steady but measured. “And what is my role?” I let the firelight flicker across my glass as I watched her.
“Your role, should you accept it, is to be the envy of the room—the trophy Liam has earned.” I saw the way the word trophy settled over her, how it sent something cold down her spine.
“You are expected to be everything,” I continued, “and he is expected to be grateful and honored to have you by his side.”

Her jaw tightened, only a tick but noticeable. She wanted to argue, yet she chose to bite her tongue. No doubt my son’s behavior hadn’t helped. I made a mental note to remind him of his obligations after this event came to its natural end.

While the silence hung between us, it became apparent: Her silence was not submission. It was strategy. Silence could be a strong strategy; many used wit, sheer force, but silence—that allowed one to observe, to let the other hand themselves with their own words. It created space, a vacuum that the untrained often rushed to fill, revealing more than they intended. A well-timed silence could be sharper than any remark, more damning than any accusation. It forced the other party to grapple with their own discomfort, to reveal their insecurities, to offer concessions they didn’t even realize they were making.

And Caroline, in her youth, understood that, but did she understand the world she waltzed into?

“They say America doesn’t have royalty,” I murmured, “but look around, my dear. I beg to differ.”

She inhaled slowly, her gaze flickering toward the room—the gold accents, the rich wood, the carefully curated legacy disguised as tradition.

“Should you wear the crown,” I said smoothly, “you must never let it slip.” Her fingers loosened on the stem of her glass.
Then, after a long moment, she lifted it slightly. “Then I won’t.”

For the first time that night, I smiled: small and approving. She had passed the test.
I had expected her to fold under the weight of my attention. She hadn’t.
I had expected her to shy away from the responsibility I had just placed on her shoulders. She hadn’t.
I had expected to see fear, intimidation, hesitation. Rather, I saw ambition. Determination. I understood why Liam chose her.
She was still thinking, her hazel eyes ablaze in the fire’s reflection; still recalibrating. Not easily defeated, I took note.

Good.

“May I ask you something?” she said softly. I tilted my head slightly. “You may.”
She exhaled, measured and careful.

“Mrs. Barnette is beautiful,” she began, “and I hope to be as graceful and effortless as her one day, but…”

She hesitated. Flattery—women used flattery for many things. To soften a blow, to maneuver through difficult conversations, to disarm men who mistook charm for submission. It was a tool, a negotiation, sometimes a shield. Caroline, I noticed, did not default to flattery. That, in itself, was telling. She sought approval, yes -- but not through meaningless compliments. She didn’t waste words.

She wanted to be taken seriously. That was rare, and far more effective. I leaned in intrigued.

She proceeded, “With Liam, I know he had a type in mind. Was it the same with you and Mrs. Barnette?”

Now it was my turn to pause. Not cold, not empty—just weighted.

I reached for my glass, taking a measured sip of whiskey before setting it back down. “A type,” I repeated, voice even, mauling over the term.
She held my gaze, unsure if she had overstepped. Then, I smiled.

Slight. Knowing.

“I wouldn’t call it that.”

Her brow furrowed faintly. “What would you call it?” “A choice.”
She straightened slightly, something unreadable flickering across her expression. “A choice,” she chewed on the word.
I nodded. “Marriage, my dear, is an extension of strategy. Some men build empires with their hands. Others build them through alliances. I did both.”

I set my glass down and studied her. “And Liam?”
I gave her a knowing look. She didn’t need me to spell that out for you; she was far too smart to ask for that question. If she wanted reassurance:

“If we had a daughter,” I said, my voice quieter now, “I would want her to be like you.”

I watched the way she absorbed the words, how they settled in her like something permanent.

Then, finally, I reached forward, my hand closing around hers—firm, steady. Not comfort, but acknowledgment. A quiet acceptance.

“About tonight: even Katherine, Princess of Wales, needed princess lessons.”

Caroline’s lips curved—not an eager smile, not even gratitude in the obvious sense, but something quieter, sharper. A small but knowing smirk, the kind that flickered at the edges before settling into something deliberate. She understood the remark for what it was.

Not indulgence. Not consolation. Acknowledgment.

Her eyes flickered, a glint of amusement tempered by something deeper—a quiet appreciation, a recognition of the game they were playing. She didn’t take offense. She didn’t bristle. Acceptance.

Slowly, I lifted her hand and pressed a chaste kiss against her knuckles. A rare act of kindness. A final seal of approval.

She exhaled slowly, nodding once.

Understanding the significance of what had just happened.

I released her hand, leaning back, reaching for my whiskey again.

“Now go find my son and keep him out of trouble.” A dismissal and a final reminder.

She had won my approval, but her work was far from over.

She nodded her farewell and returned to the party, head held high.

Liam would need to learn that is how you guide a woman in our world: carefully chosen words of expectation and minor indulgences.

