She glanced up as I stepped into the doorway, already dressed, already perfect. A soft cashmere wrap draped around her shoulders. “Caroline,” she said, her voice smooth as cream. “Come here. I could use your opinion.”

I obeyed before I had time to hesitate.

On the vanity lay a spread of options: diamonds, rubies, pearls. All of them glittering, none of them loud. There was no costume jewelry in this house. No filler.

“I need to choose a pair for Saturday’s dinner,” she said, lifting a delicate sapphire earring between her fingers before setting it back down. “Too cold for winter. The lighting in that ballroom is unforgiving.”

She picked up a different tray, this one lined with pearls. Not pristine, not flashy—just elegant. Cultivated.

Her eyes flicked toward my throat. “And you’re still wearing Liam’s.”

My hand rose instinctively to the gold chain. The single pearl resting just beneath my collarbone was small, imperfect, the kind of flaw you could only see if you were already looking too closely.

She didn’t ask about it. She just smiled faintly. “He gave it to you early, didn’t he?” I nodded. “He told me not to take it off.”
She hummed, amused. “Of course he did.”

Then she reached for a different pair—pearl drops with gold hooks—and held them up to my ears as I faced the mirror.

“These suit you better. Pearls are for women still becoming. Diamonds are for women who’ve arrived.”

She fastened them herself, her hands cool and practiced. Then she adjusted my shoulders, a light touch at my spine.

“Posture, sweetheart.”

I straightened. She didn’t smile, but her reflection did soften.

“My mother,” she said, “used to make me stand with books balanced on my head. Every night. I hated it. But I never slouched again.”

Her voice was lighter now, fond and just a little sad.

“She cared more about appearance than comfort. I suppose I understand it now. But I swore I’d do it differently. You’ll never hear me say you’re not enough, Caroline.”

That shouldn’t have made my chest tighten, but it did, because something in her voice struck a place I’d stopped expecting a mothers’ to reach.

Diane moved to the vanity again, lifting a bracelet, then discarding it just as quickly.

“There’s a difference between being shaped and being silenced. What I do with you—what Liam sees in you—that’s not about erasing who you are. It’s about elevation.”

I swallowed.

“Humor me as I recount another story. I made a mistake once,” she said, “early in my marriage. I corrected a friend of a senator in front of her husband. It wasn’t cruel. It was right. But it was loud.”

She selected a simple pin and turned it in her hand. “It took me three months to undo that moment. Because I forgot the rule.”

She met my gaze in the mirror. “Control is quiet. Always let Liam be the loud one.” I nodded, barely.
“And if I falter?” I asked.

Her fingers brushed a stray hair behind my ear, “Then you regroup. In private. And you come back stronger. Just like you’ve proven this week.”

Her touch settled at my shoulder again, firm but gentle. “They don’t need to see you bleed. Just that you don’t break.”

She picked up a small velvet box and opened it, revealing a gold hairpin shaped like a crescent moon.

“I used to wear this when I needed to feel… unshakable,” she said. “You’ll wear it at your next event.”

I didn’t argue. I didn’t say thank you. I just watched her in the mirror—poised, gracious, quietly formidable.

And wondered how long it would take for that to be me.

“You’re going to be brilliant,” she said, touching my back one more time. “And if you’re not, we’ll fix it.”

I believed her. That was the part that scared me most. “Keep the earrings,” Diane said, "They suit you."
I reached up, fingertips brushing the pearls she’d chosen—the ones that still felt like mine, even though I hadn’t picked them. In the mirror, Diane was already turning away, her reflection shrinking into the quiet elegance of the room.

And just before the door clicked shut behind her, she said without looking back, “Perfection isn’t a burden, Caroline. It’s an inheritance.”

Chapter 60.



The dining room was old money in architectural form—high ceilings, dark walnut panels polished to a quiet sheen, and oil portraits that stared down with the kind of solemnity only legacy could afford. A fire murmured in the hearth, more symbolic than necessary. The chandelier above glowed gold over bone China and weighty silver, casting gentle reflections in the wine glasses that had already been filled with something deep and expensive.

The food was prepared by the Barnettes’ private chef but felt deceptively like comfort—roast chicken glazed with lemon and rosemary, roasted root vegetables, herbed couscous served in a carved ceramic dish that looked older than the state of Vermont. Everything smelled of thyme and saffron and a confidence that didn’t need ornamentation.

Diane sat with the posture of a woman who had never once questioned her place at the table. Her hair was swept up in a style that managed to be both elegant and understated, her silk blouse the color of fine champagne. The Senator, across from her, had already folded his napkin with surgical precision and was murmuring something about municipal overreach to Michael, who looked barely interested but was nodding with just enough rhythm to avoid comment.

I took my seat beside Liam. He held the chair for me without flourish, his touch light at the small of my back—not performative, not even overtly possessive. Just... inevitable.

They’d all been watching me this week. Measuring. Assessing. But tonight wasn’t about passing a test. It was about proving I already understood the rules.

"Caroline," the Senator said, as though continuing a conversation we'd never started, "I understand Holbrook has appointed a new Vice Chancellor. Controversial, if the board memos are to be believed."

I reached for my water glass, letting the silence stretch just long enough to signal control, not hesitation.

"Dr. Previn," I said, nodding. "She's sharp. And polarizing. Which, frankly, might be exactly what we need."

Diane hummed in approval. "Polarizing women tend to get more done than agreeable men." "Until they become unmanageable," the Senator added dryly.
Michael smiled over the rim of his wineglass. "Which is when they usually start to get interesting."

I caught his eye and tilted my head. "You're assuming power and likability should ever be measured on the same axis."

Liam didn’t laugh, but the corner of his mouth curled. His hand found my knee beneath the table, the pressure light but pointed.

“She’s not just quoting your old lectures, Dad,” Michael said, “She’s improving on them.”

“She doesn’t need to quote me,” the Senator said, turning his gaze to me again. “She’s one of the few people I’ve heard speak at that university who doesn’t sound like she’s still auditioning.”

“That,” Diane said, lifting her glass toward me, “is the highest praise you’ll ever get from this end of the table.”

“And possibly the last,” Michael added.

Liam’s thumb was moving now, slow circles against the fabric of my dress, a rhythm so subtle it could be mistaken for absentmindedness if it hadn’t been him. I didn’t shift. Didn’t flinch. I let it anchor me.

“I suppose the question isn’t whether Caroline belongs at this table,” Diane said, dabbing at her mouth with her linen napkin. “It’s how long before she starts reordering it.”

I smiled without baring teeth. “You can’t reorder a system from the outside. First, you make them think you’re part of it.”

Michael gave a low whistle. “God, I hope she never turns on us.”

“She won’t,” Liam said, finally speaking at full volume. His voice was even, but the weight behind it was unmistakable. “She’s already here.”

He didn’t look at me when he said it. He didn’t need to.

The next course arrived—some delicate root vegetable tart with crème fraîche and shaved fennel—but no one touched it right away. The room had shifted.

The Senator broke the moment. “Michael, what’s the name of that editor you know at the Atlantic? The one with the fellowship pipeline?”

And just like that, the table moved on. Political gatekeeping. Academic publishing. The judicial implications of a recent Supreme Court whisper campaign. It wasn’t conversation so much as verbal fencing, and I found I liked the tempo. I liked the speed of it. The way ideas collided without anyone raising their voice.

Diane asked about one of my professors. The Senator challenged one of my takes on international diplomacy, then conceded my point with a nod that could have toppled lesser men. Michael made a comment about a Holbrook donor gala devolving into a PR scandal, and I volleyed back with a dry remark about legacy families and public amnesia.

Liam said less than all of them, but when he did speak, his words landed like closing arguments.

“She makes you all sharper,” he said, once. “That’s the point.” His hand stayed on my leg. The wine kept flowing.
By dessert—a flourless chocolate cake with espresso cream—I realized I hadn’t once felt the urge to retreat.

They weren’t testing me anymore. They were folding me in.
And Liam, still silent, still close, was watching it happen like a man who’d always known the outcome.

Michael lifted his glass, a little lazier than usual. “Well,” he said, voice just this side of slurred, “if the goal was to find someone who doesn’t just survive this family but improves it, I’d say we’re ahead of schedule.”

“Noted,” Diane said. She turned her gaze to me, eyes warm but sharp. “If you ever get bored of Liam, do let me know. We’ll find you a better use.”

“Unlikely,” Liam said smoothly. “She’s rather attached.” “Am I?” I asked lightly, turning just enough to meet his eyes.
His lips curved, but the heat beneath it was unmistakable. “You will be.” It wasn’t a threat.
It was a promise.

When the staff returned to clear the table, Liam stood first. He didn’t offer me his hand. He didn’t need to.

I stood with him, my spine straight, my breath even, my heart quiet.

And as we left the room, his hand found the small of my back again, gentle and certain. Guiding me.
Claiming me.

Welcoming me home.

After dinner, when the house was quiet, I went to Liam’s room instead of my own. The space smelled faintly of him—amberwood and bergamot, the lingering echo of his cologne warming the air like a memory.

I stood near the dresser, brushing my fingers along the edge of the watch box, still in my plaid dress from dinner. My heels were gone, traded for soft socks. My hair was still pulled back, though a few strands had slipped free. The warmth from the dining room hadn’t left me yet—not from the wine, not from the conversation, and certainly not from the look on Liam’s face when I made the Senator pause.

He was by the bed, already out of his sweater and in a black long-sleeved shirt, sleeves pushed halfway up his forearms. Watching me like I was still the center of attention. Like the show hadn’t ended.

"It was a good night," I said, almost to myself.

Liam didn’t move right away. He just watched me from where he leaned against the nightstand, hands loose at his sides.

"You were better than good," he said finally. His voice was warm, but there was something else beneath it. Pride. Possession. "You were composed, clever. Calculated when you needed to be."

I turned toward him slowly. "You make it sound like I was performing."

He shook his head. "No. You were revealing. You showed them exactly who you are."

He crossed the room, footsteps quiet against the rug, and stopped in front of me. “You showed them everything I already knew you were.”

His voice had shifted—lower, reverent. Like he wasn’t just proud, but awed.

He reached for me then, not as a man reaching for something he possessed, but as if he were laying his hands on something sacred. His fingers brushed a stray curl from my cheek, slow and deliberate, like it would be a sin to rush the moment.

“You don’t even see it, do you?” he murmured. “What you are.”

He exhaled, almost a whisper of disbelief. “You moved through that room like you were born to command it. And yet you sat there like you still had something to prove.”

His hand cupped my jaw gently, thumb brushing just beneath my lip. “To them, you were impressive,” he said. “To me, you are holy.”
He paused, eyes searching mine. “They see polish, grace, intelligence. But I see the rest. The way your mind works in silence. The precision in your restraint. The fire you keep hidden until it’s needed. They see a girl they can mold. I see a force that simply needed direction.”

His thumb traced the line of my cheekbone. “You don’t know how rare it is, do you? For someone to be both sharp and soft. To be built like a weapon and still fold like silk in the right hands.”

He leaned in, voice nearly breaking on the last line. “And you are in the right hands, Caroline. You always will be.”

I felt the breath catch in my throat. The praise came so easily from him tonight—but not like flattery. Like fact.

He looked at me like I was something divine—something found rather than made, though we both knew better.

“I see every part of you,” he murmured. “The girl no one believed in. The woman they’ll never deserve. You don’t have to ask for anything here, Caroline. You already belong to me.”

I didn’t speak. I couldn’t. The words caught somewhere behind my ribs, thick with heat and something dangerously close to devotion. I only nodded, slowly, my breath shallow against the silence.

He looked at me like I was something precious—something he'd claimed and carefully shaped, now finally ready to be admired, and I let the words settle in me as if penance for prior behavior.

Liam’s thumb traced the line of my jaw with a care that belied the weight of his grip, the way his fingers anchored me like I might vanish. His gaze held mine, deep and dark as open water, and I could already feel myself sinking into it.

“You don’t even see it, do you?” he murmured. “What you’ve become.”

He exhaled, slow and quiet. “They saw polish. I saw the process. I watched every step of it. Every crack smoothed, every edge sharpened. You were a diamond in the rough. Now?”

He leaned closer, his mouth barely brushing my cheek. “Now you’re blinding.”

I didn’t respond. I didn’t need to. I felt it in the way his touch changed—from guiding to admiring, from restraint to something closer to need.

He led me backward until my knees met the bed, then eased me down, patient and practiced. His movements weren’t rushed, weren’t hesitant. They were his—deliberate, controlled, certain.

The dress slipped from my shoulders with a sound no louder than breath. He peeled it down slowly, not for effect, but like he was uncovering something he intended to study in full. I lay back, left in nothing but the delicate pearl necklace he’d placed around my throat months ago.

His eyes caught it immediately, as his lips curved to a possessive smile.

His mouth followed, tracing along the curve of my collarbone, then down—every kiss a reminder that he knew me, all of me. That I was his work, his vision, now fully realized.

When he knelt at the edge of the bed and parted my legs, his hands slid under my thighs, anchoring me there. He looked up—not with awe, not with humility—but with the kind of heat that settled low and slow, like an ember catching breath.

“You’re mine,” he said again, voice thicker now.

His mouth was sure, his touch mapped from memory. Every movement had purpose—each one designed to undo me and then collect what unraveled. His hands never left me for long. He never looked away—not even when my breath caught, not when my hands gripped the sheets, not when I whispered his name like a confession.

“You don’t know what you do to me,” he murmured against my skin. “You undo me. Every fucking time.”

By the time he rose over me, his body covering mine with ease, my pulse was already chasing his. He kissed me slow, then deeper, until my hands slid into his hair and all that existed was the warmth of him pressing against every part of me.

“I love you,” he whispered. Not demanding, not fragile. Just certain.

I whispered it back into his skin, because in spite everything, I still chose him. I would always choose him.

When he entered me, it was not merely a warning or a claim—it was an unstoppable force, an inevitability that crashed over us like the final wave of a long-fought storm. His hand gripped mine, our fingers interlocking with an intensity that sent shivers down my spine. His rhythm was unwavering, deliberate, and powerful—each thrust synchronized with the steady beat of his breath, not driven by urgency but by an unyielding purpose. Every movement was charged with intent, as though he was not just touching me but etching this moment into my very soul, ensuring it would be unforgettable, like something that would live under my skin.

“Let me see you,” he murmured. “All of you.”

And I surrendered it completely. I offered him the soft moans escaping my lips, the seductive curve of my spine, and the uncontrollable quiver in my thighs as I surrendered to the overwhelming waves of pleasure. His eyes never wavered, a steady, consuming gaze that absorbed every moment of my unraveling beneath him.

He was patient, unyielding, a master of his craft. He extended the bliss, skillfully drawing out every ounce of ecstasy, coaxing it from me repeatedly until coherent thought was a distant

memory. Words failed me, my voice reduced to a breathy whisper, and the only syllable that lingered on my tongue was his name—a desperate, fervent plea in the throes of passion.

When it ended, we stayed close. Our breath loud in the quiet. My head on his chest, his hand wrapped tightly around my wrist like he thought I might slip away.

But I didn’t. I no longer wanted to.

The room was still lit with the soft afterglow of firelight, flickering shadows stretching long across the walls, cocooning us in a silence I didn’t want to break. Liam’s hand traced idle circles on my bare spine, his other arm tucked behind his head. I rested against his chest, knees curled beneath the covers, my cheek pressed over the steady thud of his heartbeat.

My fingers wandered across the planes of his chest, making thoughtless shapes—an arc, a line, a slow figure-eight. I didn’t speak. Neither did he. Not right away.

But then I felt him shift, slightly. Just enough to look down at me.

“So,” he said, voice low and content, “I looked at your spring schedule.” I huffed a quiet laugh against his skin. “Of course you did.”
“You’re still stuck with that 8AM on Fridays.” “Freshman year hazing. Required, unfortunately.” “I’ll drive you if it snows.”
“You’ll drive me even if it doesn’t.” He smiled faintly. “Probably.”
I kept tracing patterns, this time following the curve of his ribs. “You sound like you’re in a good mood.”

He tilted his head. “You were perfect tonight.”

“Mm.” I shifted slightly, the pearl of my necklace catching briefly against his chest. “You mentioned that already, but I’ll never tire of hearing it.”

Liam gave a quiet laugh, genuine. “Oh, now I know we’re in trouble.” I smiled into his skin, my fingers pausing over his heart.
“I was thinking,” he went on, voice lighter but still deliberate, “about spring break.” I blinked up at him. “Already?”
“You’ll be mine that week. No roommates. No distractions.” He didn’t phrase it like a question.

“Do I get to vote on this?” I teased. “You do. But I don’t count the votes.”
I rolled my eyes, grinning despite myself. “And where are you planning to take me, oh great campaign strategist?”

“Somewhere quiet. Just us. Somewhere you can’t run and I don’t have to chase.” “Liam,” I said, propping my chin on his chest, “you’re dodging.”
“I’m not.”

“You are. I need packing instructions. What climate am I packing for? Will I need a passport? Will I need your credit card to buy new clothes?”

He grinned and suddenly shifted, rolling so I was pinned beneath him, his hands bracketing either side of my face.

“You hate surprises,” he murmured, amused. “I really do.”
“And yet you keep letting me plan things.” “Because I’m learning to pick my battles.”
He leaned down, nipping lightly at my jaw before trailing kisses to my ear. “Yes, you’ll need my credit card,” he whispered. “You know what I like and what looks good on you.”

“For what climate?” I managed.

“Plan for everything: hot, cold, deep water scuba diving, hiking the Appalachian Trail” “Ugh you’re the worst! At that point, just pack for me!”
”oh yeah?” That perked him up.

“No because knowing you, you’ll only pack lingerie and keep me inside all day!”

He tickled my sides and I squeaked, squirming under him until I was laughing too hard to fight back.

“Stop,” I gasped. “Say please.” “Please, you lunatic.”
He finally stilled, watching me with a look I couldn’t quite name. Hunger and amusement. Adoration and control.

He kissed the tip of my nose. “I’ll take care of everything and you’ll love it. Promise.” I gave a mock glare. “You better hope I do.”
He chuckled, settling beside me again, pulling the blanket up higher around us. “I was also thinking about spring semester,” he said, voice quieter now. “Once hockey ends, I’ll have more time. Afternoons, weekends. Less travel.”

“And what will you do with all that free time?” “Spend it on you.”
My fingers found his again, our hands threading together like it was instinct now.

“More dinners, just us. Longer weekends. I’ll take you to that gallery in Newport. You’ll sit in the stands without having to pretend you like hockey.”

“I never pretended.”

“You just liked watching me.”

“I liked when you’d corner me by the locker room afterward.” He laughed low in his throat, fingers tightening around mine. “You’re dangerous,” he murmured.
“And you’re smug.”

His mouth brushed my temple, reverent. “You make me that way.”

A moment passed before Liam shifted beneath me, the arm around my waist tightening just enough to pull me closer. Our legs tangled beneath the covers, the warmth between us so complete it almost made me forget the cold that waited outside the walls of his room.

"You're glowing," he murmured, pressing a kiss just below my ear. "It’s the fire," I teased, though my voice came out softer than intended. He chuckled. "It’s you. It’s always you."
I closed my eyes, letting the words settle like a balm. I didn’t need them. But I wanted them. Especially when he said them like that—low, reverent, like he was confessing something holy.

"You know what I keep thinking about?" he asked, brushing his fingers along my collarbone. "The first time you walked into my room after everything. The sweatshirt/skirt set. That little skirt - that almost killed me. You were furious. But you still came. You still listened."

I bit my lip. "I didn’t think I had a choice."

"You didn’t," he said without apology. "But you still obeyed."

I turned my head just enough to see him, to study the ocean-dark of his eyes. "And now?" I asked. "What am I choosing? Or rather what am I obeying now?"
He smiled, slow and devastating. "Both. You choose and obey me. Again and again. Even when you don’t say it out loud. Even when you still think you can resist."

My hand slid up his chest, resting just over his heart.I didn’t look at him when I said it— didn’t dare.“You make it hard to want anything else.” I murmured, almost too soft to hear— like saying it louder would make it too real.

His gaze burned into mine. "Good. I want you so full of me there’s no room left for doubt." I shivered at that—part fear, part something else. Something warmer. Deeper.
“I want to erase everyone who came before me,” he murmured, kissing the edge of my jaw. “Scrape their fingerprints from your skin until there’s nothing left but mine.”

“You already did,” I breathed. “I don’t remember anyone else.” His voice dropped, a quiet exhale. “Then I did it right.”
I let myself believe it.

And when his hand slid up to cradle my face, when his lips met mine again—slow and possessive—I kissed him back because it was true. It had been for a while.

I let myself go. I let myself fall knowing he’d always catch me. I found myself asking, why did I ever resist in the first place?

Chapter 61.



The morning we left, the estate looked like something carved out of a snow globe—too pristine, too still. The frost clung to the windows in delicate patterns, and everything felt muted under the weight of goodbye.

We were already packed. Liam’s car idled in the circular drive, the driver waiting discreetly by the front bumper. Inside, the Barnettes stood in the entryway, polished and unhurried, as if saying farewell was just another form of performance.

Diane hugged me first. Her perfume was soft—gardenia and something deeper beneath it, like old wood and history.

“You did everything right this week,” she said, her voice low near my ear. “Don’t let old voices in your head make you question that. Let mine replace them instead.”

I nodded, throat tight. She didn’t say the word ‘mother,’ but it hung in the air all the same. Not a replacement to my own, rather a correction.

Her hands came up to adjust the collar of my coat. “You’ll grow into this family beautifully,” she said, “and I don’t say that lightly.”

The Senator was next: Less tactile, but not cold.

“That podcast you mentioned,” he said, handing me a slim hardcover book with a dust jacket worn at the edges, “on judicial reform? I queued it up last night. I look forward to listening to it on my next commute into the Hill. And this—” he tapped the cover—“is required reading if you want to hold your own in a policy room.”

The title read: Reflections of a Diplomat by Katherine Morgan.

“She reminded me of you,” the Senator said. “Quiet in the room. Loud on the page.” My fingers tightened on the book. “Thank you. I’ll read it carefully.”
“You always do.”

Michael was the last. He stepped close and lowered his voice just enough that only I could hear.

“Don’t let the pressure get to you,” he murmured. “I don’t want another call from Liam in the middle of the night asking how to wake you up.” I froze.

Michael’s expression didn’t change. “Call me next time. I know better ways to ease the pressure.” He winked, “And mine don’t leave bruises.”

He kissed my cheek before I could respond, then turned and clapped Liam on the shoulder like it was any other morning. “Don’t crash the car,” he said. “And for God’s sake, let her pick the music for once.”

Liam didn’t answer him. Not really. Just that small, contained smirk.

Diane and the Senator stepped forward to embrace Liam, words quieter now, meant only for their son. Something warm passed between them—brief, dignified. The kind of affection that didn’t need to be spoken aloud to be understood.

I watched it all unfold with the quiet reverence of someone watching the final scene of a play they hadn’t expected to be cast in.

Then Liam took my hand, one last glance, one last thank you and then we stepped into the cold together.

The campus gates came into view just as the sun began to dip behind the trees, casting long shadows across the stone pathways and snow-dusted quads. The familiar spires of Holbrook’s architecture felt sharper now, more defined against the quiet glow of late afternoon.

Liam downshifted, one hand on the wheel, the other resting over mine with casual possession. "They love you," he said, voice low but brimming with certainty. "You know that, right? My parents. Michael. You made an impression."

I smiled faintly, fingers tightening under his. "You sound surprised."

He glanced over, his navy eyes soft with something dangerously close to reverence. "I’m not. I just like hearing them talk about you like you’re already one of us."

I looked away, watching the landscape shift from estate homes to dorm buildings, from curated legacy to collegiate chaos. My breath fogged the window as I leaned against it slightly, the leather seat still warm beneath me.

"Diane said something to me before we left," I murmured. "She told me not to let old voices in my head undo everything I did this week. She said I did everything right."

Liam was quiet for a moment. Then: "She meant it."

I believed her, but the truth was that I didn’t know how to let go of the other voice – the one that lived in the curve of my mother’s frown, in the sting of words sharpened by disappointment. She'd never said I made her proud, never fastened a necklace for me with steady hands or let me borrow her pearls, like Diane had. No, my mom would tell me that pearls were for funerals or tea parties. That difference settled somewhere deep, something both tender and raw, something I didn’t know how to name.

Liam pulled into the reserved lot behind the hockey house, putting the car in park but not moving to open his door just yet. The silence stretched between us, but not uncomfortably.

He looked over again, brow raised slightly. "You ready?"

I turned back to him, my voice steadier than I felt. "As I’ll ever be." He kissed my knuckles. "Good. Because we’re just getting started."
And with that, we readied ourselves for the next semester, into the echo of chapel bells somewhere in the distance, into whatever version of the future we were building now. One breath, one step, one move at a time.

Chapter 62.




Holbrook in January was glass-edged and hushed, the kind of cold that didn’t bite so much as press—sharp, precise, almost elegant. Snow clung to the balustrades of old Gilded Age mansions turned into academic and administrative buildings, their ivy-covered facades trimmed in frost. The wide stone steps of academic buildings gleamed like polished bone.
Everything looked cinematic, curated, which was fitting. Holbrook wasn’t built for chaos—it was built for legacy.

Liam’s black BMW slowed beside the west entrance of my dormitory . Chloe waved from her window above, her breath fogging the glass. I smiled, warm in my coat, the kind Liam preferred on me—structured, black, cashmere. I’d left Holbrook in December with a pulse full of static. I returned now with my feet steady beneath me.

Inside my suitcase: pre-assembled supplement packs, my syllabi printed and annotated, a term calendar color-coded by Liam himself. My Adderall bottle clicked softly against the hard edge of my planner—exact dosage, strictly managed. My birth control reminder was synced across three devices — Liam had offered to hold and take on the responsibility of ensuring I took it on time, just like he did my Adderall, but with away games still on his roster, I didn’t want to take any chances. Everything I needed to succeed had already been thought through.

Liam had made sure of that.

It wasn’t until I unpacked that I realized how different it felt—how weightless. No guessing. No spiraling. Just order. This is what it meant to be chosen.

He wasn’t with me, but I could still feel his fingerprints across every part of my life—the perfect fit of my schedule, the curve of my new winter boots, the way I instinctively checked

my new watch before leaving the dorm -- not because he told me to, because it made everything easier.

The first time I walked into Lecture Hall B that week, I wasn’t the girl who almost crashed during her midterms last semester. I wasn’t the scholarship student trying not to look like one. I was Liam Barnette’s Caroline. Curated. Polished. Composed.

The professor smiled when he saw me. “Miss Greer,” he said, as though expecting greatness. And I smiled back because now, so was I.
