Caroline was shaken. She had put on a brave face as she settled against me on the couch and worked on her homework, but I could tell. The way her highlighter lines wobbled, the way she chewed on her pen cap. She had asked me to review problem sets that she could have done in her sleep. Not only did this motherfucker touch her, he crawled under her skin.

That wasn’t just unacceptable. It required consequences.

What had he said? Academia was all about relationships. He was going to have one hell of a breakup.

I scrolled through the folders on my laptop—screenshots, archived profiles, a scrubbed LinkedIn cache of his previous institutional affiliations. I already had the name of the assistant dean from his old college. I’d sent a soft inquiry through a friend in legal: was it true Nathan Hale had been investigated for ethical violations involving undergraduates at his prior post? I knew the answer before the reply came. What I needed was confirmation—and confirmation arrived this morning, neatly worded and delicately redacted.

I went on to draft three emails:

One to Holbrook’s academic affairs board, copying dean of students and president of the university, attaching documentation of Hale’s pattern, a recording of the thinly veiled

proposition he’d made to Caroline, how he’d tried to frame it as academic mentorship. Let him try to charm his way out of this.

One to the fellowship committee where Nathan had submitted a personal reference for a
high-profile student grant—along with a quiet nudge that might suggest the committee revisit his history.

And one—more casual, more poisonous—to a tenured professor Hale admired publicly on Twitter, including screenshots and a quiet, off-the-record heads-up about a potential scandal.

Pleased with the draft I hit “send” on each.

I wasn’t going to let him wriggle out of this one with bureaucratic apologies and careful reassignments.

He had touched what was mine, and that came with consequences.

Caroline shifted slightly, her head resting against my thigh now, hair soft under my hand as I stroked it slowly—calm, methodical. Her legs dangled off the edge of the arm of the couch, a posture that could have been mistaken as relaxed. She hadn’t said much since we returned from campus, only offered me her notebook and asked if I’d double-check the answer key on a set of economics problems she could’ve done in her sleep.

I hadn’t told her what I was doing. Not yet. She didn’t need to know until it was already over. I closed the laptop.

By this time tomorrow, Nathan Hale would be gone. Not just reassigned or warned—but ruined. Quietly. Permanently.

I ran my palm down the curve of her cheek, then tapped her shoulder gently. “Come here,” I said.
She let me guide her without protest, unfolding from the cushion beside me like something delicate and worn. I pulled her into my lap, arms curling around her waist as she settled against my chest, small and quiet and still trying to keep it together.

“We’re calling it an early night,” I murmured, brushing a kiss against the crown of her head. “No more studying.”

She nodded, but didn’t move.

For a while, we just stayed like that—her head tucked beneath my jaw, her breath slow and uneven, fingers curled lightly in the fabric of my shirt. I could feel the tension still humming in her, not the sharpness from earlier but something residual, echoing.

“I don’t want to go to Early American Thought tomorrow,” she said finally, so softly I might’ve missed it if I hadn’t been listening for exactly that.

I stroked her spine, slow and steady. “I know.”

I let a beat pass.“But I doubt he’ll be there.”

She lifted her head slightly, just enough to meet my eyes. “Why do you say that?”

I held her gaze for a moment, then let a small, unreadable smile flicker at the corner of my mouth. “Just a hunch.”

She didn’t press, though she probably knew I would have something to do with his departure.

Instead, I shifted slightly, angling her to look at me more fully. My hand found the side of her face, thumb brushing just beneath her cheekbone.

“Listen to me,” I said quietly. “What he suggested—that you have to sleep your way to success in academia—is complete bullshit. You know that, right?”

She blinked once, lips parted like she wanted to answer but didn’t know how.

“You’re smart, Caroline. Not just good-grades smart. You see things other people miss. You work harder than anyone I’ve ever met.”

Her eyes flickered.

“If you want to be published, you will be. If you want fellowships, you’ll win them. On merit (ok and maybe with a little help from me) —but not because of anyone like him. Not because you had to spread your legs to be considered.”

She let out a breath, shaky but real.

“I’ve never met anyone more capable than you,” I said, voice low. “Don’t let some parasite like Hale make you doubt that.”

Her fingers tightened against my chest. And finally, she nodded.

I felt it—that small surrender, not to me, but to safety, to the idea that maybe, just maybe, she didn’t have to carry everything alone.It didn’t fix what had been done, but it was enough, for now.

She was still curled into me, soft and quiet, her breathing steadier though not quite even. She still had that look in her eyes—it wasn’t fear, not exactly. Disgust. Shame. The kind of emotional residue that clung long after the violation had passed. I hated that she was feeling any of it.

She hadn’t done a single thing wrong, yet she was the one sitting in my lap like she needed to be small again just to process it.

I kissed the top of her head again and repeated, “No more work tonight.” Her fingers didn’t loosen, but she nodded again.

I reached for my phone without shifting her weight, ordering from the Italian place off- campus she liked—the one with handmade pasta and tiramisu that always made her smile. She didn’t have an appetite, not yet, but she would. And when it came, I’d plate it for her, pour her a glass of sparkling water, and make sure she ate something warm. She’d been through enough.

I shifted, scooping her in my arms and setting us down on the bed, continuing to hold her close. I grabbed the remote, queuing up one of those slow-burn historical dramas she pretended not to like but always watched straight through, the kind with layered dialogue and scenes that unfolded like chess matches. Something to watch that would allow her to disappear for a while. The screen flickered to life. She didn’t lift her head. I didn’t ask her to.

I wrapped my arms around her tighter, letting my thumb trace the curve of her wrist where her pulse beat soft and steady. I didn’t need her to talk. Didn’t need her to perform composure.

She could unravel here if she needed to. That was the point.

Let them all think I was the cold one. Let them think she was the porcelain doll in my curated display case. They didn’t see this. They didn’t see what it meant to hold something fragile and know it was the most unbreakable thing you’d ever touched—because it kept choosing to hold itself together.

I’d burn down every last institution that told her she needed to trade herself to be seen — starting with Nathan Hale.

The next day, I was pleasantly awoken by Caroline giving me head. I had mentioned it in passing to her sometime last semester or maybe over Christmas break that this is how I wanted to be woken up. She had laughed at the time, but I knew she’d come around. I threaded my fingers through her hair, keeping her where I wanted her as I felt myself coming to the edge. Just like she was trained, she swallowed every drop.

As she returned to top of the bed, I gave her a small chaste kiss, “Hey pretty girl. Where did that come from? (Not that I’m complaining. I am so not complaining.)”

She smiled and snuggled into my chest. “Wanted to show my gratitude” she started drawing patterns on my chest, “I like when you’re happy, and when I’m the one making you happy. You said you liked being woken up that way. It’s a simple enough ask, really.” She turned her head to look up at me with those big hazel eyes and an innocent smile on her face that contrasted the act she had just performed.

“Careful, I might get used to this,” I brushed her hair back as I admired her.

She didn’t say anything, just held her smile and gave an indifferent, “hmm”. It was genuine and gentle. She wasn’t coy, wasn’t asking for anything in return.

This is how I always pictured us: tangled up in sheets, happy and satiated, her soft and willing in my arms, every instinct shaped to please, not because I demanded it, no, because she wanted to.

She looked so peaceful lying against me, like nothing could reach her here, like I’d built something strong enough to keep her untouched. That was the lie, though, wasn’t it? Men like Nathan Hale exist in every corner of the world—soft-voiced predators hiding behind curated respectability. It didn’t matter that I had. I saw him from the start, that he was the type of men I recognize instantly.

It wasn’t the obvious things—not at first. He kept his distance, smiled like an academic, framed every glance in the language of professionalism, but I saw it: the precision, the performance.

The first time I noticed him, he was watching her exit a lecture hall, eyes following the sway of her ass, the curve of her shoulder under that ridiculous cream sweater she wore when she thought no one was paying attention. He didn’t stare long, just enough to take stock.

Men like Nathan didn’t make mistakes. They tested waters. They studied patterns. They used words like potential and intellect, dangled them like keys in front of the right kind of girl— ambitious, self-possessed, under just enough pressure to confuse praise with possibility.

So yes, I watched him.

It didn’t take long for the digital search to tell me everything I already knew. A few emails to a connection or two provided even more insight and evidence. Thin tenure history. Soft complaints swept under administrative rugs. A résumé held together by charm and redirection. The kind of man who knew just how much he could get away with, so long as he dressed it in the language of guidance.

Still—I needed to be sure. So I waited.

When he left that note for Caroline, inviting her to his office under the pretense of something related to class, I didn’t forbid it. I let her go. Told her I understood, but gave enough warning to appear protective but not overly jealous.

I’d been in the hallway ten minutes before she arrived, just outside the narrow vent cut into the wall near the door. The old faculty offices had terrible insulation. It wasn’t hard to catch sound if you knew where to stand.

I heard everything. The compliment about her work. The pivot to her boyfriend. The casual suggestion that she lie to me. “You can say you’re leading a study group.” And then, worst of all, the line he delivered with that soft, practiced reason: “What I’m asking isn’t unreasonable.”

I gripped the edge of the wall so tightly my knuckles cracked. Not because I was surprised.
No, he said it to her like it was a favor.

She’d gone in expecting an academic conversation. He turned it into leverage, into a transaction.

Then when I heard her voice—small at first, then sharp with fury—I knew what it had cost her to hold the line. She shouldn’t have had to, not in that room, not with him.

By the time she stormed out, face pale, steps clipped and fast, I was already standing in the corridor like I’d just finished a meeting of my own.

She didn’t know what I’d heard. I didn’t tell her. It didn’t matter that I had heard.

It mattered that Nathan Hale hadn’t just crossed a line, he’d buried himself beneath it. Now I waited for the inevitable: his departure from Holbrook.
She was still tucked against my chest when the low buzz of her phone rattled against the nightstand. Morning light was creeping in through the narrow space between the curtains, soft and gray, diffused across her bare shoulder where the blanket had slipped. I felt her shift
—relaxed, laggard—one hand fumbling for the phone as she blinked the sleep from her eyes. A beat of silence, followed by the soft sound of her breath catching on nothing.
