There was no question, no space for negotiation. Maybe it should have annoyed me, the way he expected me to obey, but instead, it warmed me, a sweet gesture.

I took a sip of coffee, and his expression shifted ever so slightly, like I had given him exactly what he wanted. Deep down, I think I knew that I had.

I reached for a pastry, but before I could take a bite, Liam plucked it from my fingers. I shot him a glare. “Excuse me?”

He just smirked, tearing off a piece of the croissant and holding it up to my lips, “Open.”

When I tilted my head, his gaze darkened, like he was waiting to see if I’d fight him on this. The moment stretched between us, something soft and dangerous settling into the air, before I surrendered, “Fine.”

Liam’s smirk grew, feeding me a small piece of the buttery croissant, “That’s my girl.”

“It would have been sexier had you fed me a strawberry, just saying,” I licked my lips teasingly.

He laughed, “Maybe if I didn’t have plans for us today.”

I had barely finished eating when Liam plucked the tray off my lap and set it aside. Before I could protest, he pulled me into him. I let out a soft gasp as I landed against his bare chest, his hands settling on my waist, his grip firm, unyielding.

I blinked up at him, my breath catching. “Liam—”

His fingers trailed over my spine, slow and possessive.

“You love me,” he murmured, satisfaction curling in his voice. “Yes,” I whispered, settling against him
His hold tightened. “And you know you’re mine.” “And you’re mine.”
Liam exhaled, his lips curving slightly. “Yes, baby.” Then, softer—softer than I had ever heard him before. “This is what you were made for: 1,000 thread count Egyptian cotton sheets, weekend escapes to one seaside town or another, never having to want for anything.” It sounded too good to be true. Maybe it was, but I didn’t care, not after this weekend.

I made a noncommittal hum.

“You don’t have to do anything anymore, Caroline.”

I frowned slightly. “What?”

His hands cupped my face, his thumbs stroking my cheekbones.

“You don’t have to worry about anything. You don’t have to think about anything. I’ll take care of you.” I looked at him, studying his face, his conviction. His eyes met mine, the color matching the ocean beyond our balcony: true blue and shimmering. I felt the warmth of him and his words envelope me. I believed him, how could I not?

So I didn’t argue. I just curled into him, letting him pull me closer. Letting him win no longer felt like I was losing.

“So, do I get to know what we’re doing today, or is this a ‘follow and find out’?”

Liam smirked over his coffee cup, eyes glinting with something unreadable. He looked entirely too pleased with himself, lounging in the hotel chair like he already had every move planned out. I should have known by now that he did.

“You ask a lot of questions, sweetheart,” he said smoothly, setting his mug down. I raised a brow. “And you avoid a lot of answers.”
Liam hummed, standing and stretching, his muscles pulling tight beneath his shirt. Then, without missing a beat— “We’re going shopping.”
I blinked. “Shopping?”

His smirk widened. Before I could respond, he tossed a light jacket onto the bed beside me. “Get dressed. We’re leaving in ten.”

I narrowed my eyes at him. Something about this felt… off. Not bad. Just calculated. Like Liam wasn’t just taking me shopping—he was taking me somewhere with a purpose.

I slid out of bed, pulling the jacket over my shoulders, still watching him carefully. “Are you planning to tell me why we’re going shopping?”

He shrugged, “Because I want to.”

I narrowed my eyes at him. That wasn’t the whole truth – not even close. But I let it slide, not worth pushing a nice gesture.


The moment we stepped inside, I knew I was out of my depth. It wasn’t just a store. It was a designer boutique.The kind of place that didn’t put price tags on things because if you had to ask, you couldn’t afford it.

The kind of place where Liam belonged effortlessly, and I… I swallowed hard, my fingers tightening around my jacket.

Liam led me inside like he had done this a thousand times before, greeting the staff with a nod as they practically fell over themselves to accommodate him.

“Mr. Barnette,” a sharply dressed woman greeted him with a bright, eager smile. “A pleasure to see you again.”

“Again?” I whispered, crossing my arms over myself instinctively.

Liam smirked, placing a hand on my lower back. “I’ve been here before.” I scoffed. “Obviously.”
The woman—Olivia, according to her nametag—tilted her head, giving me a once-over like she was trying to assess my worth. It made my skin itch.

Her expression shifted as if I passed her assessment and turned back to Liam. “How can I help today?”

Liam didn’t hesitate. “She needs a few things,” he said simply. Olivia smiled, already knowing what that meant.
She motioned to a nearby associate, and suddenly, I was being ushered toward the private fitting area.

I glanced back at Liam, suspicious. “What kind of ‘things’ are we talking about?” Liam winked. “You’ll see.”
The first outfit was a dress: soft, expensive fabric, tailored perfectly to my frame.

It reminded me of some other items Liam had purchased for me in the past. Appropriate for mixed company: networking, informal charity events, interviews.

And when I stepped out of the fitting room, Liam was already waiting.

Seated in a leather chair, legs spread, his gaze dragging over me slowly: Assessing. Claiming. A smirk tugged at his lips as he leaned back, nodding approvingly.

“That one,” he said. Like a command.

I flushed, shifting slightly. “I haven’t even seen myself yet.”

“You don’t need to.” His gaze was dark, unwavering. “You look perfect.” I rolled my eyes, suddenly very aware of how much he was enjoying this.
I looked in the mirror, studying myself. It was beautiful, I was beautiful wearing it. It might even be something I would have picked for myself. He had good taste, I’d give him that.

The next outfit was a silk blouse and a fitted skirt.

Then a cocktail dress.

Then lingerie, “to make up for the set we ruined last night,” he whispered devilishly in my ear.

I didn’t even realize how deep I had gotten until I stood in front of the mirror, watching as Olivia carried away a growing pile of designer pieces—all of them chosen by Liam.

I thought back to the first time Liam had taken me shopping last fall—how furious I’d been, how humiliated. I’d felt like a doll then, some pretty, pliable thing being dressed up to match the life he’d already decided I would have. It felt like mockery. Like ownership.

But now?

Now it just felt… normal. Familiar, even. A quiet ritual between us, threaded with meaning I no longer questioned. A gift, yes—but more than that, a reflection of the life we were already living. It was easier not to fight it. Easier to admit that I liked beautiful things. That I liked the way he saw me.

This was Liam curating, yes—but not just for the world. He was shaping something in me too, softening sharp edges, coaxing polish where there’d once been strain. And strangely, it made sense. The version of me he envisioned didn’t feel like a stranger anymore.

Still, as I watched him—so calm, so sure—I felt a flicker of unease tighten low in my chest. I turned toward him, hesitating.
“Liam…” My voice came softer than I meant. “This is too much.”

He looked up, that faint smirk still tugging at the corner of his mouth, and suddenly I wasn’t sure what I was asking him to hear. I didn’t want him to think I was shallow. Or that he could buy me off. Or worse—that I wanted him to.

His expression didn’t change, “It’s exactly enough.” “Liam—”
His gaze flicked to mine, sharp and final. “You’re mine, aren’t you?” I gave a timid smile, “Yes.”
His lips curved slightly. “Then let me take care of you.”

I let out a breath, as if arguing had crossed my mind. Yet the truth was…I liked it. I liked how effortless it was to just let him take care of things. I liked knowing that he wanted me to have the best of everything. I liked the way he looked at me in each outfit, like he was seeing his own vision come to life. “Okay.”

Liam’s smirk deepened. I had just given him exactly what he wanted. Deep down, I knew— That was all he ever needed from me.

The car hummed beneath us, gliding along the coastal highway with the kind of smooth precision that only came from Liam’s driving—controlled, quiet, deliberate. I rested my head against the window, the late afternoon sun casting long golden lines over the passing trees, the occasional glint of water breaking through the landscape like light refracting through glass.

I felt full in that gentle, aching kind of way—warm from lunch, heavy-limbed from the bath, the shopping bags tucked behind my seat like quiet proof of the weekend he’d built around me. Every detail had been curated. Every moment engineered to soothe. To remind me who I belonged to.

He reached for my hand without looking, his fingers curling around mine as if the silence between us had gotten too still.

“I got the internship,” he said, voice smooth and low.

My head turned slowly, eyes meeting his profile. Calm. Controlled. As if this wasn’t a life- shaping announcement.

“What?”

“ The Rappaport Summer Fellowship. Cambridge.” His thumb brushed over my knuckles. “You and me.”

My stomach tightened, I think out of excitement but it could have been anxiety. We hadn’t exactly discussed the summer in detail “Wait—you already accepted?”

“I’m not asking your permission, Caroline,” he said, but there was no bite in it. Only certainty. “It’s done.”

I sat up slightly, tucking one leg beneath me. “But—what about me?” That earned me a look – brief but pointed, “You’ll be with me.”
“I know, but I mean… what would I even do there?”

He turned back to the road, but his smirk was unmistakable. “Whatever you want. My mother already offered to put you on the board at the MFA or the Gardner if you’re feeling philanthropic. Or you could work with her foundation – she’d love you to join her there.
Or”—he squeezed my hand lightly—“you could do nothing. Just enjoy the city. Study. Rest.”

I blinked. “Rest?” The word didn’t sound real on my tongue. I wasn’t someone who rested. Not really. Not ever.

Liam glanced at me again, softer this time. “You don’t have to earn your place with me, Caroline.”

That landed deeper than I wanted to admit. I looked down at our hands. His grip steady. Possessive. Anchoring.

A summer in Cambridge. No dorm. No Chloe. No campus library. Just Liam. A life curated entirely by his design. I knew what it meant, even if he didn’t say it outright. There would be no version of this summer that wasn’t his. His schedule, his space, his world. And I would fit into it exactly how he wanted me to.

Still—I said it before I could second-guess myself, “I told Chloe I’d spend a week with her in the Hamptons.” Liam’s mouth didn’t twitch, didn’t tighten, but I saw the pause behind his eyes.

“I expect you to respect that,” I added, carefully. “I think you can manage a week without me.”

He didn’t respond right away. But when he did, his voice was light—so light it almost made me miss the edge beneath it, “I’m sure I’ll survive.” And then, just as easily, “We’ll talk about dates.”

I nodded once, as if that had been a win. But I could already feel the seam tightening again— subtle, silk-threaded, invisible.

I looked ahead at the winding road, the ocean stretched out to our right in that lazy, glittering way it had all afternoon. A few months ago, I would’ve resented this—another plan made without asking, another decision handed to me like a beautifully wrapped command. I would’ve pushed back, demanded control just to prove I still had it.

But now, with his hand warm in mine, I felt something closer to peace. Maybe even relief. I wanted this—wanted to belong to something already in motion, something steady and sure. And I was done apologizing for that, no matter what version of womanhood I was supposed to aspire to.

“I guess I should brush up on Boston public transportation,” I murmured. Liam laughed under his breath, low and satisfied. “You won’t need it.” Of course I wouldn’t. I’d have him.
And somehow, in this car, wrapped in sun and salt air, between certainty and surrender, that felt like the most natural thing in the world. Not a loss of freedom—just a quiet inevitability.

I turned back to the window, letting the sunlight warm my cheek. Summer was already settled. And maybe, if I was honest, so was I.

The silence settled again, soft and sprawling, broken only by the quiet sound of tires skimming the pavement and the low rhythm of Liam’s playlist—something instrumental and understated, designed for elegant dinners or curated weekend drives.

I shifted, letting my head rest against the seat now instead of the window. “Do you know where we’ll be living yet?”

His answer came without hesitation. “Beacon Hill, there are two historic buildings that were joined to create full service luxury condos .”

I blinked. “Seriously?”

He nodded once, eyes still on the road. “It’s done.”

I knew the name, of course. Everyone did. The Archer wasn’t just a building—it was a statement. A pre-war stone facade in Beacon Hill with black-and-gold awnings and a private entry courtyard. Apartments there weren’t leased—they were handed down like heirlooms. The kind of place where senators and collectors lived, where every sconce and molding was original, and every doorman knew your drink order.

“Sounds fancy,” I said.

“You’re going to love it: roof top lounge, old charm with modern amenities- I can’t wait to show you. You’re going to love it.,” Liam replied, like it was nothing. “And just steps from the state house and in the perfect neighborhood for us.”

Of course it was. I stared out the windshield, watching the coastline slip into city outskirts. “And when would we… move in?”
“June 1st. Maybe earlier.” “So we’ll be living together.”
That earned me a glance. Slightly amused. Slightly sharper. “We already do.” I looked down at our hands again, still linked between us. He wasn’t wrong.
There were clothes in his drawers. My perfume on his bathroom shelf. His bed had become the only place I could sleep through the night. I couldn’t remember the last time I’d packed a bag to stay with him—it had been unnecessary for weeks.

Still, there was something different about this. About a lease signed in Beacon Hill. About him calling it done.

He reached over, turned down the volume a notch.

“There will be dinners,” he said, casually. “Receptions. A few cocktail parties over the summer—some with my father, some with the State House interns. We’ll need to be presentable.”

I hummed.

He went on. “You’ll need proper shoes for the garden events. Something tailored for evenings. A few dresses that photograph well.”

The dresses. The ones from yesterday. The ones he’d watched me try on like he was solving for an equation. Silk-lined, expertly fitted, built not for comfort but for impression. That had been the reason.

I turned to look at him fully, slow and careful. “That’s why you took me shopping.” His smile was faint. “You think I’d let you walk into those rooms unarmed?”
Unarmed, like those heels and hems were armor. Like I wasn’t just coming with him—I was being deployed.

He continued. “We’ll have a driver, obviously. And the building’s secure. You won’t have to worry about anything.”

“It sounds lovely,” and I meant it as I sat back against the seat, quiet for a long stretch of road.

He didn’t fill the silence, because he didn’t have to. Everything was already in motion.

The city. The apartment. The invitations and calendars I hadn’t seen yet. The wardrobe, curated to whisper refinement. And me—folded perfectly into it all.

Not as decoration. As proof.

He would come home to me every night. Take me to dinners where my presence would say something, even if I never spoke. I would be the girl on his arm, the name whispered behind champagne flutes and manicured hands. Not out of scandal. But admiration.

I could feel the shape of it already—the way he saw me fitting.

And the worst part? Somewhere in the hollow of my stomach, it didn’t feel terrifying.

It felt inevitable: The next version of us, of me. Liam had already written my next chapter, and I was not longer worried about the version of me he had written into that work.

I stared straight ahead, the skyline pulling into view like a promise. The city was beginning to take shape ahead of us, that familiar silhouette of slate rooftops and historic spires rising like a crown against the sky. I could see the direction we were heading now—toward the long bridge that would cut us back into campus.

Not wanting the silence to fill with anything heavier than it already had. So I let the next words slip out casually, soft enough that it might’ve sounded like an afterthought. “You know spring break’s next week.”

He didn’t respond right away.

I kept my gaze fixed out the window. “I haven’t packed or made any wish lists.”

That got his attention. From the corner of my eye, I saw the faint turn of his head, the slow smile pulling at the edge of his mouth.

“Caroline Greer,” he said, low and pleased, “are you telling me you trusted me to plan spring break without a single input or question from you?”

I smirked, still watching the skyline, “You already told me to clear that week, something about it being just us” I laughed, “I just figured you’d have told me where we were going by now,” I murmured. “But honestly, I suspect you already have my bags packed.”

He let out a quiet laugh—one of those rare, satisfied ones that sounded like a reward in itself. “My only question,” I added, glancing at him now, “is: are we flying or driving?”
He didn’t answer right away. He just looked at me—long enough that I felt it in my chest before he even spoke.

And then: “Flying.” Of course we were.

He reached for my hand again, lacing our fingers together with that same unshakable certainty he always carried, as if the world had already been shaped to fit his grip.

“Then pack light,” he said, his voice all silk and possession. “You won’t need anything I haven’t already picked out.”

And I smiled—because of course he had.

The rest of the drive passed in quiet, contented stillness—his fingers laced with mine, the breeze from the ocean threading through the open window. I wasn’t bracing for the next turn anymore, or wondering when the road would shift beneath me. I knew where we were headed. I knew who I was with. And for the first time in a long time, that felt like enough.
Maybe even everything.

Spring Break was within reach. Summer was waiting, with a sample of what our future would look like. And whatever came after. I’d meet it all—his hand in mine, exactly where I was meant to be.


Carved in the Cliffs. Spring Break Novella.


Chapter 1: Welcome to Paradise.



Liam’s POV.


She was practically vibrating beside me in the first-class cabin, trying not to look like she was vibrating beside me in the first-class cabin. That was Caroline—always pretending she wasn’t feeling what she felt. Always trying to play it cool, even as her fingers curled around the edge of the armrest like they were anchoring her to the moment.

Her eyes had gone wide the second she saw the gate. Naples, Italy. She hadn’t said anything then—just looked at me like she didn’t know whether to throttle me or thank me. I liked her that way: speechless, a little off-balance, waiting for me to give her direction.

Now she was staring out the window, the soft gleam of cabin lights brushing over her cheekbones, catching in the gold chain of her necklace. My necklace. I watched the pearl rise and fall against her throat with each breath. My fingers itched to touch it but I restrained myself.

“You could say something,” I murmured.

She turned toward me, hazel eyes still glossy with disbelief. “Like what?” I shrugged, casual and controlled. “Anything. A thank you would suffice.”
She narrowed her eyes, but the smile tugging at the corner of her mouth betrayed her. “You’re impossible.”

“And yet, you’re here.”

She shook her head, but she was still smiling. She looked radiant—skin glowing, hair soft and swept behind one ear, one leg crossed over the other like she belonged in a glossy travel ad. Except she was mine, and there was nothing staged about this.

“I thought you said something about Vermont,” she said. “Snow and fires and reading in bed.”

“I lied.” “Obviously.”
I leaned back, letting my fingers brush the hem of her sleeve. “I found it three years ago— bookmark in some article about where European royals hide when they don’t want to be seen. I knew even then I’d take you”

The plane was moving now, rolling toward the runway. The engines began their slow build, a low hum beneath our seats, but all I could feel was the heat of her hand and the slow, steady

unraveling of control I hadn’t realized I still held.

“You’ve been planning this since before we met?” she whispered, sounding almost skeptic. I didn’t blink. “I always knew I’d need somewhere to take you when I didn’t want to share.”
She stared at me for a moment, the weight of what I was offering sinking in. It wasn’t freedom I promised her—not really. It was distance. Stillness. A place where the world could narrow to just this—just us. Where the noise fell away and nothing touched her unless I allowed it. She could forget who she had been before I touched her and begin to remember only what she had become in my hands. It was a kind of captivity, yes, but one shaped with such care and quiet devotion that it never needed force. Just the promise that she would be safe here, and the certainty that she wouldn’t want to leave.

“That sounds… perfect,” she said softly.

“It is,” I said. “Because it’s for you… For us.”

She looked down then, her fingers fidgeting with the hem of her cardigan, her voice lower. “It feels like a dream.”

“It isn’t,” I said, turning my hand palm-up between us. “This is real, Caroline. The stone beneath your feet, the salt in the air, the life I built to hold you. You’ll wake to it every morning—because it was always meant to be yours. And I intend to give it to you, again and again.”

She slid her hand into mine without hesitation.

“You’ve worked hard this year. I figured you earned a view,” I continued. She tilted her head. “Is this your version of spoiling me or conditioning me?”
I smiled. “Yes. You’ll love it. There’s a local chef I hired for the week. A library tucked into the cliffs. And a terrace you won’t leave once you see the view.”

She looked down at our hands, her voice barely above the hum of the engines. “And you? What will you be doing while I’m locked on the terrace?”

I turned my head, letting my lips brush the edge of her ear. “Ruining you. Quietly. Completely.”

The plane lifted and just like that, the last of the world slipped away. There were only twelve hours between us and the place I’d built to keep her. A place where I wouldn’t have to share her with anyone. A place where I could finally give her everything -and take everything in return.


Caroline slept for the first half of the flight. I didn’t blame her—she’d been fluttery and overstimulated at the airport, eyes darting between Italian signage and the soft leather of the

first-class cabin like she couldn’t decide which detail to be overwhelmed by first.

She’d worn a travel set that I had laid out for her that morning—a soft beige cashmere two- piece with a matching wrap, elegant but easy. Her necklace glinted when the cabin lights dimmed. She never took it off.

Now, her head rested on my shoulder, her breathing soft and even. Her hand curled loosely against my chest, fingers twitching faintly in sleep. I didn’t move. I barely breathed. Not because I feared waking her, but because there was a kind of sacred stillness in this—her draped over me like she was born to fit there.

We landed just past sunrise. She blinked sleepily as the car pulled up on the tarmac beside the plane, and I helped her down the steps. The sky was the pale pink of early spring, the kind of quiet light that made everything feel softer. Our driver opened the door of the vintage Alfa Romeo I had delivered to the airstrip, and she hesitated for a moment before slipping in, her hand smoothing over the leather seat.

“You’re spoiled,” I said, as I climbed in beside her.

“Yeah, by you,” she replied, already curling toward the window, watching the landscape shift as we pulled onto the winding road that hugged the coastline.

Olive trees bowed in the distance, wind-soft and silver-edged. The scent of sea salt crept through the open window, mingling with the faint notes of citrus and petrol and the lingering trace of her perfume.

“Tell me about the villa,” she said, eyes still fixed on the sea.

I smiled. “Seventeenth-century limestone estate. Gated, remote, views of the Amalfi. Staffed, obviously. Chef’s already stocked the kitchen with everything you like. There’s a piano. A library. A marble tub big enough for two.”

She looked at me then, and I could see the dazed kind of awe she was trying not to show too much of. “And how exactly did you find this place?”
“I didn’t find it,” I said. “I asked a local broker for something with history. Something quiet. Something not listed anywhere public.”
She gave a low laugh. “Of course you did.”
I leaned in closer, fingers ghosting down her arms. “The owner only rents it a few times a year, mostly to people they know. I paid extra for discretion—and for them to be gone long before we arrived.”
“So it’s just a rental?”
“For now.” I leaned in, my voice just behind her ear. “I wanted to see how it fit you first.” Her breath caught, but she didn’t look away. She never did, anymore.

“So,” she said quietly, “this is… your idea of paradise?”

“No,” I replied, my voice low. “You are. The villa just gives me an excuse to keep you somewhere beautiful. Somewhere no one else gets to touch.”

The wind picked up, ruffling strands of her hair across her cheek. I reached over and tucked them behind her ear, slow and reverent.

“And you’re sure I packed the right things?” she asked, finally teasing.

I smirked. “You packed what I told you to pack. And the rest is already waiting in the closet.”

She gave a soft laugh, but it was breathless, like part of her still hadn’t caught up to the reality of all this.

“How long did you plan this?” “Since December. Maybe longer.”
She shook her head, almost in disbelief.

“You think you’re hard to love,” I said, watching her. “But I’ve never known anything easier.”

Her eyes glistened, just for a second, before she turned back to the view.

We fell into silence, but it wasn’t empty. The road curved, the sea opened up beside us, and for a moment it felt like time had stretched out just for us. No demands. No performance. Just her, and me, and the slow, deliberate unfolding of something that felt permanent.


The driver turned off the coastal road just as the sun crested its descent into the horizon, casting the sky in molten streaks of rose gold and umber. The wheels crunched over gravel, the road narrowing into a winding path shaded by tall cypress trees and low stone walls, their surfaces dappled with ivy and age.

Caroline stirred beside me, eyes wide, lips slightly parted. “Is this it?”

“Mm.” I reached over, brushing my knuckles against the inside of her knee, “Almost.”

The villa revealed itself slowly, as if the land had been keeping it secret. Pale limestone walls washed in the softest blush of sunset, windows framed by dark green shutters, and a wraparound terrace with ironwork that caught the last of the light like filigree. It was perched above the cliffs, watching the sea like it had done so for centuries. Timeless. Private. Ours.

“Liam…”

I didn’t look at her. I was watching the way the light hit the glass doors, already imagining her silhouetted there. Her hair mussed from sleep, one of my shirts slipping off her shoulder. The image sank into my chest like satisfaction.

The driver parked in the gravel roundabout, and I stepped out first. The air was perfumed with rosemary, sea salt, and citrus. I circled the car to open her door before she could reach for the handle.

She stepped out slowly, her heels crunching against the path, and turned in a full circle, breath catching.

“This isn’t a vacation house,” she said finally. “This is a Bond villain lair.” I laughed, pressing a kiss to her temple. “You say that like it’s a bad thing.”
A housekeeper emerged from the entry, a woman in her fifties with salt-and-pepper hair tied in a low bun. She greeted us with warm Italian, and Caroline returned the greeting in careful, respectful reply. She always met the moment with grace, and I let myself admire her for it, confirming what I already knew - she was meant for everywhere I placed her.

The woman ushered us inside. The light hit Caroline first. It spilled in through the arched glass doors, golden and fractured, catching in her hair and lighting up the pale curve of her cheek. She stepped over the threshold like she was afraid to disturb the air, her fingers brushing against the carved stone doorway as if needing to confirm it was real. I didn’t say anything. I just watched.

The interior was cool and spare, a blend of centuries-old stone and minimalist restoration. High ceilings. Terracotta floors. Linen-draped windows that billowed gently from the sea breeze.

She paused in the center of the villa’s grand room—all ivory walls and vaulted ceilings, aged wood beams, and open archways that led to the sea. Her bag slid from her shoulder, landing softly on the antique rug, forgotten.

“I want to get married here,” she said. Quiet. Barely audible. Like it had slipped past her lips before she could catch it.

My throat went tight. Not because it surprised me. Because she said it without thinking.
Because something inside her already knew.

She turned to face me a beat later, eyes wide with embarrassment, her hands covering her mouth as if to take it back. I didn’t let her.

I crossed the room slowly, deliberately. She didn’t move, nor did she look away. Her pulse flickered beneath the delicate skin of her throat, and I knew she felt it too—the weight of the moment, the inevitability of it.

“Say it again,” I said, moving her hands from her face.

She shook her head, a soft laugh catching in her throat. “I didn’t mean—”

“Sweetheart,” I cut in, low and serious now. I brushed her hair back, my fingers lingering at her jaw. “Say it again.”

She swallowed. Met my eyes. “I want to get married here.”

God, she didn’t even hear what that did to me.

I leaned in, pressing my mouth to her temple, breathing her in. “Good,” I murmured. “Because you will. Not today, not this week. But here, yes.”

I stepped behind her, wrapping my arms around her waist and drawing her back into my chest. She let me.

We stood like that for a long moment, looking out the open doors to the sea. The kind of view people traveled lifetimes for. She drifted forward, slow and reverent, fingers brushing the archway as she stepped through it and out into the gold-washed air. I followed a beat behind.

She leaned against the rail and stared. The ocean stretched out in every direction, glittering and endless, crashing against the cliffs far below.

“You planned this. Every detail.” “Of course.”
She turned to look at me, eyes bright with disbelief and something softer beneath it. “Why?”

I stepped in close, settling my hands on either side of her hips, caging her against the rail without touching. “I thought you’d like it,” I murmured against her temple.

She let out a shaky breath. “Like it? It doesn’t feel real.”

“It’s real. Everything here—every room, every sunrise you’ll see through these windows, every night you fall asleep with the sea at your back—it’s yours.”

She closed her eyes like it hurt to believe it. I didn’t move. I didn’t need to.

When she opened them again, she looked at me the way she had the first time I claimed her— equal parts fear and awe. It still stirred something in me. That she could look at me like that and still stay.

I brushed my knuckles along her cheekbone. “Welcome to paradise, sweetheart.”

She leaned into me, body relaxing by degrees, until the tension that had crept in with the surprise melted beneath my hands. This was why I’d brought her here—not for the coastline, breathtaking as it was, or the view that seemed carved from a dream, or even the kind of privacy most people couldn’t buy if they tried. I chose it because I knew that the moment she saw it, she’d understand. She’d see it the way I did—not as a vacation, not as an escape—but as ours. Every room, every weathered stone, every horizon stretching into light. All of it— meant for her.
I eased back just enough to take her in. Her cheeks were flushed, her expression softened by awe… and something quieter, deeper. Something that looked like surrender, “Come see the rest,” I said, threading our fingers together. “You’ll want to know where the ceremony will be.” I teased.

She laughed this time, but her grip didn’t loosen, just followed me without hesitation.

I didn’t tell her that I may have already spoken to the villa’s housekeeper about future reservations and that I now knew the best months for sunset ceremonies. I would run background checks on every wedding vendor within a hundred-mile radius when we actually planned our wedding.

But she wasn’t ready to know that yet. We both knew that when the time came, she’d say yes (not that I would ever give her much of a choice) — in a way she already had.
We moved through the villa in unspoken rhythm, the last of the sun casting long, golden shadows across the stone floors. She didn’t let go of my hand. Outside, the sea kept its slow, endless rhythm, a lullaby against the cliffs. Inside, everything felt suspended—like time had softened around us, stretching to make room for something rare. I didn’t need to say it, and she didn’t ask. We had the whole week ahead of us where nothing else existed. Just her. Just this. Just us.

Chapter 2: Marble Tub Confessionals.



The tub was carved from pale marble, veined with soft gold, sunken into the floor like it had been placed there by gods. Candlelight flickered on the walls, the scent of lavender and lemons curling like smoke through the air. Caroline sat between my legs, her back resting against my chest, her skin warm and slick from the bath oils I’d poured in myself.

The villa was silent around us. We’d let it go still. The whole world had narrowed to this— the water, the steam, the quiet sound of her breath against my collarbone.

Her fingers traced idle circles over my forearm, where it wrapped possessively around her waist. Every so often she’d tilt her head back against my shoulder just enough to glance up, as if to confirm I was still watching her. Still here.

Of course I was. I hadn’t been able to taken my eyes off her all night.

She let out a soft sigh, a contented little sound that burrowed beneath my ribs and took up residence.

Then she spoke—low, almost sleepy, but deliberate.

“Tell me everything about the future you’ve planned for us.” Her voice was barely above a whisper. “I want to know everything.”

I didn’t answer right away —not that I didn’t know— but because I wasn’t sure how much she was ready to hear.

I kissed her shoulder, slow and reverent, my lips lingering just long enough to make her shiver, “We talked about this before.”

She nodded, the motion brushing damp curls against my chin. “Tell me again” I let a moment sit between us and then spoke.
I didn’t tell her everything—but enough: “That cabin in Vermont? The one you thought we were going to for Spring Break, that will be ours; a place to retreat when the world gets too loud. Books, fireplace, watching the foliage change, the snow fall outside, our domestic

escape. Do you ski?” She shook her head, “You’ll learn next winter then. We’ll ski then enjoy the fire at night.”

She tilted her head, eyes closed, soaking in the words like warmth.

“Let’s see, I’ve already mapped out the rest of your undergrad schedule. You’ll finish with highest honors, top of your class, of course.”

“Naturally.”

“Your thesis advisor doesn’t know it yet, but he’s about to discover just how brilliant you really are.” She smiled faintly at that, curling deeper into me. “You’ll apply to top fellowships,” I continued. “The ones with housing in D.C. or New York. Not because you’ll need it, but because it’ll look good. You’ll have options. Everyone will want you, but you’ll take the one closest to where I’m working.”

I ran my palm down the length of her torso, possessive even in stillness.

“And you’ll say ‘yes’ when I ask,” I murmured against her temple. “Not because you feel pressured, but because you’ll have already decided I’m the only man who ever really saw you.”

She turned slightly, just enough to meet my eyes. “Where will you be?” she asked softly. “In this future?” I smiled.
“Beside you. Behind you. In front of you—wherever you need me to be.” I kissed her neck again, lower this time, tasting salt and steam. “But always yours.”

She was quiet for a moment. And then: “What about after?” “After what?”
“After school. After fellowships or clerkships or stunts with the PeaceCorps.”

My arms tightened around her. “You’re not joining the PeaceCorps - it’d be a waste of your talents. But to answer your question: Then we do it again. A new chapter, maybe a new location. A life full of curated decisions and quiet victories. You’ll publish. You’ll speak.
You’ll reform policy and programs. You’ll redefine what it means to be a woman with power. And I’ll make sure the world never touches you the wrong way.”

She turned fully then, shifting to straddle me, the water lapping gently around us. “And what if I want something else?” she asked.
I brushed a lock of hair from her cheek, letting the silence stretch before I answered.

“Then I’ll shape the world to give it to you. I want the most beautiful, most brilliant, sharpest woman next to me. If you outgrow the plans we make, we recalibrate. Together.”

I felt her breath catch but I didn’t let her speak. Instead, I kissed her—slow, certain, claiming, as if sealing our fate.

She didn’t pull back from the kiss, not right away, but when she did, it wasn’t because she was retreating. Her eyes were wide and steady on mine, searching—not for affection. For truth.

“What about kids?” she asked quietly.

The steam curled between us like smoke. Her thighs were still warm around my hips, her hands splayed across my chest, but her voice had shifted. Not dreamy. Not teasing.

Genuine.

“What about them?” I murmured.

She swallowed, a small motion I felt against my mouth before she spoke again. “When?” I didn’t answer right away.
I studied her face instead. The flushed pink of her cheeks. The faint freckle beneath her right eye. Her pulse fluttering fast at her throat. I saw the unspoken question behind the ask. When will you take me from my own ambition? When will you pull me back and cut me down to size. But that was never my desire. Our children would be raised and loved by us, regardless if they were cared for by a nanny, au pair or by Caroline herself.

“When we’re ready,” I said finally. “Maybe after the fellowship, maybe sooner.” She nodded slowly, as if she’d expected that. But her gaze didn’t break.
“Two,” I said more softly, recalling earlier conversations – how different that context was compared to now. “Maybe three. I like the idea of a girl first. Smart. Quiet. The kind who watches everything before she speaks. Just like her mother.”

Caroline flushed deeper, her mouth parting—but I wasn’t finished.

“Then a boy,” I went on, brushing her damp hair back from her face. “He’ll worship her. And both of them will know who you are. Not just the mother who packed their lunches and taught them Latin roots, but the woman who earned her name before she ever changed it.”

Her eyes shimmered, but she didn’t let the tears fall. She was stronger than that. She always had been.

“We’ll need a nanny or au pair,” she said casually. I nodded in agreement.

“And a chef since you hate cooking,” I teased. “You really are spoiled.” I kissed the tip of her nose.

“And where would we live?” she asked, her voice soft again.

“Here in the summers,” I said, “until they’re old enough to know how lucky they are. Vermont, when we want stillness. D.C. during my clerkship and while you’re dominating whatever NGO you’ve taken over, then wherever I’m appointed. But eventually?”

I cupped her jaw. “New York. A townhouse on the Upper East Side. Quiet on the outside. But inside, full of power.”

She smiled, slow and careful.

“And what about your political career?”

The words were gentle, yet deliberate. She was testing to see whose career would take priority when the time came.

I looked her straight in the eye. “I’ll run when it’s time. Not before. Not a second too early.” “And me?”
“You’ll be by my side,” I said. “Perfect. Poised. The secret to my appeal.” Her brow furrowed slightly. “And if I don’t want to be the secret?”
“You won’t be,” I said. “You’ll be the spine of it. Every policy, every handshake, every move I make—anchored by you. They’ll admire me, but they’ll respect you because they’ll know I don’t stand without you behind me.”

She exhaled, trembling.

My thumb traced her bottom lip.

“And when they ask how I did it,” I said, “how I stayed clean, how I built a name without ever falling—I’ll tell them the truth.”

She blinked. “Which is?”

“I chose the right girl, and never let her go.”

Caroline leaned forward, pressing her forehead to mine. “You really believe all that?” she whispered.
I didn’t blink. “I don’t believe it,” I said. “I’ve already made it true.”

And then I pulled her back into me—because the future wasn’t waiting to be forged, it had already been put into motion. It was already ours.

She rested her head on my shoulder, her skin warm against mine, candlelight dancing along her shoulders, soft and golden. Her fingers trailed along my chest—slow, thoughtful, almost absentminded—but her eyes were anything but distracted.

She looked at me like she already belonged to me. Like she had for a long time.
Then, so quietly it felt like a secret just for us, she said, “I’m yours. But I’m never letting you go either.”

Something in me stilled.

Not surprise. Not even pride. Just a dark, quiet certainty settling deeper into my bones. I’d always known I had her, but this—this was her claiming me back. I fucking loved it.

I didn’t say anything at first. Just watched her. Let it land. Let it echo in the steam around us and settle into the heat between our bodies. Then I reached for her jaw, tilting her face toward mine, slow and reverent.

“You won’t have to,” I said. My voice was low, the kind of low that left no room for argument. “Because there’s no version of this where I walk away. You get that, right?”

She nodded, her breath catching a little.

“No matter what happens,” I went on, my thumb brushing along her lower lip, “you hold on. You don’t run. You don’t push. You stay: loyal and disciplined, honest and open to me. The rules we’ve already set.”

“I won’t run,” she whispered. “There would be nowhere for me to go.”

I kissed her then—deep, anchoring, like I could etch the moment into both of us.

Goddamn, she didn’t know what it did to me, hearing her say it like that – not just surrender, but possession. She didn’t need to let me go. I would never let myself belong to anyone else, who else was there?

She shifted in the water, turning again so that her back pressed tighter against my chest, her head tipping until it rested just beneath my jaw. The golden glow ensconced us in a way that felt ethereal. The bathwater had gone tepid, but she hadn’t asked to get out, nor was I in any rush.

Then she spoke—quiet, deliberate, the way one might utter a confession in a church pew. “I understand it now.”
My hand stilled where it had been trailing lazily along her thigh. The muscles in her legs softened under mine, and I could feel the edge of tension—the weight of something about to break open.

She continued without prompting: “Love,” she said, her voice low, the steam catching in her throat. “The way you described it. I didn’t, not at first. I fought it. I thought it was control, or maybe obsession. I didn’t understand what it meant to be chosen like that. To be… built into someone’s future before I even realized I’d agreed to stay.”

My grip around her waist tightened, not painfully, but with unmistakable certainty. “And now?” I asked.
She inhaled slowly, fingers lifting to brush the back of my hand. “Now I see it. It’s not a feeling – not the way other people describe it. It’s architecture. It’s being remade by someone who already saw your shape, your purpose—long before you ever could.”

She turned then, slowly, so we were face to face. Her knees drew up between us in the wide tub, water shifting around her like silk. Her eyes found mine, unguarded. Hazel, but dark in the low light. Fierce in the way only surrender could be.

“I love you,” she said. “And not because it’s easy, not because it’s soft. I love you because you saw me before I even existed like this, because you made this version of me real.”

Her palm came to rest over my chest, above the beat she owned.

“I’m yours. I’m not going anywhere either. I’m not letting go. Not in a year, not in ten. Not in this lifetime or the next.”

Something sharp and brutal swelled in my chest. Not pain. Something better.

“I never wanted easy,” I said, the words low against her lips. “I wanted irrevocable.” She kissed me, then deliberately, not with need, with promise.
When she pulled back, I traced the pearl at her throat, the one she never took off. My claim. My reminder.

“We’ll raise our children to believe in this kind of love,” I murmured. “The kind you don’t fall into. The kind you build, that you bleed for.”

Her eyes filled with something I couldn’t name.

She nodded. “ Then show me what it means to be yours, in a way no one else ever has been. ”

And I would.

God help the world, I would.

Chapter 3: Pasta like a Promise.




Next Morning.

The light poured in through the arched window in slow, golden layers—honeyed and warm, like something ancient and deliberate. I’d left the curtains half-drawn on purpose. She slept better in darkness, but I wanted the morning to claim her gently. No alarms. No schedule. Just her, in my bed, in my world.

She stirred beside me, a quiet shift beneath the linen sheets. One bare leg curled against mine, and I felt her toes brush instinctively against my ankle. Always reaching for contact, even in sleep. I didn’t move—just watched. The fall of her chestnut hair over her cheek, the way her fingers curled toward the pillow like she was still holding onto a dream.

When her eyes fluttered open, hazel and still half-dazed, she blinked at the ceiling for a beat before turning toward me.

I smiled. “Buongiorno, amore mio.”

She gave a sleepy little exhale. “You speak Italian now?”

I reached out, brushing my knuckles down her arm, slow and light. “Only when I’m trying to seduce my wife-to-be.”

Her rolled her eyes, but she didn’t pull away. “Fiancé comes first, you know.” I shrugged. “Minor detail.”
She laughed, soft and low, and let her head fall back into the pillow. “Tell me there’s coffee,” she murmured.
“There’s coffee, and breakfast, and whatever you want.” I leaned in, pressing a kiss to her temple. “But first, I want to see you in the sunlight again.”

She groaned and tugged the sheet over her face, not that it mattered. I’d already seen it, the way the morning touched her skin like it had been waiting for her all night, the faint marks on her hips from where I’d gripped her last night, the lazy smile she didn’t try to hide. All mine.

She didn’t move for a long time. Just laid there, half under the sheet, her cheek pressed to the pillowcase, eyes half-lidded as she watched me like I was a dream she wasn’t sure she wanted to wake from. I knew the look. I felt it in my chest. I slowly pulled the sheet down to reveal her face, her eyes still adjusting to the early hour.

God, I loved her like this: Quiet. Soft. A little messy from sleep, but entirely mine. There was nothing composed about her right now, no calculated smile, no polished reply for my parents or professors or the public. Just Caroline—bare and blinking, wrapped in the bed I’d brought her to.

I reached over and slipped one of the straps of her silk sleep top back up onto her shoulder, letting my fingers linger on the slope of her skin.

“Still tired?” I asked, though I already knew the answer.

She nodded, slow and exaggerated, then let her arm slide across my stomach like she meant to anchor herself there. Her fingers idly traced the edge of my ribs. She did that sometimes when she was barely awake—like her body remembered needing to be close even before her mind had caught up.

“Let’s never leave,” she mumbled into my chest.

“Tempting,” I said, resting my hand over hers. “But I think I’m supposed to run for office someday.”

Her hand stopped moving. “Can’t you just work remotely? Or work at a position in Rome? Let’s just start our future here.”

I smirked. “Sweetheart, if I had my way, we’d already be married, and I’d have destroyed your passport.”

She laughed, eyes still closed. “Romantic.”

I leaned down, brushing her hair back, my lips near her temple. “Don’t need grand gestures. I just need this.”

She tilted her head toward me, eyes finally meeting mine. “You’re such a sap in the morning.”

“Like I’m the only one.”

There was a pause then—comfortable, full of something that felt like permanence. Her expression softened, like she could feel it too.

“I used to think mornings were the worst,” she said, her voice barely above a whisper. “Now I wake up and… it doesn’t feel daunting. It feels like there’s already a path beneath my feet.”

I swallowed against the sudden pull in my chest. “That’s what it should feel like.”

She didn’t respond—just curled in closer, pressing her cheek against the line of my shoulder, her body folding into mine like it had always belonged there. The villa was silent around us, the breeze drifting in through the open balcony doors, carrying with it the scent of lemon trees and salt air. Everything outside this bed was muted. Softened. Distant.

I could have stayed in that moment forever.

“You hungry?” I asked after a while.

She made a noise that might’ve been a yes or a maybe or just a contented sigh. I ran my hand slowly down her back. “Breakfast on the terrace? I’ll carry you.” She laughed against my skin. “You would.”
“Damn right.”

But neither of us moved, not yet, because the sunlight was just starting to stretch across the sheets, and her skin was too warm against mine, and we still had hours—whole lazy, lovesick hours—before the world could ask anything of us again.
And I wasn’t ready to give her up to the day -- not when I could still feel her heartbeat against my ribs, not when everything I’d ever wanted was already here, tangled beside me in this bed, whispering that she didn’t want to leave either.

The terrace overlooked the cliffs, the sea spread out below us like something hand-painted for our benefit alone. Pale stone stretched beneath our bare feet, still warm from yesterday’s sun, and the breakfast table—set for two—was already laid with white porcelain, seasonal fruit, espresso, and a carafe of something citrus-bright I hadn’t asked for but Caroline would like.

She curled her legs beneath her as she sat, the hem of her silk sleep shorts brushing mid- thigh, one of my old white button-downs hanging off her shoulders. She hadn’t bothered with a bra, and I didn’t bother hiding my satisfaction. She caught the look, of course. She always did. Her mouth curled as she reached for a slice of pear. “Stop looking at me like I’m something to devour.”

I sipped my espresso. “Too late.”

The sea breeze stirred her hair, catching the ends and pulling them around her cheek. She tucked it behind one ear with the kind of elegance that didn’t need to be taught—it was instinct. I wondered if she knew how much I admired her when she didn’t try.

When she simply was.

“So,” she said lightly, peeling a blood orange with careful fingers. “What are we doing today, Commander-in-Chief of all things planned?”

I raised an eyebrow. “Is that a complaint?”

“No,” she said, a beat too fast. Then, more honest: “Maybe a little. Do you ever get tired of planning everything?”

I leaned back in my chair, watching her. The way she tilted her head, the way her fingers paused mid-motion. She wasn’t criticizing—she was curious. Testing the edges of control like she sometimes did, not to pull away but to understand it.

“I don’t plan because I have to,” I said, voice even. “I plan because I want it to be perfect. For you…for me too.”

She looked up at that, a flicker of emotion catching in her expression. She tried to hide it by lifting the espresso to her lips, but I caught it anyway.

“You don’t trust anyone else to make it perfect,” she said, more softly this time. “No,” I said. “I don’t.”
There was a pause—long enough to taste.

“I just… wondered if it ever felt like a burden,” she said, shrugging one shoulder. “Knowing I’m always going to look to you for the next step.”

I set my cup down. Reached for her hand across the table. “Sweetheart, that’s not a burden. That’s the reward.”

She smiled, barely, like she didn’t quite believe me. Or maybe like she believed me too much.

“Tell you what, I’ll let you pick lunch,” I offered. “One menu, three courses, and no Google reviews beforehand.”

She feigned shock. “Freedom? In this economy?” “Temporary freedom,” I clarified. “Under supervision.”
She laughed softly through closed lips, her thumb was stroking over mine where our hands had joined. I knew she liked all of this—the curation, the assurance, the structure. She’d stopped resisting the rhythm months ago. Now she moved with it.

Caroline bit into the orange slice. Juice caught the corner of her lip. I reached out and wiped it with my thumb, slow, reverent.

“Anything else you’re curious about?” I asked.

She tilted her head, amused. “You mean besides where you’re taking me tonight, what I’ll be wearing, what wine you’ll order, and whether I’ll be allowed to make any choices without prior approval?”

My mouth curved. “Very funny. For that, maybe I won’t share any plans for the rest of the trip —you’ll just dress in what I lay out and follow me wherever I lead. Now wouldn’t that be fun.”

She shook her head in dismissal, laughing, the sound catching in the sun-warmed air. She looked so light in that moment it almost made my chest ache.

I realized, again, as I had a hundred times before—that this was what it meant to win: To wake up with her still wrapped around me, to lead her out into the day I’d built, to watch her

laugh because she felt safe, and bright, and mine. Always mine.

She finished her espresso slowly, curled into the corner of her chair, bare legs tucked under her and hair swept over one shoulder like it belonged there.

I let the silence stretch. She didn’t fill it—she didn’t need to anymore. That was one of the things I loved most about her now. The stillness she carried. The way she didn’t need to perform to be seen. She knew she was seen. She knew she was mine and the security that carried.

“I thought we’d walk into town this afternoon,” I said casually, pouring more juice into her glass. “There’s a woman in town who runs a private cooking class. She teaches out of her home—Old-school kitchen, wood-fired stove, lemon trees outside the window. She only takes couples. No walk-ins”

Caroline gave me a look over the rim of her glass. “You do remember I hate cooking, right?” “I do,” I said, unbothered. “That’s why I arranged it.”
Her brow lifted. “Your logic is truly impeccable.”

“It’s an experience,” I replied. “You knead dough, flirt with the other student, drink too much local wine—everyone wins.”

She leaned back in her chair, skeptical. “Does she speak English?”

“Only to her dog, apparently,” I said. “But I speak enough Italian to manage.”

Caroline snorted. “You don’t speak Italian. You attempt to speak Latin with misplaced confidence.”

“Same roots,” I said smoothly. “Same roots, different century.”
I ignored her. “You’ll get by just fine. Your accent’s better than mine, but I’ve got the grammar.”

“Keep telling yourself that, amantis.”

“Cute.” I grinned, impressed that she knew the word for “lover” in Latin. I shouldn’t be surprised. “She said we’re making scialatielli al limone,” I continued. “And before you ask
—that’s not a spell, it’s pasta.”

Her smile curved, faint but amused. “You booked this weeks ago, didn’t you?” “Of course I did.”
She stretched then, slow and feline, her shirt lifting just enough to make it difficult to think in full sentences. “And after I offend a kindly Italian grandmother by burning her pasta?”

“We’ll walk through the lemon groves. You’ll pretend not to take photos, I’ll pretend not to watch you. Then I’ll take us down to the beach below the villa and we’ll ruin that linen dress you packed for dinners.”

She stilled, laughter catching in her throat. “No! I like that one!” “I’ll buy you three more.”
“And the lemon groves?”

“I’ll buy those too, if you want.”

She laughed then, real and soft and unguarded. “Sei fuori di testa.” You’re out of your mind.

I stood, stepping behind her chair to bend low and press my mouth to the spot just below her ear. “No, sweetheart. I’m just very, very in love.”


The kitchen looked like something out of a regional cookbook from the 1970s—sun-warmed tile, herbs strung over the window like dried relics, copper pans dented with use and reverence. The kind of place that pretended to be charming by accident, but was absolutely intentional in every detail. Just like Caroline.

Nonna barely cleared five feet and had the command of a five-star general. She barked instructions in rapid-fire Italian the second we stepped through the door—pointed, declarative, no translations offered. Caroline’s face lit up like she understood every word. I caught "coppia" and "mano," maybe something about rhythm. Caroline just nodded sweetly and rolled up her sleeves like this wasn’t a setup.

“She said to work together,” I told her, handing her a ball of dough. “Like a well-oiled team.”

“She said ‘like husband and wife,’” Caroline corrected under her breath, already kneading. “Close enough though.”

Caroline stood beside me in an apron she’d clearly tried not to wear—sleeves rolled, hair tucked behind her ear, already dusted in flour like she belonged here, even though she insisted she hated cooking. She didn’t look like she hated it now.

Nonna was tiny and fast-moving, like a kitchen spirit too old to tolerate laziness. She pointed at the dough, then gestured sharply between our hands and the bowl of warm water.

"Lavorate insieme," she snapped. "Come marito e moglie. Altrimenti—la pasta vi tradisce."

Caroline’s mouth curved. “She wants us to mix it like husband and wife. Otherwise, the pasta will betray us.”

I glanced down at the dough, then back at her. “That’s a lot of pressure for something made of carbs.”

Caroline shrugged one shoulder, already reaching for the flour. “She also said the dough can smell pride.”

“She’s weaponizing carbohydrates,” I muttered.

“She does know we’re not married, right?” she whispered.

I just shrugged, she shook her head muttering something in Italian I didn’t quite catch.

Caroline’s hands were already in the bowl, moving with slow, deliberate precision—dragging water through flour, letting it cling to her skin, gathering it into something real. I followed her lead, flattening my palms, folding slowly. She barely looked at me, but I could feel her registering every motion. She leaned her weight forward, flour catching in the fine hairs at her temples. My brain short-circuited for a moment watching her—sunlight haloing her shoulders, her lips parted slightly as she focused, the curve of her hip brushing mine as she shifted.

Nonna passed behind us again, muttering as she inspected our progress, pulling me out of my reverie. She stopped by Caroline, nodded once in silent approval, then turned to me and lifted the rope of dough I’d just shaped. Her expression twisted in disappointment—like I’d personally offended her ancestors.

“She said you’re too aggressive,” Caroline translated, deadpan. “And emotionally unavailable.”

“That can’t be right.”

“She said you folded it like a man who doesn’t believe in compromise.”

I stared at the little pasta rope like it had personally betrayed me. “Is that really what she said?”

“More or less.”

I narrowed my eyes. “You’re enjoying this.”

“Deeply,” she said, then leaned in, dropping her voice. “She also said the way you fold pasta says everything about how you treat a woman’s heart.”

I paused. That earned a small laugh from me—more reflex than amusement. “And how do you think I treat yours?”

Caroline didn’t answer right away. Her hands kept moving, kneading and rolling, stretching and cutting. “You handle it like something that belongs to you,” she said finally, eyes still on her task. “Which is accurate. Just not always gentle.”

There it was. Not accusation. Not softness either. Just truth, spoken without hesitation.

I took it without comment and copied Caroline’s technique—slower this time, more deliberate. If I was going to be judged by the quality of my pasta, I was at least going to

outperform every fictional husband she’d ever imagined.

Nonna circled like a hawk, inspecting our work. She stopped beside me, lifted another rope of dough I’d just rolled, and tsked under her breath.

“Troppo freddo,” she muttered, pinching the end. “Troppo controllo. Le mani devono essere vive.”

Caroline coughed to cover a laugh. “What now?”
“She said your hands are cold. Controlling. Too technical.” “Cold?” I echoed. “My hands are warm.”
“She means metaphorically,” Caroline said, still smiling. “She says the dough should feel your intentions. Not your precision.”

“She’s attacking my philosophy.” “She’s Italian.”
I nodded slowly. “You know, for someone who hates cooking, you’re oddly competent at it.” “You already knew this,” she teased.
“I’m just saying, you’re very…” I studied her as she shaped the dough into soft, lemon- scented strands, cheeks flushed, arms dusted with flour. “…pliable when you want to be.”

Nonna said something else, this time with a smirk, and motioned to the scialatielli stretched between Caroline’s fingers.

Caroline blinked. “Oh my God.” “What?”
“She said the way I handle dough means I’ll be a patient mother and a… stubborn wife.” I raised a brow. “She said all that from your pasta technique?”
“She said I roll like someone who likes to win quietly.” I grinned. “That is dangerously accurate.”
We worked like that for a while, in tandem. A shared rhythm. The sound of Nonna’s instructions folding into the music on the radio. The scialatielli strands grew long and even, dusted with semolina and laced with lemon peel. Caroline moved with a sort of quiet confidence that made my throat tighten. Her brow furrowed slightly when she worked, like she was trying to master something more than pasta.

