Sunlight from the porthole streaked of light across the bedroom’s walls, dancing in the corners like quiet witnesses. I reached for the sheet and pulled it around us, not because she was cold—but because she was mine, and covering her felt like claiming her all over again.

She made a soft sound, not quite a sigh, not quite a word. Her fingers traced mindless patterns over my chest, delicate and slow, the way she always did when she was too content to form a thought but too awake to sleep. For a while, I let her touch me in silence. Let her explore whatever she needed to find.

Then I asked, softly, like the words weren’t already decided for us, “What do you want to do tomorrow?”

She shifted just enough to glance up at me, brows slightly drawn in confusion. “You’re asking me?”

“I’m allowed,” I murmured, brushing a thumb over her jaw. “Even dictators take suggestions.”

She smiled, sleepily, and nestled closer. “I don’t know. I thought you had everything planned already.”

“I do,” I said. “But that doesn’t mean I don’t want to hear what you want.”

Her expression softened, surprise flickering behind her eyes. It wasn’t that I never gave her choices—it was just that the illusion of choice had always come with rails. This felt different. I saw the way she registered it. Processed it.

She bit her lip. “What if I say I want to stay in bed all day?”

“Then I’ll cancel the driver. And the lunch reservation. And the private tour I booked.” “You did not book a private tour.”
I arched a brow. “You’re on the Amalfi Coast, sweetheart. Did you think I wouldn’t exploit every inch of it for you?”

She laughed, the sound curling against my skin like a reward. “You’re too much.” “No,” I corrected, kissing the top of her head. “I’m yours.”
Her hand paused where it traced over my chest. For a heartbeat, she just lay there—quiet, steady, wrapped around me like something sacred.

Then she whispered, almost shyly, “We could do the tour. If you want.”

I smiled into her hair. “We’ll do both. The bed. Then the tour. Maybe we’ll even make it to dinner.”

She hummed. “And after that?”

“After that,” I said, voice low, “I’ll find a new way to make you mine again.”

And she didn’t argue, just brought my hand to her lips to kiss my knuckles, then settled against me. She was asleep within ten minutes.

I could tell by the way her breath shifted—just slightly, like her mind still hadn’t quite let go of mine. Her leg remained draped across my thigh, her arm curled against my chest, like she couldn’t bear even inches of space between us. Her skin still bore the faint flush of what I’d done to her, the curve of her hip marked with the ghosts of my fingers.

I hadn’t moved, where else would I want to be?

It was somewhere between the last kiss and the sound she made when she came apart under me, the thought had landed, heavy and unshakable.
What if I didn’t wait?
What if I didn’t follow the plan?

My father wanted law school, a future with a strong foundation. He wanted optics. He wanted practicality.

But I already had it. All of it. Caroline, soft and pliant in my arms, warm and flushed and still slightly dazed. Caroline, who wore my hoodie, my name, my schedule like second skin.
Caroline, who didn’t know yet that she would never get away—not because she couldn’t, but because she wouldn’t want to.

I stared at the crown of her head and imagined her in white. Not the symbolic kind. Not the someday kind. I meant now. I meant here. A remote villa on the Amalfi coast. A chapel carved into a hillside. A priest with just enough incentive to ignore paperwork, timelines,

consent issues. A tailor who worked fast, a boutique with lace soft enough for her skin. A single ring. It didn’t have to be the final one. Just… the first. A quiet one. For now.

It wouldn’t be public. Not yet but it would be binding. Because if she said yes, if she gave herself to me not just in body but in contract, then there wouldn’t be a damn thing anyone could do to pry her out of my arms. Not my father. Not the law. Not time. And God help the man who tried.

I didn’t realize how tightly I’d gripped her until she stirred. Her voice was sleepy, slurred, the kind of tone she only used when she trusted me completely.

“What are you thinking about?”

I hesitated. I could lie, maybe I should have but this wasn’t a moment that should have been tarnished with deception, not with her curled so perfectly against me, her heart beating right beside mine.

So I rolled to face her. Brushed a strand of hair from her cheek. And let the words fall soft and dangerous from my mouth. “You know,” I said, voice low, “there’s an old chapel a couple of miles from the villa.”

Her eyes fluttered open, hazel gone dark with curiosity. “Okay…”

“It’s remote, quiet, doesn’t even have a website.” I let my thumb trail her jaw. “I’m fairly certain the priest could be persuaded to perform a very private ceremony.”

She blinked.

I pressed forward, gentle but firm. “No press. No ring—yet. Not the final one at least. Just something simple. Something symbolic and binding. For us.”

“You mean…”

“I mean eloping, sweetheart.” My voice didn’t waver. “I mean no more questions. No more waiting. Just you and me. Tied together, the way we already are—but with vows.”

She stared at me like she wasn’t sure if I was serious. Like she didn’t know I’d already mapped the entire thing out in my head three times over. Dress fittings, forged documents, who I’d have to bribe to keep the records sealed, if that’d be necessary.

“I know what my father wants - he says it looks immature to marry too young, that the ground under our feet hasn’t settled,” I added. “But I don’t care about that. Not here, not with you.”

She was quiet. Processing. I didn’t press. Instead, I ran my fingers along her waist, grounding her. Grounding myself. And then: “I want this, Caroline. Not someday. Not after law school. Now. You as mine in everyway possible.”

Her breath caught. I watched her carefully—watched the way her mouth parted, the way her fingers curled tighter against my side. I could see the war in her eyes. The part of her that still

clung to logic. The part of her that loved me too much to resist.

This wasn’t a question, not really. It was a test that I already knew how she’d answer.

She didn’t pull away, not physically, not emotionally. She just stared at me, her pupils wide in the dark, her breath held like she didn’t trust it yet. I felt her heartbeat against my ribs, a hummingbird flutter beneath the calm. And then, finally—softly:

“I wasn’t expecting this.”

Her voice was careful. Not cold. Not afraid. Just… stunned.

I cupped the side of her face, let my thumb trace her cheekbone the way I did when I needed her to feel every syllable.

“I know but I want this now,” I murmured. “No extravagance, no family or more waiting. Just you and me. Ours.”

Her lips parted, but I didn’t give her time to argue—not yet.

“I know what you’re going to say,” I said. “You want a ring. A real proposal. Something polished and planned and approved.”

Her lashes lowered, a flicker of guilt.

“And you’ll have that,” I promised. “When we’re back in the States. When the time is right for the all invested parties: For the family, for the legacy, for the image that we curate.” I shifted closer, until our foreheads nearly touched. “But this, sweetheart… this is for us: a vow, a ceremony, a name.”

Her gaze met mine again, searching. “And if I say yes…” she asked, voice barely audible, “what happens when we go back?”

“We don’t tell anyone,” I said simply. “Not yet. We wear it like a secret. Like armor. You’ll still get your diamond, your hand-picked moment. But you’ll be mine completely.”

“You already think I am.”

“No,” I said, voice darker now. “I know you are. But this makes it permanent.”

She exhaled slowly. Her fingers flexed against my chest, like she was bracing herself—for me, for the truth, for the gravity. “I always thought…” she began, but trailed off.

“What?”

She looked up, hesitant. “When I got married, it would be when I was older. After school, once I got my footing in the world. Once everything made sense.”

I smiled, slow and razor-edged. “Nothing about us was ever supposed to make sense.” And then, because I needed her to feel it—not just hear it—I slid my hand to the back of her neck

and pulled her mouth to mine. Not demanding. Not claiming. Just… certain. When I pulled back, her eyes were a little glassy.
“And you’d still propose?” she asked, voice wavering.

“I already picked the ring.” I kissed the corner of her mouth. “This won’t change that. That one’s for the world.”

My thumb swept her lower lip, savoring the softness. “This one’s for me.”

“Why the chapel? Why not here? The captain can marry us.” I couldn’t tell if she was curious or impatient.

“Because you deserve a wedding that doesn’t include you wearing a bikini” She laughed.
Then she bit her lip before blushing. “Ask me.”

I sat up and looked down at her, brushing her temple to cheek with the back of my knuckles, “Say yes. Say you’ll stand with me in that chapel and bind yourself to me before God.”

I saw her breathe quiver before she nodded. In a soft whisper, so quiet that I may have missed it had I not been waiting, she said “yes.”

“I love you,” I grinned in a way that I knew looked foolish. I didn’t care. She was mine.

In every way that mattered. And soon, she’d wear it. In ink. In ceremony. In silence. Our secret marriage but our new beginning.

“I love you, too,” she reached up to touch me and bring my lips down to hers. I could feel her smiling against me, just as I couldn’t stop smiling either.

“Do I get to pick out a dress or will you just have one delivered?” she asked.

I laughed, holding her closer now, “Whichever you prefer, mi amore. Just as long as you’re saying your vows to me in 3 days.”

She nodded, “Three days?”

“That will be enough time for any arrangements.”

“You should brush up on your Italian then, since your vows will likely be in Italian.”

I kissed her forehead, “ No, Latin or English. Yours won’t be in Italian, either. I want to hear you bind yourself to me in a language I comprehend.”

She laughed softly at that, shaking her head before tucking herself back against me, the warmth of her body making it almost painful to think about letting her go. I let my palm rest

over the small of her back, feeling her heartbeat against my ribs, anchoring her there for just another breath.

“I’ll take care of everything,” I told her, not as a promise but as fact. “The chapel, the priest, the papers. You just have to show up.”

Her eyes flicked up to mine, and I could see the truth settling in—how real it already was. I kissed her once more, slower this time, like I was already sealing the vows we hadn’t yet spoken.

“We’ve got the rest of the evening,” I murmured, letting my eyes trace over her, memorizing her like this—bare legs tangled in the sheets, her hair fanned out like something divine. “Let’s go upstairs. Watch the sunset. Drink something cold while the whole damn horizon turns to gold. You’ll wear that smile, and I’ll be the only one who knows why. And I’ll pretend that the attention you receive from the crew isn’t enough to drive me into a jealous rage.”

Her blush deepened. "If you insist," she kissed my shoulder before stirring.

I rose, crossing to the wardrobe built into the cabin wall, and slid the doors open. I reached for the soft ivory sundress, cut low enough to remind her who chose it. I brought it over and set it on the bed beside her.

“I don’t want you prancing around in a bikini anymore today,” I said quietly. “This. For me.”

She slipped out from under the covers, the sunlight spilling through the porthole painting her skin in warm streaks as she stepped into it. I moved behind her, zipping the dress up her back, taking my time, my knuckles grazing the curve of her spine. She turned, smoothing the skirt, and I didn’t bother hiding the satisfaction in my gaze.

I changed into a short sleeve button down and lightweight pants that waited for me in the same wardrobe.

When she was ready, I took her hand and led her toward the stairs. The sound of the ocean grew louder with each step, the air shifting from the cabin’s warmth to the crisp salt of evening.

Up on deck, the horizon had already begun to melt into orange and rose. I stood behind her at the rail, my palms braced to either side of hers, the sea stretching out endless in front of us.

Three days. That was all I needed.

As the last rim of sunlight drowned in the horizon, I kissed her—not to let go, but to make it ours, sealed in heat and breath, unbroken even by nightfall.

Chapter 5: Marry Me.



The preparations were handled in a matter of days. No extravagance. No fanfare. Just precision.
As I made the necessary arrangements, Caroline busied herself in town in what I could only imagine was dress shopping or something like that.

The chapel was older than most of the villages in the valley, carved into the cliffs like a secret only the devout would ever find. The kind of place no one stumbled across by accident.

There was a bouquet was waiting for her at the entrance of the chapel—white garden roses, small and restrained. The priest didn’t ask many questions. He wasn’t the pastor of the chapel – that man had declined with trembling hands and a nervous look that told me he’d didn’t trust me or had heard of my family. Still everyone has a price, the priest who stood with us now knew better than to speak of what he saw. I’d made sure of that. His vows were delivered in accented English, thick but formal.

I’d bribed the parish, the notary, the local registrar. Paid extra to keep the documents from being scanned or filed beyond some hidden church record. There’d be no official record until I said there could be.

And now, she stood in the back of the stone chapel, the golden light of the Italian dusk slipping through the stained glass and scattering itself across her skin like a benediction, and I tried to remember how to breathe. The air itself seemed to still around her, holding its own reverence.

She wore a simple white dress that hugged her waist and slipped over her hips like it had been made for her alone—no glitter, no embroidery, nothing to distract from the quiet brilliance of her. A lace-trimmed veil rested against the soft curls spilling around her shoulders, framing her like something holy, like a relic that had somehow taken on the shape of a woman.

She didn’t look nervous. She looked inevitable. As if she’d always belonged in this place, in this light, in my life—like the path from that first moment I saw her had only ever been leading here. The space between us was measured in feet, but it felt like years I’d been

waiting to close it. I fought the urge to rock on my feet, that old nervous habit from my youth. I wasn’t nervous. I was burning.

She moved through the narrow aisle alone, the hush of the chapel so complete I could hear the faint rustle of lace with each step. Dust motes swirled in the shafts of dying light, turning her into something almost unreal—an apparition carved out of gold and shadow. My lungs forgot their work; I could do nothing but drink her in, the slow sweep of her gaze locking on mine until the rest of the world dissolved. By the time she reached me, I wasn’t sure if I’d been holding my breath for seconds or centuries, only that the moment she was finally beside me, the ground beneath us shifted, and nothing—nothing—would ever unmake it.

Her fingers slid into mine, warm and certain, and she smiled—just the smallest curve of her lips, like it was a secret she meant for me alone. “Hi,” she breathed, the word barely carrying between us.

I opened my mouth, meaning to say something composed, something worthy of this moment, but what came out landed somewhere between “wow” and “hi,” rough-edged and hopelessly inadequate. She blushed—God, she blushed—and it was the most beautiful thing I’d ever seen. My tie felt suddenly too tight, the weight of my dark suit too heavy for the body I was trying to keep still. I wasn’t thinking about the priest waiting or the words we’d be asked to speak. I was just standing there, every ounce of composure I’d ever honed threatening to crack under the sheer, unbearable fact of her, and the knowledge that she was about to be mine in name as much as in truth.

The priest began speaking. I didn’t hear most of it. I watched her instead—the way her lips parted as she breathed, the flutter in her pulse just beneath her neck, the way her fingers twitched slightly where they held the bouquet. She looked up at me, and I swore my knees nearly buckled.

God, she was beautiful, and she was going to be mine in every way that mattered. My wife. Fuck, the word nearly undid me.

“Do you, William James Barnette,” the priest intoned, “take this woman to be your lawfully wedded wife, in sickness and in health, in wealth and in poverty, forsaking all others until death do you part?”

“I do.” My voice cut through the still air. Certain. Final.

“And do you, Caroline Elizabeth Greer take this man to be your lawfully wedded husband in sickness and in health, in wealth and in poverty, forsaking all others until death do you part?”

She didn’t hesitate. “I do.”

My lungs expanded for the first time in what felt like years. The priest gestured. “The rings?”
I reached into my pocket—not for the ring I had envisioned for this moment. No, this one was smaller. Simpler. A thin band of platinum I’d chosen for the way it would glint against

her skin without drawing attention if anyone happened to notice.

I slid it onto her finger slowly, watching her mouth part as her breath caught, as I repeated back more vows and declarations.

Her hand trembled, but she didn’t pull away.

She gave me hers next—small, delicate, warm. I hadn’t let her see my ring before today. I wanted the moment.

It was heavier than hers. Matching platinum, like hers, but engraved inside. “Only ever yours.” along with today’s date.
Her lips parted. She read it silently before sliding it onto my finger, swearing the same as I had.

The priest pronounced us—his words gentle, reverent.

I didn’t wait for permission to kiss her. I stepped forward, took her face in both hands, and kissed her like I was branding her. My wife. My future. My only.

She melted into me—every inch, every bone, every breath. When we broke apart, she stared at me for a long time.
“Tell me how forever sounds in your voice” she looked up at me through dark lashes. I leaned close, lips brushing the shell of her ear.
“Permanent,” I said. And then I smiled. “Finally.”


The villa was drenched in silence when we returned. Not the quiet of vacancy, but something deeper. A hush meant for things sacred. The old stone walls didn’t echo—they absorbed. Like they knew what had just happened and swore to keep it.

She walked in first, barefoot now, heels dangling from her fingers. Her curls had loosened on the drive, tendrils falling around her flushed cheeks, and the train of her dress whispered across the tile like a secret. I followed her in, the chapel’s faint incense still clinging to us like smoke.

She stopped in the middle of the sitting room—one of many in this sprawling place—and just stood there. The fire was still burning low in the grate, untouched since this morning.

Shadows flickered across her face. Neither of us said anything at first.
Then, softly—like she didn’t want to break the spell—she turned. “Does anything change now?”

I studied her. She meant it. To her, this might still feel like something symbolic. Romantic. A whispered vow between two people so wrapped up in each other they couldn’t bear the world’s noise any longer, but to me—it wasn’t a symbol. It was a seal, an oath.

“No,” I said slowly. “And yes.”

Her brow lifted, but she didn’t speak.

I stepped closer, pulled her gently to sit beside me on the velvet settee. Her dress pooled around her like water. She looked fragile in the flickering light, but that was a lie—one I knew better than to believe. Fragile things shattered. She had only ever bent. Bent to me.

I took her hand and turned it over, her new ring catching the sunlight that streamed through the floor to ceiling windows.

“For now,” I murmured, twisting the band around her fingers. “Let’s keep this ours. When we are back, only wear it when we’re home by ourselves.”

She nodded faintly, trying not to look too disappointed. Then I reached into the pocket of my jacket.
“I brought you something else.”

It was a slender black box. Matte. Understated. When I opened it, her breath caught again— not in awe this time, but something quieter. Sharper.

It was a bracelet. Delicate, gold and minimalist—but affixed with a small, custom-designed lock in place of a clasp. The key, I slid from my pocket. A matching pendant, thin and sharp, already looped onto a fine chain.

Her eyes flicked up to me.

“It doesn’t come off without it,” I said. “Not by accident. Not without intention.”

She looked at it for a long moment, and I could almost see the shift—the way its meaning took hold. The ring had been romantic. This was something deeper, more unyielding. A promise and a claim in equal measure. Ownership, disguised as ornament; devotion, rendered into beauty; control, made precious.

When she finally extended her wrist, I took it in my hand and fastened the lock. The soft click seemed to settle into the space between us, quiet and absolute.

“There,” I murmured, brushing my thumb over the inside of her wrist. “Now you’ll carry it with you. Always.”

Her fingers moved to the chain holding the key. “And this?” I smiled. “I’ll wear it.”
She nodded again, slower this time. I knew the look in her eyes. Not doubt. Not fear. Just awareness. The shift of something heavy finding its place inside her.

She glanced at my hand—at the ring she’d placed there not an hour ago.

“I’ll switch it to my right hand when we return,” I said simply. “When the world is ready to see it—when they understand—it’ll go back.”

“But you’ll wear it?”

“Every day. I’ll tell anyone that asks that you gifted it to me when we were in Italy and they’ll be none the wiser.”

She leaned into me then, her head finding my shoulder, her breath warm against my collar. I kissed her temple.
“What now?” she whispered.

“Now?” I exhaled, letting the full weight of satisfaction settle into my bones. “Now you’re mine in every way that matters. The rest will catch up.”

“You’re mine too,” her fingers touching my ring, before lacking her fingers with mine. “Don’t forget that part, either.”

Her words landed like a brand, burning in all the ways I wanted.
“Not a chance,” I murmured, turning my hand to press my mouth to her knuckles, tasting the faint salt of her skin. “That part’s carved into me now.”

We stayed there on the velvet sofa, her head on my shoulder, the faint hiss of the sea drifting in through the open terrace doors. The air still carried the heat of the Italian evening, laced with salt and the sweetness of the bouquet that laid next to us. The whole villa felt like it had exhaled with us—quiet, sated, ours.

I traced my thumb along the edge of her wedding band, still new and gleaming against her skin. “You have any idea,” I said softly, “how hard it’s going to be to let go of you long enough to sleep?”

She smiled against my neck, and I tightened my arm around her.
“Oh my god, you’re really my husband!” she laughed like it suddenly dawned on her; like reality had finally been realized.

I kissed her hair, smiling like a fool. “Yes, Mrs. Barnette,” I said, savoring it like the first taste of wine, “And I will be for the rest of our lives.”


End of Spring break.



Chapter 73.


Spring break passed in a blur of lemon trees, sun-warmed stone, and the salt-stung breeze of a town so quiet it might’ve slipped out of time. We stayed in a villa nestled on top of the cliffs above the Amalfi coast, the kind of place with shuttered windows and linen sheets that smelled like sun. Mornings were slow—espresso on the terrace, my legs draped over his lap, the sea spread out below like a secret we didn’t have to share. Afternoons drifted between private walks and coastal drives, every detail orchestrated, every choice already made.
Evenings belonged to him—strong and possessive, his hands learning me again and again like the coastline we kept tracing by day. I didn’t have to think. Liam had thought of everything.

By the time we flew back, my skin was warm, my body loose, and the ache of the semester felt impossibly far away. I hadn’t opened my laptop once. I hadn’t needed to. Everything that mattered was already waiting for me—at his side, in his plans, written in the itinerary I never asked to see. Spring break hadn’t been a break. It had been a rehearsal. For something permanent.

It was so hard to try to get back to reality but the bouquet Liam had sent on the first day back to classes had helped. The scent of fresh flowers drifted through my dorm, subtle but lingering, mixing with the faint polish of acetone and spring air filtering through the open window.

The bouquet sat proudly on my desk—lilies, white roses, and soft pink peonies—arranged in an elegant glass vase.

No note. No message. Just there like it was obvious, like I should have expected them.

I smiled to myself, stretching my legs across my bed as I carefully swiped another coat of polish over my nails. It struck me how all semester I had felt light, not weightless, not free in the traditional sense. Content. I was no longer the shaken leaf of a girl that first stepped onto campus in the fall. I no longer quivered or flinched in the presence of anyone, least of all Liam. There was something grounding in the feeling of belonging.

I blew gently on my nails, admiring the soft pink sheen before setting the brush back into the bottle. My thoughts weren’t running in frantic, skittering circles. I was no longer trying to force every minute of every day to be productive, to be worthy of the scholarship, or the space I took up, or the air I breathed. I had time. Time to do nothing. Time to paint my nails, to breathe, to just exist.

And then the door swung open, and Chloe strolled in, sunglasses perched on her head, grinning. “Well, don’t you look cozy,” she teased, plopping down at the foot of my bed.

I let out a soft laugh, stretching my arms. “I am.”

Chloe reached for one of my hands, inspecting my nails with a nod of approval. “You’re glowing, by the way. You know that?”

I rolled my eyes, but my lips twitched up. “I’m not glowing.”

“You are,” Chloe insisted, giving me a knowing look. “Your skin is clearer, your hair is glossier, and you’re smiling more.” She smirked. “What’s your secret? Expensive serums? Drinking more water? Or…” She shot a pointed glance at the flowers on my desk. “A certain hockey captain?”

I shook my head, laughing softly.

Chloe sighed dramatically, falling back against the pillows. “God, it’s spring. The semester is almost over. How did that happen?”

I leaned against my headboard, picking absently at the seam of my duvet. “I have no idea.”

Chloe turned onto her side, propping herself up on her elbow. “And just look at us. We survived. We’re thriving. I mean, think about it—when we first got here, we were clueless little freshmen. And now? We’ve made it.”

I smiled. “It does feel different now, doesn’t it?”

“Completely.” Chloe grinned. “And next year is gonna be even better.” I nodded, letting her words sink in. She was right.
Everything felt different now. Better. Smoother. Like I had settled into something— something secure, unshakable.

Chloe stretched, kicking off her shoes. “Anyway, I cannot wait for summer. We’re spending most of it in the Hamptons this year. You have to come for at least a weekend.”

I hesitated.

She noticed. “You are coming, right?”

I smiled broadly, “What I’m no longer welcome for a week?”

Chloe laughed. “Oh my God! A week would be even better! ”

I shook my head quickly, smiling. “I already told Liam. Let’s work out dates with him!”

Chloe studied me for a second, then nodded. “Does that mean you’re summering with him?” I squeezed her hand lightly.

“Boston, he got the statehouse internship!” I squealed. She laughed, “who is happier - you or him?”
I grinned, sinking deeper into the cushions, one leg tucked beneath me as I absently turned the delicate gold bracelet around my wrist—one of the new ones Liam had surprised me with last week. “Honestly? Me. Definitely me.”

Chloe gasped dramatically. “Wow. Caroline Barnette letting a man win something for once?”

I smiled without correcting her, the name catching on something warm and secret just behind my ribs. “Please. I’m letting him think he won. There’s a difference.” I tossed a pillow at her without looking.

She caught it with both hands and beamed, that sharp, conspiratorial kind of joy only Chloe could pull off. “So tell me everything. Boston, right? Is he going full political heartthrob this summer?”

I nodded, still fidgeting with the bracelet, the tiny clasp cool against my fingertips. “He got the statehouse internship. Which, of course, he’s pretending is just ‘an opportunity to observe’ while already planning which office he’ll run for in a decade.”

She laughed. “Naturally. And you?”

I let the smile spread slowly, unhurried and certain. “I’m working with a nonprofit—his mom helped set it up. Focused on education reform and community housing initiatives. Nothing flashy, but it matters.”

Chloe’s eyes lit up. “Caroline. That’s amazing.”

“And,” I added, twirling the bracelet once more before letting my hand fall to my knee, “he may have, very subtly, replaced half my closet.”

Her mouth dropped open. “Wait—seriously?”

“Summer wardrobe,” I said lightly. “Apparently linen and silk were nonnegotiable.” Chloe groaned. “God, I want to hate that, but it’s so chic I can’t.”
I gave her a look. “You’d hate it if it wasn’t so well-tailored.” She threw the pillow back at me. “You know me too well.”

We both laughed, the kind of easy, full-bodied sound that belonged to lazy afternoons and the scent of sunscreen still clinging to skin.

The window was open, and the breeze brought in the soft rustle of tree branches and the distant echo of someone’s music drifting up from the lawn. I didn’t feel rushed, or behind, or like I was scrambling to keep up. Everything felt… aligned.

“You’re really doing it,” she said softly. “The whole thing.”

I ran my fingers along the smooth edge of the bracelet. “Yeah,” I said. “And this time… I actually want to.”

“Let’s order in and watch old movies. It’s been forever since we had a proper movie night. Tell Liam you’re occupied tonight!”

I laughed and texted him. He had been more forgiving these days. It made me wonder if he would have been this gentle had I just given in on day one. I didn’t let that thought linger. It didn’t matter now.

Chinese food arrived within 20 minutes and we queued up “Breakfast at Tiffany’s.”

“We have to finalize housing for next year soon,” Chloe said, digging a crab ragoon out of the white container.

“Are you telling me you don’t want to room with me,” I turned to her, my face dropping. “Oh my god! Of course not! I was worried you wanted a single for more sexy times!”
I laughed and threw a fortune cookie at her, “Um, I’m good thanks! I think if I have sex more often, he might actually break my vagina! Do you know you can get a bruised cervix!?”

She erupted into a fit of giggles “TMI, Caroline, TMI!”

“No seriously, my GYNO fucking commented on it at my last appointment. I was modified!” We laughed so hard, I had tears in my eyes. We caught our breath and I continued, more seriously:

“Well then the only thing to figure out is Alex,” I liked Alex, more than I did in the beginning but I don’t know if I wanted her snark 24-7 again.

“Oh she’s rooming with one of the chem chicks” Chloe waved her hand dismissably. “That’s... convenient”
“My thoughts exactly.”

I looked over at Chloe, with her soft blonde hair, her button nose and socialite cheekbones. I tried to imagine what my year would have been like if I were like her. Did she ever feel out of place? She came from money, with the right name and right connections. She didn’t start her

semester being raped. I chewed my cheek, I hope that last assumption was true — I made a silent prayer that it was.

“What’s your greatest accomplishment this year?” I asked.

Chloe hummed, leaning her head against the pillow as she thought. “Hmm… I mean, surviving first year at this school is an accomplishment in itself,” she teased, echoing her earlier words, but then her expression softened. “Honestly? Probably just… being happy.”

I tilted my head, intrigued. “Being happy?”

She nodded, twirling a strand of her blonde hair around her finger. “Yeah. I don’t know… I was nervous coming here, even with my family’s connections. I mean, you know how it is— half these people have been friends since birth, their families tied together through money, summer homes, or country clubs. And I was worried that even with my last name, I’d feel out of place.”

I swallowed, understanding more than she realized.

“But,” Chloe continued, shrugging, “I found my people. I found you. And Alex, even if she’s a bitch sometimes. And the girls on our floor, even if they’re all in a weird cult-like obsession with SoulCycle.” She laughed. “I just… I feel settled, I guess. I’m not trying so hard to fit into a mold I don’t belong in. I’m just here. And I’m happy.”

I smiled, warmth spreading through my chest. “That’s a really good one.” She grinned. “I know. Now you have to answer.”
I hesitated, running my fingers along the hem of my pajama shorts. “My greatest accomplishment?”

“Yup.”

I took a slow breath, the answer forming before I could stop it. “I beat my Adderall addiction.”

Chloe’s playful expression fell into something softer, something real. “Oh, Caroline.” She sat up, reaching for my hand. “That’s huge.”
I nodded. “Yeah.”

“I mean it,” she said firmly, squeezing my fingers. “I could tell you were struggling. I was really worried for a while. You really scared us that night. I thought I was going to lose you.”

I exhaled slowly, forcing a small smile. “It got bad. Worse than I thought it would. But… I got through it.”

I didn’t say how. I didn’t say who had pulled me through. Chloe didn’t ask, God bless her. She just smiled, squeezing my fingers again. “I’m proud of you.”

I nodded, my throat suddenly tight.

Chloe let out a deep sigh, flopping back against the pillows. “God, can you believe how much has changed since fall? We’re basically different people now.”

I let out a breath of laughter. “Yeah.”

Chloe turned her head toward me, smirking. “Like, let’s do a little then vs. now moment. Caroline Greer, Fall Semester: neurotic, tense, terrified of her own shadow. Caroline Greer, Spring Semester—thriving, glowing, allegedly recovering from a bruised cervix.”

I snorted, throwing another fortune cookie at her. “You’re the worst.” She giggled, catching it. “And yet, you love me.”
I shook my head, laughing, but the truth of her words sat warm in my chest. I did love her.

Chloe had been my constant through all of it—the chaos, the stress, the spirals. Through the things I could talk about, and the things I couldn’t. Through Liam.

She had no idea what he really was, what he had done, how deeply woven he was into the fabric of me now. And she never would. But still—she was here.

And I would hold onto that for as long as I could. I nudged her ankle with mine. “Okay, my turn.” She gave me a mock-suspicious look. “Be nice.”
“I will—mostly,” I said, grinning. “Let’s see. Fall semester Chloe: chronically late, chronically online, drank so many lavender oat milk lattes I started worrying about your blood pressure. Walked through campus like a runway model on speed, ghosted no fewer than four decent men in a single month, and insisted every minor inconvenience was a cosmic test.”

Chloe gave an exaggerated gasp. “Rude.”

“True,” I countered. “But now—spring Chloe? Still dramatic, still late, still deeply attached to caffeine, but…” I paused, smiling at her. “Softer. Not less spark, just… more grounded. You listen more. Laugh deeper. You don’t need all the noise anymore to feel like yourself.”

She blinked at me, caught off guard.

“And you actually let people stay,” I added, quiet but honest. “That’s new. Letting someone win an argument? Unheard of. Letting the man win something for once? Revolutionary.”

Chloe made a face, but she was clearly touched. “I hate you.” “You love me.”

“Only because you’re not wrong.”

I smiled, turning my wrist and absently tracing the edge of the gold bracelet. “So, yeah. That’s my official Chloe spring semester recap.”

She didn’t say anything for a beat. Just looked at me like I’d said something she hadn’t let herself believe until now. Then she exhaled and bumped my knee with hers again.

Chloe nudged me with her foot. “We survived. And I didn’t even text Ethan once, so I feel like I should get a medal.”

I gave her a look. “You also went an entire year without hooking up with a hockey player. That’s discipline.”

The pause that followed was just a fraction too long.

Chloe’s expression didn’t shift, but I caught the faintest flicker of something in her posture— a delay in the laugh, a subtle tightening of her jaw.

“…What?” I asked slowly.

She smoothed her hands down her thighs, too deliberately. “I mean… technically.” I stared. “Chloe.”
“It was a one-time thing,” she rushed out. “No repeats, no strings, and it was months ago. You were still living on coffee and ambition and pretending not to be in love.”

My mouth fell open. “You—you—hooked up with Ethan and didn’t tell me?” She looked mildly panicked. “It didn’t count!”
“Was there penetration?!” “Not the point.”
I gaped at her. “Liam’s going to die.”

She groaned. “Liam is not going to find out.”

“Oh, Chloe. You’re blushing. You’re literally blushing. He’s going to take one look at you and know, but let’s face is — he probably doesn’t already know.”

“I will end you.”

I grinned, victorious. “And here I thought I was the one with secrets. He doesn’t call you, ‘Kiddo,’ now does he?”

She threw a pillow at me. “Ew. No!”

I caught it with one hand and leaned back, smug. Sunlight spilled across her duvet, catching on the curve of my bracelet as I toyed with it—absently now, comfort in motion. She was still pink in the cheeks, but her laughter had turned soft again, real.

Chloe crossed her legs primly. “Next semester, we’ll be older, wiser, impossibly chic. No more breakdowns over boys who wear cargo shorts.”
I tilted my head. “You sound suspiciously like my mother.” “Ew, take that back,” she groaned.
I laughed, leaning my head back against the wall. It was peaceful, easy, normal. And for once, I let myself sink into it. “I love this movie”

“I do too”

I reached for my phone and without overthinking I texted Liam.

Caroline: I love you

Liam: I love you, too.

And in that moment, I was so happy I could burst.

I set my phone down, the last text from Liam still glowing faintly on the screen, and let my head fall back against the pillows, absently twisting the bracelet on my wrist. I thought about how strange this semester had been—strange in the way good things sometimes are, like your body keeps bracing for impact long after the danger’s passed and then one day, you relax.
Shoulders slump, you let out the breath you forgot you were holding, the tension in your chest finally releases.

Sure, there’d been the creep of a TA, but Liam had handled that in a way only Liam could— clean, quiet, absolute.

Across the room, Chloe was curled up with her phone, thumbs flying with the kind of energy that usually meant someone was about to fall for her without a chance in hell. I thought about our late-night movie nights or karaoke sing offs, the impromptu dorm facials, the way we walked into parties now with sharper eyeliner, better posture, and higher heels not because we were trying to impress anyone—but because we didn’t need to anymore.

The bracelet caught the light just like it had when he fastened it around my wrist in Amalfi— one of those sun-soaked days when we stayed in bed too long and everything tasted like citrus and sea salt. Even the weekends away had felt like stolen chapters from someone else’s story.

Except this one was mine.

The quiet after the storm. The steadiness I hadn’t dared to want last fall. A life where I didn’t have to look over my shoulder. Where I could just… look ahead.

It almost felt too good to be true. Almost.



Chapter 74.


7 weeks later. Boston.

The hydrangeas were heavier than I expected, digging into the crook of my arm where the paper wrapping had started to tear. I shifted the shopping bag higher on my hip, trying not to crush the stems as I walked.

The streets of Beacon Hill were warm and drowsy, late afternoon sun slanting across the brick sidewalks, catching the edges of iron railings and manicured planters. I ran through the list in my head, keeping a steady rhythm with my steps. Final call with the caterer. Confirm silent auction items. Double-check table numbers with Diane’s assistant. I still needed to replace the earrings I lost last week, pick up Liam’s tailoring, maybe sneak in an hour to finish the seating chart if I could—

“Good afternoon, Miss Greer.”

I blinked out of the spiral, smiling automatically at the doorman as I shifted the flowers to wave.

“Afternoon, Edward.”

The lobby was quiet, the kind of preserved stillness that always made the building feel more like a private museum than a place people actually lived. The elevator chimed low as I stepped in, smoothing the side of my dress where the bags were digging into my hipbone.

By the time I reached the our floor, I was already thinking about the emails I hadn’t answered yet, the call Liam had asked me to make about a dinner reservation. It was almost a relief, the mindless busyness of it. The charity event was a mountain, but it was a mountain I knew how to climb. One more perfect appearance, one more flawless night, and then—

I shifted the flowers higher, fumbling for my keys. Balancing the bag awkwardly against my thigh, I twisted the handle.

It gave way immediately. No resistance. The door swung open under the weight of my hand.

I stood frozen in the threshold, every thought burning out at once. My first instinct was simple, stupid: Maybe Liam forgot to lock it. Maybe housekeeping had come through and left it open.

I stepped inside carefully, the edge of the door brushing the back of my heel.

The apartment was silent. No cleaners. No noise from the kitchen. No clatter of keys or the familiar muted tones of Liam’s voice on a work call.

I took another step, arms still full, the hydrangeas crumpling slightly against my side.

That was when I heard him. A voice, casual, almost amused, floating from the living room: “You’re home early.”
I froze, the blood draining from my face so fast my fingers went numb around the flowers. The door eased shut behind me with a soft click, sealing me in.

I didn’t move. Couldn’t. For a second, I thought maybe I’d imagined it—the voice, the casual lilt of it—but then I heard the soft tap of keys, the faint scrape of something shifting on the coffee table.

Slowly, as if the air itself had thickened, I turned toward the living room.

Nathan Hale sat sprawled on the couch, one arm draped along the back cushions, a laptop open in front of him like he belonged there. The black cord of the hard drive snaked across the table, plugged in and humming faintly in the heavy silence, signaling that whatever he had planned, he had given himself time to setup.

He was dressed too neatly for it to be an accident—pressed shirt, clean-shaven, the careful polish of someone trying to appear respectable. If it weren’t for the situation, for the sour sweat-slick fear crawling up my spine, he might have passed for a guest.

His mouth tipped into a lazy half-smile when our eyes met, as if we were old friends caught at something amusing.

“Close the door, Caroline,” he said lightly, as if he hadn’t already trapped me.

The flowers slipped from my arms before I realized I’d let them go. They crumpled on the entryway rug, petals bending under the weight of the paper. My hands were empty now.
Useless.

Nathan leaned forward slightly, tapping the edge of the laptop with two fingers. “Don’t scream. You and I both know you’re smarter than that.”
The urge to bolt, to run back into the hallway and scream for Edward, was so loud in my head it drowned out everything else. But my body didn’t move. Not a step. Every inch of training Liam had drilled into me—composure, stillness, calculation—held me in place like a vise.

I drew a slow breath, anchoring myself against the rising nausea.

“What do you want?” I asked, and hated the way my voice caught at the edges. Nathan chuckled under his breath, a soft sound like flipping a coin.
“To talk,” he said. “That’s all.”

He gestured with an open hand toward the armchair across from him, an invitation so absurdly polite I could almost pretend he was harmless.

I didn’t move.

My pulse pounded behind my eyes, but my hands were steady. I shifted the strap of my bag, angling my body just slightly—enough to shield the motion as I slipped my phone from my dress pocket. No sound. No flash. My thumb moved fast.

Caroline: The Naples dress doesn’t fit. I’ll try another. Sent.
The screen went dark as I curled my fingers around the phone again, tucking it out of sight just as Nathan looked back up.

He patted the seat next to him, impatient this time. His smile thinned. “Let’s not make this harder than it needs to be.”

He nodded once, still calm, still polite—like a professor nudging a student into compliance. This time, however, the invitation had weight behind it.

I didn’t argue. I crossed to the armchair slowly, sank into it with the easy grace of someone who had nothing left to lose.

As he adjusted the laptop on the coffee table, something dark shifted beneath it—a flicker of matte black, tucked just far enough under the edge to be almost nothing.

“Let’s not waste time pretending we’re alone in this, Caroline” he said suddenly, extending a hand. “Phone.”
I sighed, taking the phone out of my pocket and placed it in his waiting hand.

Nathan took the device, turning it over in his hand like he was testing the weight of it. He thumbed through the open apps, quick, efficient. Checked my recent calls, my texts.
I watched the flicker of his eyebrows as he paused, just briefly, on the last messages sent. Satisfied, he put it in his jacket pocket.

His smile sharpened. He reached toward the laptop again, and with a flick of his wrist, tapped a key.

The screen shifted. And everything in me dropped away.

Nathan’s fingers moved lazily over the trackpad, and the video on screen flickered into full resolution.

This wasn’t the party footage—the cell phone clips I’d been half-bracing for. This was different. Cleaner. Steadier. Surveillance footage.

A hidden camera. Somewhere high in the corner of Liam’s dorm room, the wide-angle view catching the sharp, sterile corners of the mattress, the glint of the lamp by the window. I saw myself stumble in first—laughing softly, clinging to Liam’s jacket as he guided me backward

through the door. The sound was muted, but I didn’t need the audio to know I was slurring, swaying slightly on my heels. Roofies. Not just the vodka sodas. I swallowed, even though Liam had shown me the footage early on. He used it as a way to taunt me. Now I was grateful it didn’t hold its shock factor.

Liam caught me when I tripped over the edge of the rug, his hands steady, patient. He slid the door shut behind him without ever taking his eyes off me. On the screen, I smiled up at him
—dreamy, half-dazed—and he smiled back, slow and easy. He led me to the bed, easing me down like I was something fragile, his hand slipping beneath the hem of my dress. The camera caught everything. The way his mouth found the inside of my thigh. The way he kissed his way up my body, slow and worshipful, while my hands flopped weakly against the mattress.

It caught the way he pressed two fingers inside me, coaxing a sound from my throat, a soft moan that seemed to spur him on. The way he lowered his head between my legs, kissing, tasting, teasing until my hips gave a helpless jerk beneath his mouth. And then the way he climbed over me, whispering something into the hollow of my throat, one hand steadying my waist as he pushed into me. My head lolled back. My arms slipped off the sides of the bed. I wasn’t moving anymore.

Nathan let the footage play for a moment longer than necessary before glancing up at me, a grin twitching at the corners of his mouth. He was waiting: for the shatter, for the betrayal, for the horror.

I blinked once, slowly, then crossed my arms over my chest. “I think it’s really gross you’re watching a video of my boyfriend and me having sex.”

“Caroline, he was raping you.” I could hear the edge in his voice, the frustration that this wasn’t playing out his way.

“It’s called role play, Nathan. Maybe you’re not familiar with the concept.”

For a second, something wild flickered in his eyes—something that didn’t know how to reconcile what he was seeing. Then he barked out a sharp, humorless laugh.

“You think that matters?” he said, tapping the laptop lightly with his knuckles. “You think anyone’s going to listen when that hits the internet? A senator’s son, raping a barely conscious girl? Headlines, outrage, interviews on every network.”

I raised an eyebrow, unimpressed. “Give the press and Barnette’s a little more credit,” I said lightly.

Nathan shook his head, still smiling thinly. “You don’t get it. It doesn’t matter what the truth is. It matters what it looks like.”

He clicked through another folder casually, as if browsing a sale rack.

Photos now. Stills of me blowing Liam, of me topless with hickeys covering my chest, all the photos that were once an insurance policy now on display. A few I didn’t recognize: me half-

dressed, asleep, curled into Liam’s side. One photo caught my eye: the strap of my dress slipping low, my body limp in the backseat of a black car. I didn’t remember the night. I didn’t remember a camera.

Nathan whistled low under his breath.

“This would be great content for OnlyFans,” he said, dragging the image onto the desktop. “Hell, maybe I should start a page staring, ‘Holbrook’s Sweetheart.’ Could make up for the income I lost since your boyfriend had me blacklisted.”

“It would be taken down within seconds,” I said flatly.

Nathan shrugged, almost good-natured about it. “Doesn’t mean it wouldn’t spread first. You can’t scrub the internet once it gets a taste.”

He clicked back to the folders, lingering for effect. Then he tilted his head, studying me like a problem he hadn’t solved yet.

“Did you know,” he said lightly, almost conversational, “he had files on your friends too?”

He didn’t wait for me to answer. The next click opened a new window—and the names started appearing.

The folder expanded, clean and clinical.

First name: Ethan Cooper. Next: Chloe Everhart. Then Alex, her name half-cut off by the size of the file window.

Nathan flicked through them with a flick of his fingers, like he was scrolling through music playlists.

He paused on Ethan’s: A juvenile incident report blinked open—a DUI charge from two years ago, sealed and forgotten, buried by wealthy parents and powerful alumni connections. The header alone would be enough for a scandal if the right people wanted it to be.

Nathan didn’t even glance at me as he spoke, like he already knew the knife was sinking in.

“Ethan wasn’t always careful, was he?” he mused, tapping the corner of the file with exaggerated care. “Drunk. Rear-ended a professor’s car. Sealed up in a day. No charges.”

I kept my expression smooth, but my stomach twisted wildly. He shifted to the next file.
Chloe Everhart – Private Documentation.

He double-clicked, and the screen lit up with the photo I recognized instantly—the one I had taken at a house party last year. The real photo had just been Chloe laughing, head tipped back, champagne glass raised high, glitter catching on her lashes. But here, the image was wrong. A small bag of white powder had been sloppily edited into her hand. The shadows

were off. The lighting too sharp around the edges. It was a bad forgery, but to someone who wanted to believe it—to someone eager for blood—it would be enough.

Nathan laughed under his breath when he saw me recognize it.

“I know, right? Sloppy work. Probably rushed it between blackmailing you and forging your little academic file.”

I said nothing. Inside, nausea clawed at the back of my throat, but I locked it down. Forced myself to shift my weight casually, like I was just bored, mildly inconvenienced by the circus he was trying to build around me.

Nathan closed Chloe’s file with a snap of the trackpad and sat back.

“I don’t even need it to be airtight,” he said easily. “People see what they want to see. Especially when it’s scandalous. Especially when it’s juicy.” He drummed his fingers against the couch cushion, studying me with the kind of predatory patience that made my skin crawl.

“You could stop this,” he said softly. “Clear my name. Give me one statement. One recorded confession. Tell them Liam manipulated you. Tell them he forced you into all of it—sex, cheating, drugs. Play the victim.”

He smiled, almost kindly. “That’s all you have to do.” I met his eyes, calm and unblinking. “And if I don’t?”
Nathan’s smile sharpened into something colder. “You already know,” he said.

He leaned forward, lowering his voice to a conspiratorial whisper. “I burn it all. I bury him. I bury you. I bury your friends. I send these to every news outlet, every board, every family.
By the time I’m done, you’ll be the cautionary tale every Holbrook freshman learns about.” He let the words hang between us like smoke.
I clenched my teeth and lowered my gaze—not in submission, but to hide the way my hands were starting to tremble.

“You still think you’re so special, don’t you?” he said, watching me too closely.

I gave him nothing. No flinch, no tell. Just the same blank calm I’d learned to weaponize.

But under the surface, my pulse was ragged. I could feel the strain—like a held breath stretched too thin. And if he kept going, if he kept digging, I didn’t know how much longer I could keep it from tearing open.

Nathan clicked the trackpad once more, pulling up another folder with no label, no date. Just empty gray until the video loaded.

The screen sharpened into focus—and my heart nearly stopped.

It wasn’t me. The girl was about my size, maybe a little taller. Darker hair. Pale silk dress— the same style Liam picked for me. She perched delicately on the arm of a chair, hands smoothing the fabric across her thighs, glancing nervously at the door. Waiting, like she’d been taught or instructed. The setting was elegant, muted. A room with heavy drapes, dark floors, the edges of expensive furniture just out of frame. I didn’t recognize it. No landmarks or time stamp to help place the scene.

A shadow crossed the screen—and then Liam appeared.

He looked a little younger, more relaxed. His shirt sleeves pushed to his elbows, smile slow and deliberate.

He walked over to the girl, brushing a hand lightly against her shoulder as he passed—the exact way he did to me when he wanted to remind me to sit straighter. The girl reacted instantly, pulling her shoulders back, tilting her chin up, offering a bright, tentative smile like she was waiting for permission to move.

He touched her necklace—small, silver, delicate—and adjusted it at the clasp. He reached to smooth the strap of her dress. Tucked a strand of hair behind her ear.

Gestures that felt like a language like only he and I spoke.

The girl shifted her weight, standing when he gently cupped her elbow and guided her to her feet. She laughed when he said something too low to hear—nervous, breathless. She didn’t resist when he pressed a kiss to her forehead. She leaned into it. Obedient. Eager. Familiar.

Nathan let the footage roll another moment—long enough for Liam to whisper something in the girl’s ear, long enough for her to nod, blushing, and lower her gaze like it was second nature.

Then he paused it—frozen on the image of Liam’s hand sliding possessively down the girl’s back to rest at the small of her back. The same possessive touch I thought he reserved for me.

I stared at the screen, cold blooming under my skin.

Nathan leaned back, watching me, his smile small and merciless.

“Looks familiar, doesn’t it? I’m sure you thought it was special,” he said, almost gently. “Am I right?”

I didn’t answer; saying anything felt like a betrayal: a voice that would crack, a word that would reveal too much. Somewhere, deep down where all the rationalizations couldn’t reach, I’d already started to wonder.

How many times had he smoothed my hair like that? Tugged my necklace straight? Corrected the way I stood, the way I smiled, the way I waited for him?
How many times had I thought it was love when it was just… method.

Nathan closed the laptop halfway, soft and deliberate.

“You weren’t made,” he said, voice low and cutting. “You were replicated. She has her own file, too. You should look through it yourself.”

The breath left my lungs in one long, shaking exhale. I gripped the edge of the chair to keep from falling. Nathan’s smile sharpened.
“You’re not his only project, Caroline,” he said. “You were never his only.” He let the words settle, filling the room, thick as smoke.
I stared at the half-closed laptop, breathing shallowly through my nose, willing myself to stay upright.

This was in the past. I knew he had a past. He never lied about that. The girl was just… a phase? a mistake? Nothing serious.

I gripped the back of the chair harder, fingertips whitening.

But the thoughts slid in anyway, sharp and slick, too fast to stop. Did he drug her too? Did he break her the same way? Did she have her own folder buried somewhere in that hard drive? Her own collection of whispered yeses and desperate obedience?

He said I was the only one. He said there was no one before me who mattered. He said— The acid rose in my throat, burning. When was this? Where was this?

The background gave nothing away—just heavy curtains, a sliver of dark wood furniture, the curve of a fireplace that could belong in a hundred different rooms. There was no date. No timestamp. Just the same touches. The same movements. As if everything he ever gave me had been practiced first on someone else.

Nathan watched the panic flicker across my face and smiled wider, leaning back like he had all the time in the world.

“You see it now, don’t you?” he said softly. “You were built, not chosen.”

I drew in a sharp breath, the edges ragged, my heart pounding so violently I thought it might crack my ribs from the inside.

I had to move, had to get away before the walls closed in completely. I forced my voice steady, even as the air around me felt too thin.

“I need the restroom,” I said, keeping my tone bland, almost bored, as if none of this touched me.

Nathan gestured lazily toward the hallway without rising.

“Go ahead. I’ll be right here.”

He didn’t bother to watch me go. Thinking because he had my phone, the laptop, the hard drive that he thought he’d already won.

I locked the door behind me with shaking fingers, twisting the bolt until it scraped against the frame. The mirror above the sink caught me—sharp-eyed, flushed, every nerve blazing. Not broken. Not yet. But close.

I wish I could check if the message went through, ensure that he had seen it. He had to have read it, right?
Unless he was in a meeting. Unless he didn’t have service.
Unless he’d turned off notifications again because of security protocols or Senate calls But we had cameras.
There were a few in the apartment; three that could capture Nathan now: the entryway, the hallway, the living room. I knew the angles. I’d seen the footage before.

He had to know someone was here. He would’ve seen the door unlock. Seen Nathan sitting on the couch like he owned it. But fear crept up my spine. If Nathan could stroll into our apartment – past the doorman, the front desk – and then break into our safe, could Nathan have also turned off the cameras?

No. Liam would’ve noticed. He was obsessive about surveillance. He checked it constantly, monitored traffic patterns like he was mapping a war zone. There would alarms or notifications on our phones.

I braced my hands on the counter, heart thudding. He had to know. He had to be coming. I just had to hold out long enough for him to get here.
I took a deep breath as if to fill me with confidence or bravery or some fresh resolve, smoothed my skirt, adjusted my jewelry like armor, and opened the bathroom door with the same easy grace I used at Diane’s galas, at foundation luncheons, at every carefully curated event Liam had ever placed me in.

Hostess mode: A smile. A soft pivot of the shoulders. A glide into normalcy. I stepped back into the living room, voice light, almost apologetic.
“I need to get these flowers into water,” I said, crossing calmly toward the kitchen, the hydrangeas still crumpled on the floor where I had dropped them. “Can I get you a drink? I should have offered you that when you first came in. You caught me off guard.”

Nathan didn’t move, didn’t smile, only watched me carefully as if I were a ticking bomb. He reached down, tapped the laptop shut with a quiet, definitive sound, and placed something on top of it. The gun.

He didn’t brandish it. Didn’t point it at me. Just set it down—careful, deliberate—like it belonged there alongside the laptop and the blackmail files and everything else he intended to use against me.

I paused, one hand curling loosely around the stems of the broken flowers. I looked at the gun then back at Nathan before returning my gaze back to the gun again.

“And what do you intend on doing with that?” I asked lightly, as if I were commenting on a misplaced napkin.

Inside, my heart was hammering so hard I could feel it at the base of my throat.

Scream.

Call the doorman.

Move.

Do something.

Nathan rose from the couch slowly, brushing invisible lint from the front of his shirt. He crossed the space between us in three easy steps and extended his hand.
I gripped the flowers a little tighter, feeling the stems snap wetly under my palm.

Nathan’s eyes didn’t leave mine. He was close enough now that if I made a mistake, if I ran, if I screamed—he could end it before I reached the door.

I breathed in through my nose, out through my mouth. Steady. Controlled.
His mouth tightened, the first real crack in his polished composure.

Nathan moved the gun from the laptop to the coffee table, setting it squarely between us, a silent threat humming in the air.

