I pressed closer, desperate. “Just delete the incriminating ones,” I said, breathless, pleading. “Can’t we keep the others?”

Liam’s face twisted, the emotion slamming into him so hard he closed his eyes for a beat, breathing raggedly through his nose.

He shook his head, sharp and final. “Caroline,” he said, voice rough and stunned, “What the fuck?”

He leaned his forehead against mine, his hands tightening just slightly against my jaw. “They almost ruined you. They almost condemned me. They almost tore everything apart.”

I blinked up at him, the breath catching painfully in my chest.

“I can’t believe you even want to keep anything after that,” he whispered, his voice wrecked, furious in the way that meant he was furious with himself, not with me. “I won’t let them exist. I won’t let there be a single thing left that anyone could ever use against you or me again.”

My hands curled tighter into his shirt, the enormity of his certainty, his protectiveness, slamming into me harder than any fear Nathan had managed to plant.

“I’m sorry,” I whispered, the tears starting again, hotter this time. “I just—they’re our history. Our story. Maybe I’m feeling sentimental but I don’t want to destroy our past because of him.”

Liam silenced me with a kiss to my forehead, his hands smoothing down my arms, anchoring me in every way he knew how.

His voice dropped, quiet and lethal.
“If I could carve the memories into stone, I would. But I won’t keep a single byte of footage that someone like him could twist. Not when it nearly cost me you.” He laughed under his breath, “I never thought you’d be the one begging to keep your own blackmail material. You never cease to surprise me.”

I stayed there, pressed against him, for one more breathless beat, while another fear, older, colder, curled back up my spine. I tipped my face up again, my voice barely a whisper. “Liam, what about the other video?”

Liam’s hands froze. I swallowed hard, forcing myself to meet his eyes even though it felt like ripping skin from bone.

“The one Nathan showed me,” I said, the words cracking apart on my tongue. “The one with the other girl. She almost looked like me. Same gestures, same reactions.” I swallowed remembering the clip.

Liam’s hands froze against my skin. For a long, breathless moment, he didn’t move.

Then slowly, like he was afraid any sudden motion might break me further, he pulled back just enough to see my face, his thumbs sweeping slow, steady lines across my jaw.

“You saw that,” he said, voice low, wrecked.

I nodded, the ache burning behind my ribs so hard it felt like I might splinter apart. Liam closed his eyes for a beat, drawing in a slow, ragged breath.
“It was before you,” he said, the words rough and stumbling in a way Liam Barnette never sounded. “Long before, like senior year of Doverfield”

I wanted to look away. I wanted to disappear into the warmth of him and pretend I hadn’t seen what I saw. Instead, I held his gaze — even when it gutted me.

“It wasn’t what you think,” Liam said, opening his eyes, the full, brutal weight of them crashing into mine. “It was nothing. She was nothing.”

I swallowed hard, the memory of that video flashing behind my eyelids. “You—” I tried to say, but my voice cracked. “You… shaped her.”

“No,” Liam said sharply, immediately, his hands tightening on my face. “I played a part. I went through the motions. But there was no shaping. No molding. No making her into anything that mattered.” He exhaled shakily, his forehead pressing against mine, grounding us both.

“I didn’t even know what I wanted back then,” he whispered, broken. “I didn’t even know what I was looking for. It was a trying out the dynamic but not in a relationship, not in anything serious.”

His breath shuddered across my skin. “Then I saw you,” he said, softer now, devastating. “Remember what I thought? ‘Finally’.”

My heart stuttered.

“Finally someone worth keeping. Someone worth everything.” The tears blurred my vision, hot and silent.
“You were the only one. You are,” Liam said fiercely, his hands cradling me like I was breakable and sacred. “Past, present, future. No one ever compared to you.”

I made a small, broken sound, sagging into him, and he caught me instantly, pressing frantic, desperate kisses into my hair, my temple, my jawline.

“Please,” Liam whispered into my skin. “Please don’t let this come between us.”

His voice cracked completely, the raw, unvarnished need ripping through every word.

“I’ll burn the past to ash,” he said, wrecked. “I’ll tear down anything that hurts you. Just don’t—” He swallowed, hard. “Don’t pull away from me.”

I clutched his sweater tighter, my fingers twisting into the fabric, the force of him — the force of us — leaving no room for anything else. I could have pulled away. I could have let bitterness harden in my chest, but I didn’t want to. I didn’t want this — us — to break under the weight of a past that had never really been ours.

He wasn’t asking to be forgiven; he was asking not to lose me. And now, especially in the wreckage, I knew that I didn’t want to lose him either.

I stayed folded against him, the weight of everything we had survived pressing down on my chest until it hurt to breathe. The wreckage was still there—the cracks, the fears, the broken places Nathan had clawed open -- but so was Liam. Still here. Still holding me like I was the only thing in the world that mattered.

I pressed my forehead into his collarbone, breathing him in, steadying myself against the rough, uneven thud of his heart.

“Promise me,” I whispered, my voice so soft I wasn’t sure it was even real. Liam stilled, every part of him tightening around me like armor.
“Anything,” he said immediately, the word raw and desperate against my hair. I closed my eyes, clinging to the warmth of him.
“Promise me you won’t ever… treat me like I’m replaceable,” I said, the confession tearing itself from my throat, broken and bleeding. “Promise me I’m not just—”My voice cracked, and I shook my head helplessly.

Liam caught my face in his hands, tilting it up until I couldn’t hide from him, not from the way his eyes burned into mine like there was nothing else, nothing else in the whole fucking world but this.

“You’re not,” Liam said, fierce and steady. “You never were.”

His thumbs brushed slow, reverent lines over my cheeks, tracing the places where tears still clung.

“You’re it for me, Caroline,” he whispered. “You’re all there ever was. All there ever will be.”

I let out a broken sound, sagging into him, and he caught me instantly, wrapping me tighter in his arms, tucking me against his chest like he could fold the whole world down to just us.

He kissed the top of my head, slow and lingering, then pressed his mouth to my temple, my cheekbone, the corner of my mouth—each kiss soft, grounding, sacred.

“I’ll spend every day proving it to you,” Liam said against my skin. “If you let me.”

I tilted my head up, searching his face, and found nothing there but fierce, brutal devotion. No hesitation. No shame. Only love, twisted and broken and real.
I reached for him, my fingers curling into the hair at the back of his neck, pulling him down into me.

Liam kissed me like a man drowning—slow, aching, pouring every broken piece of himself into my mouth, his hands trembling where they framed my face, his body anchoring mine with a desperation that was terrifying and beautiful all at once.

And I kissed him back. Not perfectly. Not cleanly. Only with everything I had left.

If there was one thing that I was sure of after all of this: we had survived and we would keep surviving. Together.


He didn’t say anything at first—just held my gaze for one quiet, blistering moment before slipping one arm beneath my knees and the other around my back, lifting me into his arms like he was afraid the floor might shatter under me if I stayed there too long.

I let my head fall against his chest. I didn’t care that I was still trembling. I didn’t care that the world outside us was still scorched earth. I just let him carry me, let the steady rhythm of his heart and the strength of his arms be the only truths I trusted.

The bedroom door opened softly behind us. I didn’t open my eyes until I felt the sheets beneath me—cool, clean, untouched by the nightmare in the other room. Liam eased me down like something sacred, his hands never once leaving my body.

He knelt beside me, one hand stroking my hair back, the other finding mine and lacing our fingers together.

“You’re safe,” he murmured, the words quiet but absolute. “You’re mine, you’re safe, and no one’s ever going to hurt you again. I swear it.”

I swallowed hard, tears threatening again, but he kissed them away before they could fall— softly, reverently, brushing his lips across my cheek, my jaw, the hollow beneath my ear.

“Look at me,” he whispered.

What I saw when I obeyed nearly undid me.

There was no distance in him. No calculation. Only Liam—raw, exposed, every inch of him burning with a love so fierce it could have brought the world to its knees.

“I need to feel you,” I said, barely above a breath. “Please.”

His answer was a kiss. Not urgent. Not rough. Just his—deep and anchoring and endless. His mouth slid over mine like he was trying to relearn the shape of it, like he needed to memorize every inch in case it was the only thing keeping him tethered to earth.

He undressed me with a slowness that bordered on reverence, his fingers brushing each new patch of bare skin like he was apologizing to it—like he needed me to know that nothing and no one would ever claim it but him.

“Every inch of you,” he whispered against my stomach, “is mine to protect.”

I tugged at his sweater, desperate to touch him, to feel his skin against mine. When he finally laid over me, chest to chest, skin to skin, I felt the tremble still thrumming through his body like an aftershock.

“You’re shaking,” I whispered.

“So are you,” he murmured, his lips brushing the corner of my mouth. “But I’ve got you.”

He slipped inside me with the same care he’d given every kiss, every word, every vow that night—slow, deep, grounding. His eyes never left mine. He didn’t move right away, just held himself there, forehead pressed to mine, his breath shaking with restraint.

“I love you,” he whispered, so quietly I might have missed it if I hadn’t been listening with every broken piece of me. “I love you, Caroline. You’re my whole fucking world.”

“I know,” I breathed, wrapping my arms around him, anchoring us both. “I love you too. I found myself in you.”

His hips moved—just once—and it was enough to break something open in both of us. Not lust. Not release. Just relief. The kind that comes only after surviving something unbearable.

We moved together slowly, like a prayer spoken in a language only we understood. There was no need to rush. No need to prove anything. He kissed my tears away as they came. I held him tighter every time he whispered another promise against my skin.

You’re safe.
You’re mine.
No one will ever touch you again.

When I came, it was soft and shaking, a crest of emotion that stole my breath and left me gasping his name like it was the only word I still remembered. Liam followed, voice catching on a groan that sounded more like surrender than release.

He stayed inside me, breathing hard against my neck, his arms wrapped so tightly around me I thought we might never come undone.

“You’re everything, Caroline,” he murmured. “And if the world tries to take you from me again—I’ll burn it to the ground.”

And this time, I didn’t cry.
I just closed my eyes and let him hold me through the quiet.

Somewhere between the broken kisses and the slow, desperate way Liam pulled me fully into his arms, the adrenaline burned itself out.

I must have drifted, because the next thing I knew, I was blinking blearily up at the ceiling, the room dim and hushed around us, the heavy weight of Liam’s arms still wrapped around my body like a second skin.

The blanket had been dragged up over both of us. His heartbeat thudded steadily against my back, a slow, grounding rhythm that pulled me back from the edge of sleep every time I started to drift too far.

He was murmuring into my hair, the words low and rough and steady, like he hadn’t stopped speaking even as I slipped in and out of consciousness.

“The apartment’s doing a full investigation,” Liam said, his mouth brushing the crown of my head. “They’re changing the locks first thing tomorrow.”

His hand swept a slow, soothing line down my arm.

“If you don’t want to stay here tonight, we can move to a hotel. As long as you need. As long as you want.”

I let out a small, broken noise, something caught between a sigh and a whimper, pressing closer to him.

He tucked me tighter against his chest, one hand slipping under the hem of my borrowed sweater to spread across the bare skin of my stomach, warm and possessive.

“You don’t have to make any decisions now,” he murmured. “You’re safe. That’s all that matters.”

His phone buzzed softly against the nightstand, but Liam didn’t move to check it immediately.It buzzed again, then again. A soft, resigned breath rumbled through his chest before he shifted slightly, reaching for it without letting me go.

“It’s my mom,” he said, glancing at the screen.

I blinked up at him sleepily, my body too heavy to move, but Liam stroked my hair back from my face, his touch infinitely careful.

“I’ll call her back,” he said softly. “She can handle things for you. I’m sure my dad has filled her in on what happened.”

Another buzz. Another message. Liam glanced down, reading the text aloud without expectation.

“She says everything for the gala is under control. That you’re not to worry. That they can manage if you want to take the time off and that she is so sorry you had to go through the ordeal. She sends her love to us.”

His hand resumed its slow, steady strokes across my back, calming, anchoring. “The gala’s in two days,” he said against my temple. “Most of the work’s already done. Take tomorrow off.”

I closed my eyes, breathing him in, the safe, steady rhythm of him, the way he never loosened his hold even as the world started creeping back in around us. For now, it could wait. All of it could wait. I wasn’t alone. I was home. And home was Liam. And for the first time since I opened the door the apartment, I was no longer afraid.

Chapter 77: My Beginning, My End.



The gala glittered like something out of a dream.

In the Isabella Stewart Gardner Museum, soft light filtered down from the glass ceiling above the courtyard, gilding the stone arcades in warm gold and casting delicate shadows over the mosaic floors. The air smelled faintly of ancient stone and blooming jasmine, the stillness broken only by the quiet trickle of water from the marble fountain at the center. Ferns spilled over terracotta urns, orchids trailing soft petals in the corners, and somewhere overhead, candlelight shimmered in the leaded windows of the upper galleries. It didn’t feel like a museum—it felt like a secret held inside time. Music floated in the air—soft, elegant, the kind of melody that made you stand taller, smile sharper, pretend better.

It should have been overwhelming. It should have felt like drowning.

And maybe it would have—if not for Liam’s hand, warm and steady against the small of my back, anchoring me as we crossed the threshold.

His touch never wavered -- not when the crowd surged forward in a glittering wave of silk and velvet, not when heads turned, eyes following us as we slipped into the current of donors and dignitaries and politicians in thousand-dollar suits, not when the world pressed closer, smiling too brightly, laughing too loudly. No, Liam stayed at my side, his body always close enough to shield, without making it look like protection.

I could feel the weight of the stares as we moved deeper into the room—feel them tracking the sweep of my floral ball gown, the soft spill of silk moving around my legs like water, the

delicate shimmer of pink sapphire earrings catching the light where they dangled against my bare throat: Diane’s jewelry, borrowed for the night. A silent, gleaming reminder of her approval.

My hair hung loose around my shoulders, not pinned back or twisted into something severe, but soft, flowing, intentional. No longer a girl trying to survive anymore. Now, I was a woman standing exactly where she belonged.

To anyone watching, we looked like a perfect pair—Polished. Powerful. Untouchable. Liam looked devastating—black tuxedo cut sharp across the broad planes of his shoulders, crisp white shirt undone at the throat just enough to look deliberately, carelessly dangerous.

And he stayed close, always close. His hand a constant pressure against my back, reminding me: You’re not alone.

“You’re doing perfectly, sweetheart,” Liam murmured against my temple, his breath warm, his voice low enough that no one else could hear.

I turned my head slightly, enough that his jaw brushed my hair, the smallest contact sending a jolt of steadiness through me.

“I’ve got you,” he said, so soft it was almost a prayer. And I believed him, because without him and his steady hand, his body shadowing mine, his voice threading through my bones, I wasn’t sure I would survive the weight of all the eyes watching.

The music swelled as we crossed the mosaic floor, a low, lilting song stitched through with strings, the kind of music meant for spinning, for smiling, for pretending. Liam’s hand stayed at my back, guiding me forward without force, his touch a constant hum against my spine.

I caught flashes of familiar faces in the crowd—donors, trustees, polished strangers with old names and older money—but Liam’s presence at my side carved a path through them, seamless and unhurried.

Then I saw her—Diane, sweeping toward us in a soft cloud of champagne silk, her hair pinned perfectly, her earrings catching the light. Her smile was warm, radiant, the perfect combination of hostess and mother.

“Caroline,” she breathed, reaching for my hands the moment we were within arm’s reach. “You look absolutely breathtaking.”

I smiled—bright, effortless—the way I had been taught, the way she expected. “Thank you,” I said, squeezing her hands lightly.

Diane’s gaze roamed over me, pleased, proud. “You’ve outdone yourself,” she said warmly. “The gala is exquisite. Everyone is raving about the arrangements—the venue, the program, the auction list. You’ve elevated everything.”

I flushed slightly, the praise hitting harder than I expected. This didn’t feel like just an event, it felt like a test. How to act under pressure, how to rise to meet all expectations, despite

circumstance. It was about walking into this courtyard with my head high, my hands steady, my mask perfect, when less than forty-eight hours ago, I hadn’t even known if I would make it to morning.

“You’ve made us all so proud,” Diane continued, her voice lower now, meant only for me. “And your strength—”She paused, squeezing my hands again, harder.“—your strength is noticed, darling. More than you know.”

The words hit something deep inside me, something raw and half-healed, but I smiled through it, the practiced, polished smile she would expect.

Liam’s thumb brushed lightly against the curve of my spine, a silent reinforcement.

“I’m just glad everything came together,” I said smoothly, my voice steady even as my heart gave a small, traitorous stutter.

Diane leaned in and kissed my cheek lightly, the perfect, glittering matriarchal blessing, then straightened, her face radiant for the watching crowd.

“I’ll let you mingle,” she said with a wink. “But don’t go far—everyone wants a piece of you tonight.” She drifted away in a swirl of silk and whispered congratulations, her absence leaving a small pocket of breath between us.

I exhaled slowly, feeling the tension I hadn’t realized I was carrying bleed out of my shoulders.

Liam leaned closer, his mouth brushing the shell of my ear. “She loves you, you know,” he murmured, low and private. “Had she not had hostess duties, she would have embraced you and ensured you’re okay.” He looked down at me with a smirk, “She thinks you civilize me.”

I let my body lean into him for just a second longer than was appropriate, soaking in the anchor of him, the steadiness, before straightening again and facing the crowd. I knew what he was doing, trying to distract me from overthinking or from my thoughts going to a dark place. I loved that he knew had to stop those thoughts before they started.

The night wasn’t over yet, but with Liam beside me—with his hand steady at my back, his presence threading through every heartbeat—I knew I could survive it.

It wasn’t obvious (of course it wasn’t) when Senator Barnette crossed the room, cutting a deliberate path through the sea of glittering gowns and black-tie elegance, I felt a slight shift in the air. The way the crowd seemed to bend unconsciously around him.

He stopped in front of us, his expression easy, genial.

“Liam,” he said warmly, clapping a hand onto his son’s shoulder.

Liam straightened slightly, the only sign he gave that this wasn’t just a casual greeting. “Senator Barnette,” I said, my voice smooth, my smile polished.

The Senator turned his attention fully to me, and his smile deepened, genuine. “Caroline,” he said, almost fond. “Would you mind if I stole you for a moment?”

I caught Liam’s glance—steady, calm—and gave the smallest nod.

The Senator guided me upstairs toward one of the archways that looked down onto the courtyard and crowds, away from the glittering press of bodies, but allowing us to observe the party below.

For a long moment, he didn’t speak. He stood there beside me, his hands loosely folded behind his back, surveying the crowd, the floral arrangements, the statues and mosaics below as if considering the weight of it.

“You handled yourself beautifully tonight,” he said finally, his voice low but warm. “As I knew you would.”

I stayed quiet, letting the compliment settle between us.

He turned slightly, his gaze sharper now—not cold, but cutting in a way that made it clear he wasn’t speaking to a child. “You had choices, Caroline,” he said. “Back when things… turned difficult.”

His meaning didn’t need to be spelled out.

“You could have saved yourself. Walked away, thrown Liam to the wolves. No one would have blamed you.”

I swallowed, the memory flashing behind my eyelids, but I stayed silent.

“And yet,” Senator Barnette continued, his voice gentling almost imperceptibly, “you didn’t.”

He studied me for a moment longer, as if weighing something far heavier than the words he chose.

“You protected my son,” he said. “You protected this family.” The approval in his voice wasn’t just approval. It was pride. “You stood your ground with the kind of courage that humbles men twice your age and twice your power.”

And then, his voice dropping into something softer, darker, utterly certain:

“I know Liam didn’t court you, Caroline.” There was no judgment in the words. Only certainty. “I’m not blind to my son’s unconventional practices. Yet, you made yourself indispensable. I don’t know of another young lady who could do the same if pressed.”

I should’ve been numb to it by now. Forty-eight hours was long enough to let the knowledge settle, to file it away alongside everything else I had chosen not to flinch from. Still, hearing it aloud so plainly and calmly, felt like a piano chord struck in a quiet room. I felt a familiar tightness in my chest, the same kind that I felt nearly every day in the fall. Still was I truly surprised? Further, did I care now that I had grown into who I was – stronger, happier?

He smiled then—a small, rare thing that felt heavier than any formal accolade. “Liam chose well.”
The breath caught in my throat, but I forced myself to breathe, to stand straighter, to meet the full weight of what he was offering.

“You proved you belong here,” he said quietly. “Not as an outsider. Not as a guest.” His gaze softened slightly—still sharp, but warmer now. “You’re one of us, dear.”
I blinked hard against the sudden sting behind my eyes, grounding myself in the cool stone beneath my fingers.

“You carry the name, even if you don’t wear it yet,” he said. “And you carry it well.” A beat of silence as his words washed over me, heavy and irrevocable.
Then, with the casual finality of a man sealing a fate already decided, he added: “We’re lucky to have you.”

I swallowed thickly, managing a nod, my throat too tight to trust words.

The Senator offered a faint, knowing smile—something private, almost fatherly. He glanced back toward the courtyard where Liam waited, patient and watchful, his body wound tight with something almost feral beneath the polish.

Testing the waters, I decided to joke, my voice rough with nerves. “So do I get to call you ‘Dad’ now?”

His eyes sparked with humor, a low chuckle rumbling from his chest. “Wait for that ring that I know my son is itching to buy,” he said, voice low and teasing. Then, in a fake whisper that still somehow carried the full weight of a benediction: “But you can get away with calling my wife ‘Mom.’”

And with that, he turned and slipped back inside, leaving me standing alone beneath the stars
—changed, claimed, and seen.

I stayed on the mezzanine a few seconds longer after Senator Barnette disappeared into the crowd, the warmth from crowd wrapping around my bare shoulders, the weight of his words still humming under my skin.

It was only when I turned back down the cloisters that I saw Liam already cutting through the crowd, his focus locked solely on me. He didn’t rush, didn’t make a scene, but there was something in the way he moved—purposeful, deliberate—like there was no force on earth that could have kept him from reaching me.

By the time I stepped back into the warmth and glow of the courtyard, Liam was there, slipping a glass of champagne into my hand with a smoothness that made it look like he had been waiting for the perfect moment all along.

“You’re perfect,” he said under his breath, his hand settling low at my waist, fingers pressing lightly through the silk of my gown. “So fucking perfect.”

I took the glass from him with a small, grateful smile, letting the first sip wash over my tongue, the cold bite of it grounding me back in my body.

Liam’s gaze never wavered, scanning my face like he was memorizing every flicker, every tremor.

“What did he want?” he asked quietly, too low for anyone else to hear.

I shook my head, setting the flute down on a passing tray “Nothing bad,” I said, my voice steady enough to convince even myself. “Just… approval.”

Liam’s hand flexed at my waist, pulling me a fraction closer.

“He should be grateful,” he muttered, so low and rough I barely caught it. “He doesn’t deserve you.”

I leaned into him for a moment, letting the solid heat of his body shield me from the gleaming, glittering world spinning around us.

From the outside, we must have looked effortless. Perfect. Untouchable. But under the surface, it was just us. Still raw. Still wrecked. Still surviving. Together.

Liam pressed a kiss to my temple—quick, searing—then straightened just enough for a shadow of his usual smirk to play at his mouth.

“You ready to go back into the lion’s den, sweetheart?” he teased lightly. I exhaled a soft laugh, the tension bleeding out of my shoulders.
“As ready as I’ll ever be.”

And with Liam’s hand steady at my back, we turned back toward the crowd—just as Michael spotted us across the room, lifting his glass in lazy salute.

Michael reached us before we made it three steps back into the crowd, a glass of something dark and probably overpriced dangling carelessly between two fingers. He looked immaculate, of course—black tie crisp, grin sharp, eyes gleaming with the kind of mischief only a Barnette could wear like a second skin.

“Little sister,” Michael drawled as he slid up beside us, lifting his glass in lazy salute. “Or should I say, future sister? Either way—congratulations. You’ve officially burned all your bridges to your freedom. No escaping now.”

I laughed—really laughed—and the sound cracked something tight in my chest that I hadn’t even realized was still there. “Guess I should have read the fine print,” I said lightly, lifting the flute Liam had handed me.

Michael winked. “No refunds. No returns. And definitely no swapping out for a less complicated model.”

Liam’s hand tightened fractionally at my waist, the silent, unmistakable mark of ownership he barely bothered to hide anymore.

Michael caught the movement and chuckled under his breath, clearly enjoying himself. “Relax, Romeo,” he said, tossing a wink at me. “If I was going to cause a scandal tonight, it’d be a lot more interesting than stealing your girl.”

Liam said nothing, but the slow, warning look he gave his brother made Michael laugh under his breath, unapologetic.

“In fact,” Michael added, leaning in slightly like sharing a secret, “I’m technically hiding right now.”

I arched an eyebrow, amused despite myself. “Oh?”

Michael tipped his glass toward the far end of the ballroom where a cluster of women in jewel-toned gowns hovered by the champagne fountain.

“Recognized a senator’s wife who might still be under the impression that I plan to whisk her off to Paris,” he said lightly. “Figured I needed a few minutes’ reprieve before someone throws a drink at me.”

I smothered a laugh behind the rim of my glass.

“This is my first gala, Michael,” I said in mock sternness. “Please try not to ruin it with your galivanting.”

Michael grinned, utterly unrepentant. “No promises, love. Some traditions are sacred.”

Liam muttered something under his breath that sounded suspiciously like “God help us all,” but when I glanced up at him, he was fighting a smile.

Michael chuckled, taking another sip of his drink like a man deeply pleased with himself.

“In all seriousness,” he said, straightening slightly, “you pulled this off beautifully, Caroline. Mom is practically ready to build you a shrine. And Dad—” He cut a sly glance toward the Senator still holding court across the room. “Well. Let’s just say you’re no longer a guest in this family. You might even get a key to the pool house after tonight.”

I flushed under the praise, but it was easier to take from Michael—wrapped in sarcasm and affection rather than expectation. “That seems to be the line everyone is telling me tonight,” I said lightly.

Michael shrugged, the corner of his mouth lifting.

“Naturally,” he agreed easily. “You even make Liam look like he has a heart.”

Liam made a small sound of amusement. Michael tipped his glass again, a final lazy salute, before melting back into the crowd—likely toward more trouble, or more champagne, or both.

I watched him go, a real smile curving my mouth—lighter, freer than I had felt all night.

Liam leaned down, his mouth brushing the shell of my ear. “You’re the best thing that’s ever happened to this family,” he murmured, voice rough against my skin.

The words sank deep, lighting something warm and terrifying inside me. I wondered if there was any truth to behind them.

The music shifted—something slower, something spun from violins and memory, rich and golden in the air—and Liam’s hand tightened lightly at my waist.

“Dance with me,” he murmured, low enough that it wasn’t a question.

Around us, the cloister to the side of the courtyard shimmered—candlelight and laughter blurring into a soft, gilded haze. All the guests, the clinking of glasses, the polite, glittering noise fell away the moment Liam and I began to waltz.

He pulled me close, one hand steady at the small of my back, the other catching mine in a loose, protective grip, like I was something precious he couldn’t bear to set down, like he hadn’t spent months breaking and remaking me into something that could survive this world.

The first notes of the song brushed the room, and we moved—slow, unhurried, like the universe had finally found a rhythm meant only for us.

“You,” Liam said quietly, his mouth brushing the shell of my ear, “are the most beautiful thing I’ve ever seen.”

I smiled up at him, breath catching, the words hitting deeper than they had any right to. “You’re biased,” I teased, even though my throat tightened with emotion.

“Not nearly enough,” he said, his voice rough around the edges.

We turned together, slow and seamless, his body anchoring mine with a devotion that felt bone-deep.

For a while, we didn’t speak—just moved—two broken things stitched together under silk and candlelight.

But eventually, Liam leaned in again, his forehead brushing mine. “I used to wonder,” he said softly, “how much of you I could keep. How much I could shape without losing you completely.”

I blinked up at him, heart aching in my chest. “You didn’t lose me,” I whispered. “You found me.”

A muscle ticked in his jaw—emotion he couldn’t quite swallow. “You survived me,” he said instead, and for once there was no edge to it—no self-loathing, no threat. Just quiet awe.

“I was never going to survive without you,” I said.

Liam’s hand tightened on my back, pulling me closer, until I could feel the steady, relentless thud of his heart against my ribs.

“I keep thinking,” he said, voice so low I had to lean closer to catch it, “what comes next.” I smiled into his chest, breathing him in.
“And what do you see?”

He laughed under his breath, the sound rough and almost boyish.

“Years,” he said. “Years of you making me chase you around kitchens and boardrooms. Years of you wearing my name like it was always yours. Years of waking up to you every morning and thinking, ‘God, how did I win this?’ Years of watching our kids grow up and debating which bad trait they get from whom.”

My throat closed, too full, too overwhelmed. “You make it sound easy,” I managed, half- laughing, half-crying.

He tipped my chin up gently, his gaze burning into mine. “It won’t be easy,” Liam said. “We’ll fight. We’ll bleed. We’ll break things and fix them badly and try again.”

He leaned in, brushing his mouth against my temple. “But it’ll be ours.”

The music swelled, rich and aching. And standing there, wrapped in Liam’s arms, I realized
—maybe we don’t always get to choose how love finds us. Maybe the choices are made long before we know how to reach for them. Maybe fate isn’t a bolt of lightning, but a slow, relentless storm, carving out paths we were always meant to walk.

All I knew was this:

I was his.
And he was mine.
No past could change it.
No future could scare me from it.

“I want everything with you,” I whispered, fierce and certain.

“You already have it,” Liam said, voice breaking on the edges of a smile. “You always did.”

He spun me gently under his arm, pulling me back into his chest with a fluid grace that felt like breathing. I let myself fall against him, smiling into the strength of him, the certainty.

The crowd and art blurred around us—gold and silk and intricate stonework swirling away into nothing.

Only him.
Only us.

When I tilted my head back to look at him, Liam was already staring down at me—like I was a miracle he didn’t know how to deserve but would burn down the world to keep.

He kissed my forehead, soft and lingering.

“My Caroline,” he murmured against my skin. “My beginning. My end.”

The music faded, the final notes curling like smoke into the night. Liam didn’t let go and neither did I. We stayed there, breathing each other in, as the rest of the world moved on without us.

Chapter 78: Epilogue 1: At Last.


The bridal suite smelled faintly of white roses and Jo Malone perfume, the air thick with hairspray, silk, and the low hum of women mid-transformation.

Chloe stood barefoot on the chaise lounge, veil in hand, arranging it with the kind of exaggerated focus that made me laugh.

“If Liam doesn’t cry,” she said, squinting at the angle like the fate of the ceremony depended on it, “it’ll confirm my theory that he’s some kind of very polished android.”

I smiled into the mirror. “Don’t worry,” I said. “I’ll kick him in the shins hard enough to force a tear.”

Ava let out a soft laugh behind me. “God, you really do look beautiful, Caroline.”

Rachel, my dear friend from post-Holbrook fellowship work, chimed in from the vanity, chin in hand, her expression touched with something wistful. “How are you feeling? Nervous?
Overwhelmed? Planning a last-minute escape?”

“None of the above,” I said honestly, smoothing my hands down the silk at my waist. “I feel unequivocally happy.”

Chloe hopped off the chaise and walked over, crouching to fix the hem of the gown. “I still remember how you used to short-circuit when he walked into the room freshman year,” she said, grinning. “You’d pretend to be fine and then forget how to speak the second he looked at you.”

“She’s not wrong,” Ava added, laughing. “He had half the table under some kind of spell that first Thanksgiving.”

I tilted my head. “Except my mother.”

Right on cue, the door creaked open and in walked Diane and my mother, mid-conversation
—voices polite, but unmistakably in disagreement.

“I’m telling you, Diane, the ivory would’ve picked up the warm tones in the vineyard better than the white.”

“Well, white is classic,” Diane replied evenly. “It photographs beautifully.” I caught Chloe’s eye in the mirror and rolled mine.
But then they both stopped. Quiet, still. Their eyes met mine—first my mom’s, then Diane’s
—and something settled in the room. Mom blinked hard, her mouth parting slightly. “Oh, honey,” she said, her voice catching. “You’re—You look beautiful.”
Diane smiled with pride, “You look radiant.”

My throat tightened. “Mom, will you help with the buttons? I was hoping you’d be the one to finish buttoning me up.” I asked, turning slightly toward her.

She moved without answering, her fingers trembling just slightly as she began fastening the row of tiny buttons down my spine. She didn’t speak again until she finished, and even then it was only a soft, steadying breath behind me.

Diane stepped forward with a velvet box in hand. She opened it to reveal a delicate chain and glistening gem.

“I wore this on my wedding day,” she said, holding up a single diamond pendant strung on a thin gold thread. “I thought you might want it today.”

I nodded, too moved to speak as she fastened it around my neck. It felt like something being sealed. Not just the day, but a lineage. A belonging. Something I had felt from the beginning, only now it was official.

The necklace settled just above my collarbone, catching the light as I turned slightly toward the mirror. A quiet hush lingered behind me, the girls now murmuring softly about touch-ups and timing, but their voices faded as I lost myself in the reflection.

The pendant glinted against my skin. My hair was down—loose waves falling past my shoulders the way I wore it the first time we were here—and the gown, with its classic square neckline, its clean, architectural lines in perfect white silk, and a bodice folded into a subtle twist across my chest before the skirt pooled into a modernized train framed me like I was meant to be seen this way. I looked like someone worthy of the life I’d stepped into. Worthy of the name I’d already begun to wear in private.

Diamonds mean you’ve arrived, I remembered Diane saying seven years earlier.

And I had. I’d arrived exactly where I knew I would stand—even as a freshman at Holbrook. Back when Liam had only just begun to write our story that would take me longer to accept. Back when I had only just begun to admit how much I wanted that to be true.

My quiet reverie shattered with a sharp:
“Caroline Elizabeth!”

I blinked, my head whipping toward the voice. Chloe straightened from where she’d been adjusting the hem again, eyebrows lifted. Ava and Rachel both turned in slow motion.

My mother was standing near the table where all the bridal documents had been neatly stacked—officiant details, today’s timeline for the wedding planner, menus for the vendors— and in her hands was a crinkled piece of parchment she clearly hadn’t meant to find.

The official Italian seal was unmistakable, as was the date – and the signatures.

I hadn’t even noticed she’d finished with the buttons. Let alone helped herself to snooping through the wedding paperwork.

Mom looked between the document and me, then up at Diane, her expression twisted somewhere between scandalized and baffled. “Why is your marriage license dated SIX YEARS AGO!?,” she said, voice sharp and brimming with anger she failed to contain.

Diane’s eyes widened slightly, though not only with surprise alone but also amusement she tried to mask with a polite cough.

“Oh, because Liam and I eloped over spring break when I was 19,” I smiled sweetly, a quiet triumph behind my eyes.

My mother’s mouth dropped open. “You mean to tell me—”

“That Liam and I never ‘lived in sin’ together?,” I said. “I thought you’d be happy.” Maybe I sounded a little too sarcastic, but given the grief my mother had given me for years – and went as far as to nearly make a scene over the color of my gown, I let myself have this moment. The champagne probably helped.

“I knew it!” Chloe shrieked, hands flying to her hips. “This is a personal betrayal , Caroline. I’ll yell at you properly later, but for now, I need the SparkNotes version. Immediately. No edits. Now!”

I clasped her hands in mine and laughed, “Oh my God, Chloe, it was so romantic! It was just the two of us, in that small chapel carved into the cliffs just a couple towns over. Simple dress, lace veil – just us, a priest and a single witness. We kept it private.” I turned to Diane who had been more quiet and reserved given this new revelation, “We knew better than to cause any scandal.”

“Private?” Mom’s voice hitched. “You got married and didn’t tell your own mother?”

“We didn’t tell anyone,” I added sternly . “Liam ensured most of the documentation remained unregistered, until today. Also, sorry, Diane – Liam feigned being Catholic so he wouldn’t have to wait for a bishop’s approval”

Diane stepped forward then, gently plucking the paper from my mom’s hands. “Always impatient when he knows what he wants,” she shook her head. “Nothing we can do about it now other than ensure all paperwork is now properly filed. I believe we have a wedding to carry on with, ”

“And today is the public wedding,” I added softly, looking at my mother. “This is the one we wanted to share. The first ceremony was for us. I won’t apologize for it.”

My mom looked like she didn’t know whether to cry or throw something.

“Mom,” I said gently, “in the end, it’s the same outcome. We’re celebrating, we have a beautiful setting, a beautiful photo op—and I’m still vowing myself to the man I’ve always belonged to, and him to me. Does it really matter how we got here?”

That seemed to soften something in her. The flare of anger ebbed, replaced by something more maternal. She let out a slow breath and looked at me again—really looked.

“You’ve always done things your own way,” she murmured. “Even when I thought you weren’t ready.”

“I was,” I said. “For him, I was.”

A beat passed. Then she rolled her eyes heavenward, muttering something about needing more champagne.

And just like that, it passed.

Diane handed the paper back to me, her smile small and knowing. “I assume you’re still not planning to frame this one?”

“Not anywhere where guests will see it.” I said, returning it back with the other official documents.

A soft knock came at the door. Not rushed. Not hesitant. Just a quiet signal that the moment had come.

Chloe cracked it open, peeked out, then turned back toward me. “Your dad,” she mouthed, stepping aside.

He entered in full black tie—classic tuxedo, white pocket square, polished shoes. Somehow the tall and lanky man who taught me how to ride a bike and flip an omelet and speak up in class looked suddenly out of place in a room filled with florals and satin and perfume—but only for a breath.

He adjusting the cuff of his sleeve like he'd come in early and wasn’t sure whether to apologize or just keep moving. His eyes found mine—and then stalled.

He froze halfway into the suite. His mouth opened, then closed again, his gaze held me like it was the first time he saw me. His shoulders rose, then fell on a long, quiet exhale.

“You're… all grown up,” he said finally, the words rougher than usual. “And somehow, it still feels like yesterday I was buckling you into a car seat.”

The lump in my throat rose fast, sharp and sudden.

“Dad,” I whispered, unable to keep the smile from tugging at my lips. “You’re going to make me cry before I even get down the aisle.”

He let out a soft huff of laughter, blinking hard. “Don’t you dare. I promised myself I wouldn’t lose it in front of the Barnettes.” He stepped closer, took both of my hands in his, and studied me again—eyes filled with something proud and steady. “Are you ready, kiddo?”

I nodded, taking my bouquet from Chloe and threading my arm through my Dad’s. “I’ve never been more sure of anything.”

“Then let’s go give you the sendoff you deserve,” he said, his voice thick with emotion he barely kept in check. “And make that man of yours sweat a little while we’re at it.”


3 Years Prior

The path curved gently behind the chapel, bordered by late-summer roses, hydrangeas just beginning to blush, and black-eyed Susans nodding in the breeze. This wasn’t a usual route we took when Liam visited. Maybe that should have been the first sign.

He had suggested a walk, nothing elaborate—just a break between study sessions and dinner plans. But now, as we moved deeper into the garden, something about the quiet between us began to shift.

He wasn’t wearing anything special, a soft navy sweater, sleeves pushed to his forearms, and perfectly cut jeans that still managed to look intentional. Yet there was a calmness to his posture, a deliberateness in his stride.

When we reached the edge of the stone path, he stopped, letting his gaze drift toward the chapel steps. The same ones I used to climb to my own personal sanctuary. The same ones he first saw me on.

He didn’t say anything right away. I watched his profile as he looked toward that familiar façade, his expression softened by something that felt almost reverent.

“I think about it all the time,” he said finally. “The first time I saw you—right there.”

His voice was quiet. Like he was telling it to the building itself. “You were wearing that pale blue dress and that ridiculous strand of pearls. You were squinting into the sun like you weren’t sure where you belonged.”

I smiled faintly. “And you were staring.”

He glanced at me then, his mouth tugging into the shadow of a grin. “I was.”

He turned fully toward me, something catching in his throat before he spoke again. “I remember thinking—finally.”
The word settled between us, low and certain. “Finally someone I couldn’t walk past.”

My heart stuttered – I knew the story, but it still had a stupid effect on me. Our story would always be contradictory and duplicitous. As the time went on, how it started became less and less important — especially when what we had now was perfect.

“I knew it then,” he said, voice low. “And I didn’t marry you in Italy because I wanted to keep it secret—I did it because I couldn’t wait .”

I blinked back something sharp and rising in my chest, remembering the wind that day, the lace veil I’d worn, the way he’d held my hands so tightly in that tiny chapel carved into the cliffs.

“But that was instinct,” he said. “That was possession. I couldn’t imagine another day where you weren’t mine in every single way.”

He chuckled, maybe more to himself than to me as he reminisced, “Remember how you teased me because I didn’t know Italian,” he went on, his voice warming. “But I still found a priest who spoke just enough English and wasn’t too pious to accept a bribe.”

I laughed, even as my pulse spiked.

He stepped closer. “We kept it ours. Because that was the only way it could be then. But I promised you, and I think it’s time.”

My breath caught. He stepped back and without ceremony but with devastating gravity, he dropped to one knee.

I barely had time to gasp before he was opening a velvet box. Inside, nestled against the silk lining, was a solitaire diamond that made my breath hitch. A solitaire diamond set in platinum. Flawless. Timeless. A Harry Winston piece that felt both ancestral and deeply, intimately personal. The kind of ring that looked like it belonged to someone who already carried a legacy — like it was made for me.

“Now I want the world to see the finished version,” he paused, “I promised you I’d give you this: A real proposal. The ring. The moment. I’ve waited years to do it right.”

Liam looked up at me, and for a second, I forgot how to breathe.

“I never doubted you,” he said. “Not from the moment I saw you on those steps. Not through the fights. Not when you tried to run or push me away. Even then, I knew.”

His voice lowered, the words more vow than question. “You were always going to wear my name. Now I want you to wear it in every room we walk into. On every document. Every headline. Every breath. I’m not asking you to marry me, Caroline, it was never a question for either of us.”

“But I’ve been waiting to make it right,” he said. “To give you the proposal you deserve. Not because I need to ask, but because I don’t want to hide my commitment to you or that you belong to me in every possible way.”

I swallowed hard, blinking fast, the garden blurring at the edges.

“So say yes to pomp and circumstance but more importantly, say yes to me and wearing my name in public like you have in private for all these years. Say yes so the world will know I’m yours just as you’re mine.”

My throat closed and for a moment, I just stood there, trembling.

Then I nodded—helpless, joyful, completely his—and whispered, “Yes.”

When he stood, I didn’t give him time to slide the ring on my finger. I threw my arms around him instead, and whispered yes against his shoulder, against his throat, again and again.


Present

The music shifted.

It was subtle at first—just a change in key, a new swell of strings drifting down the aisle like the first breath of something sacred. Chloe appeared beneath the archway in her pale pink dress, bouquet cradled in front of her, chin lifted with the kind of regal mischief only she could pull off while walking in heels. She didn’t look at the guests. She looked at me and winked, like this wasn’t a ceremony so much as a coronation—and I was the queen.

The moment she reached the front, the next note rose. And the world held its breath.
My hand rested lightly in the crook of my father’s arm, fingers curled into the fabric of his tuxedo jacket. He glanced down at me with a look I hadn’t seen since I was a little girl asking if he’d walk beside me for something I hadn’t yet dared to name. He didn’t say anything— not right away. Just pressed a palm over mine.

“You ready, kid?” he asked, voice gruff but steady. I nodded. “I think the question is, ‘are you?’”
“Not a chance,” he said. “But I knew this day would come and I’m happy for you both.” I laughed softly, eyes already scanning the altar.
The aisle stretched like a dream before me—layered in ivory petals, framed by sprays of white blooms spilling from stone urns and tucked between lavender stalks and feathery greenery that softened the stone. The sea glinted behind the altar, far below, waves catching sun and shadow as they lapped against the cliffs like the heartbeat of something ancient.

Every head had turned. But I only saw him.

Liam stood at the center of it all, beneath an arbor heavy with roses and winding vines, a cathedral of white blooms that bowed toward him like gravity knew where power belonged.

His tuxedo was perfectly cut, but it was his face that held me still. Composed but undone. Eyes locked on mine like he’d never seen anything so absolute.

“Okay,” I whispered, breath catching as the first step landed. “Now this feels real.”

My father chuckled low under his breath. “Smile and walk slow with me. All eyes are on you, make it count.”

Each step echoed with something bigger than nerves. It wasn’t anticipation—it was recognition. I had dreamed of this moment more times than I could count, imagined every petal and promise, but the reality of it landed differently. He was real. He was waiting. And I wasn’t just walking toward him.

I was walking into what had already been mine.

The breeze lifted the hem of my veil as we stepped beneath the arch, my dress trailing behind me like smoke. I didn’t release my father’s arm until we reached the front, and when I did, he leaned in close, pressed a kiss to my temple, and said, low and full of something I wouldn’t let myself name:

“You’ll always be my little girl, but damn if he doesn’t look like he deserves you.” Then he placed my hand into Liam’s, “Take good care of her, son.”
And with Liam’s fingers closed around mine, the world sharpened into clarity. The moment I touched him, I breathed again. I hadn’t realized I’d been holding it.

“You’re breathtaking,” he murmured, his voice low enough for only me. “And I’m very lucky I’m not the crying type.”

I smiled coyly. “Don’t make me kick your shins – I bet Chloe that you’d cry and I’m not about to lose on my wedding day.”

He chuckled under his breath but wiped a non-existent tear for me anyway.

The officiant began, but I barely heard the words. The ceremony passed like light through a prism—reflected and refracted into color, music, and warmth. My mother dabbed at her eyes. Diane sat tall, chin lifted with barely concealed pride. The sea below glistened like it, too, was bearing witness.

Then came the vows. We hadn’t written them together. We hadn’t needed to.

The hush that fell over the guests didn’t matter. Nothing did except his eyes—dark and certain.

Liam’s voice was low, but steady as he cleared his throat to speak first.

“Caroline, I don’t come to you with conditions. I come to you as a man who has built his life around a single truth—that you are my constant. I vow to keep you, protect you, and love you with everything I am. I vow loyalty and to honor you, I vow honesty in all things great and

small - including that you snore and hog the blankets.” There was a pause for quiet chuckles, “It’s a cute snore,” he stage whispered. Once the respectful silence returned, “My love, this isn’t a new beginning. This is a declaration of everything I’ve always provided and always will provide for as long as I shall live.”

My chest tightened, emotion pressing sharp against my ribs. When it was my turn, I didn’t falter.
“William,” I said with a quiet smile, “you changed the shape of my life. You saw the woman I could become long before I ever believed in her myself. You didn’t wait for me to catch up
—because you already knew. Even when I felt small, you saw strength. Even when I faltered, you never did. Quietly, relentlessly, you made me yours. And I finally understand what it means to belong.”

I swallowed.

“You’ve never let me go, not once, and I plead that you never will. So today, I vow this: Loyalty, not just in name, but in every room your reputation precedes you; Respect in public and to save any challenge for private, I vow my whole self to you, keeping nothing from you. I vow always to meet you where you are, no matter how high the cost. I vow to build something grand and beautiful and ours. And finally, I vow to love you always: through sickness and health, through riches and poverty, through victory and defeat, and to be yours for as long as we both shall live.”

A hush fell. The kind of silence that wasn’t empty—but full. Reverent. Swept with salt air and soft tremors.

The rings were exchanged—platinum bands, timeless, the same set we had exchanged six years earlier. Mine slid on with ease. His, gleaming under the sun, was still engraved on the inside with the words I already knew by heart: Only Ever Yours and a date no one else would recognize.

When the words were spoken—husband and wife—they felt less like a declaration and more like an unveiling. Like the truth had been living between us for years, and now the world was finally being let in.

He kissed me with restraint. Just enough to meet the moment. Just enough to anchor it.

Then he leaned in, brushing his lips just beneath my ear, and whispered, “Mine. All mine. And now—publicly, irreversibly—yours.”

The clink of silverware quieted as Michael stood, raising his champagne glass with the kind of theatrical gravity that made half the guests brace themselves. Liam was lounging with his arm around the back of my chair, I leaned into him as if it were the most natural thing in the world.

“I’ll keep this short,” he said, eyes glinting. “Mostly because if I say anything too heartfelt, my mother will cry, and my father will pretend not to.”

A ripple of laughter passed through the tables.

“For those of you who don’t know me, I’m Michael Barnette—Liam’s older brother. The one with a longer rap sheet and significantly better looking. And for those of you who do know me, I apologize in advance because what you heard about me is probably true.”

Another light wave of amusement. Michael smiled, slow and self-deprecating.

“I’ve known Liam since before he had language, which—if you know Liam—was probably the most frustrating time of his life – not being able to argue properly must’ve killed him. For the rest of us, it was the most peaceful time of our lives.”

Laughter again. Michael let it land before continuing.

“I always knew he’d end up with someone impressive. Someone terrifyingly smart. Someone who could match him word for word, step for step, and still somehow soften all that sharpness without ever dulling it.”

He paused, gaze turning towards me now. There was something quieter in it—older, steadier. “I didn’t know it’d be her at first. But then—”
He turned to Liam, tilting his head.

“Then you introduced her like she was a footnote. And I knew. I knew immediately, because you only downplay the things that matter most.”

He looked back at me. “Caroline, darling, you are smart and beautiful and terribly witty; you deserve only the best man in your life, and I won’t stop until I find out how this mistake ever happened.” The guest laughed as Liam glared, “But, love, you were part of this family well before the paperwork caught up. And I mean that in the best way—not the terrifying, mafia- adjacent way we sometimes imply.”

Smiles broke across the room. Michael raised his glass slightly higher. My cheeks hurt from smiling.

“To Liam—who somehow convinced a woman like Caroline to put up with him for life. And to Caroline—who made a brilliant, irrevocable mistake we’re all very grateful for.”

A beat. Then, softer: “To a marriage that was inevitable, long before we were invited to witness it.”

He smiled then. Genuine. No pretense. “To the Barnettes.”

Glasses clinked across candlelight and crystal as my husband cupped my cheek and kissed me.


The opening notes of At Last floated into the warm night air, that first swell of strings melting into Etta’s voice like honey poured over candlelight.

We’d chosen it together—unanimously, without even pretending to argue—because the irony was too rich, and the truth too sharp. The first time he saw me, the first time I told him I loved him, the first time we exchanged vows and now here, publicly joined in front of friends and family.

Liam’s hand closed over mine as we stepped onto the dance floor, his tux pressed in perfect contrast against my gown. He didn’t say anything at first. Just looked at me with that expression he wore so rarely in public—like I was everything he’d ever wanted, and now the rest of the world was catching up.

“You ready, Mrs. Barnette?” he asked under his breath.

“More than ready,” I whispered, resting my palm against his chest.

His arm curved around my waist, guiding me gently into the first slow steps. The silk of my dress rustled faintly with every movement. The breeze off the sea threaded through the arches of the courtyard, catching the soft lights strung overhead, making them flicker like stars. All around us, the guests faded to a blur.

“At last,” Liam murmured, letting the lyric settle between us. “She says it like a revelation.” “I say it like a promise,” I replied, my voice light. “Besides, you agreed.”
“I agreed because you were right,” he said, and his thumb traced the curve of my spine through the silk. “It had to be this one.”

I tilted my head slightly, watching his expression. “Because we earned it?” “No,” he said simply. “Because it’s our story.”
That shut me up for a beat.

“You can’t just say things like that,” I muttered, half-laughing, half-flushed. “You married me. You knew the terms.”
I narrowed my eyes at him. “Romantic honesty?”

“Relentless devotion,” he said smoothly. “And well-timed compliments.”

I exhaled a breathy laugh, leaning closer. “Careful. I might fall in love with you.”

“Might?” He leaned in, his voice a dark tease at my ear. “Sweethear, it’s a little late if you’re just figuring this out.”

We moved in slow rhythm, the music wrapping around us like ribbon. I rested my head briefly against his shoulder, breathing in the familiar scent of him—something clean and

sharp, threaded with warmth and something darker I could never quite name. His heartbeat was steady beneath my hand.

Liam’s hand slid just slightly lower on my waist as we moved through the slow curve of the song, his mouth near my temple, his posture too perfect for anyone but me to notice how amused he was.

“So,” he murmured, voice soft enough that only I could hear it over the lyrics, “did Chloe buy the tear?”

I bit back a smile, tilting my head just enough to meet his eyes. “You mean the microscopic, totally invisible one you pretended to wipe away at the altar?”

“It was subtle ,” he said, mock-wounded. “Elegant.” “It was imaginary .”
“She was standing off to the side and practically behind you,” he said, deadpan. “I figured your veil and the flowers over the archway would obstruct her view”

I laughed, pressing closer. “I hate to break it to you, but she leaned over to Ava and said, ‘that man wouldn’t cry if his Aston Martin caught fire.’ ”

Liam exhaled a quiet sound that could have passed for a laugh. “Then I’m afraid your bet is officially lost.”

“Tragic,” I sighed. “I really thought you’d come through for me.”

He shook his head, feigning offense, “What exactly was the wager anyway?” “Only a bottle of Dom,” I sighed.
He leaned in, his mouth brushing just beneath my ear. “I’m sure I’ll find a way to make it up to you tonight.” My smile deepened, my fingers tightening around his shoulder as the music curled around us.

Liam glanced up briefly as we turned again, his hand tightening slightly at my waist. “Speaking of Chloe,” he murmured.

I followed his gaze—and there they were. Chloe, still glowing in her pale-pink bridesmaid dress, disappearing discreetly toward the back of the villa, her hand wrapped confidently around Ethan’s tie.

“Oh my God,” I said under my breath. “Is she dragging him into the lemon grove?” “Looks that way,” Liam deadpanned. “You’d think they’d wait until dessert.”
I snorted. “Should we send them a bottle of champagne?” “Let’s hold off,” he murmured. “Another six years, minimum.”

“Agreed.”

The song continued as we fell into a comfortable silence for a few bars until his voice brushed low against my cheek again, so low only I could hear it. “I told you once, remember?”

I lifted my chin slightly. “Told me what?” “That someday, the world would see it.”
“Hmm?” I asked, knowing exactly what he meant.

He looked down at me, eyes burning in that quiet, contained way that always made my knees a little weak.

“That you were mine,” he said. “And that I was always—only—yours.”

The song tapered, the final chord blooming before it faded. Applause swept around us, but it barely registered. I hadn’t taken my eyes off him.

We fell quiet again as the dance floor filled with guests and music swelled around us. The sound of the sea rose just beyond the cliffs, adding a rhythm of its own—ancient, grounding. I caught a glimpse of Diane watching us, her expression full of pride. Michael hovered near the bar, already hatching something that would either end in laughter or scandal. My dad was beaming at our table, raising a glass toward the Senator as they clinked flutes like old conspirators. And my mother—my mother dabbed at her eyes behind her program, pretending it was the wind.

It should’ve been overwhelming. But it wasn’t. Because all I could feel was Liam’s hand in mine.

My father took it next, for the brief but treasured father-daughter dance. “Remember when we used to dance to this song in the living room,” he asked. “Now look at you. All grown up and happy. I don't remember a time you looked so happy.”

Diane and Liam shared a turn, her hand resting lightly on his shoulder, the other in his. They didn’t speak much—just smiled, something quiet and mutual passing between them. When the music paused, she smoothed the front of his jacket and whispered something in his ear that made him shake his head, grinning.

After that, we were back in each other’s arms, spinning through the night—drinking champagne, dancing until our cheeks flushed and the silk of my gown picked up dust from the old stone tiles. My heels disappeared somewhere between dessert and the second chorus of “Fly Me to the Moon,” and Liam just leaned down, laughing into my ear, “Already barefoot, isn’t there something else that’s supposed to go along with that?” I smacked his arm for good measure.

We didn’t notice when the cake was cleared, or when the candles began to burn low. But I did notice the moment Liam’s fingers slipped down my spine and curled around my waist, his

eyes catching mine in a way that made my breath stutter. “You ready?” he asked, low and gleaming.
“For what?” I teased.

He didn’t answer. Just grabbed me, caveman style – one arm hooked under my knees, the other over my back, slinging me against his chest like I weighed nothing at all.

“Liam! You barbarian!” I squealed, pushing a hand into his back to prop myself up as he started walking straight off the dance floor, past our guests, down the lantern-lit path toward the villa.

“They’ve had enough,” he said with mock gravity. “I’m reclaiming what’s mine.”

Laughter broke behind us—clapping, cheers, someone whooping like they’d waited all night for this—and I buried my face in his neck, giddy and glowing and wholly undone. I waved back cheekily, laughing too hard to have any shame.

I looked beyond the reception area, just before we disappeared past the last stone arch. The sea was still out there, black and eternal. The altar remained tucked in moonlight. The breeze played with my hair one last time before Liam set me down, opened the villa door, and pulled me inside.

He closed it behind us. And just like that, the world fell away—until there was only him, only me, only us.

Chapter 79: Epilogue 2: Sunday, Softly.




The light fell golden through the open doors, softening everything it touched—the marble floors, the weathered beams overhead, the half-drunk espresso on the table beside him. Liam sat on the stone steps of the villa’s sun-drenched courtyard, shirt sleeves rolled to the elbows, a small smear of Claire’s raspberry jam drying just beneath the collar of his white linen shirt. He hadn’t noticed it yet. Caroline had. But she didn’t tell him.

Instead, she watched from the kitchen doorway, arms folded, hip resting against the frame, hair a little tousled from the wind that crept in off the sea. There was a time when she would’ve straightened it. Now—now she liked when he saw her soft.

Alexander had fallen asleep on Liam’s chest not long ago, limbs heavy and flushed with the stubborn heat of a three-year-old who’d refused a nap until he crashed mid-sentence. His little head nestled beneath Liam’s chin, damp curls stuck to his temple. One hand gripped his father’s shirt, still in a loose fist.

Claire sat cross-legged in front of them, blue eyes narrowed in fierce concentration as she painted Liam’s toes with sparkly purple polish.

“She said she’s making you beautiful,” Caroline said lightly, stepping down the stairs. Her voice carried the lilt of a smile, something softer than amusement. Something like adoration.

Liam didn’t look up. “Tell your mother I’ve always been beautiful.” Claire answered for him, without pause. “You’re not done yet, Daddy.”
Caroline crouched beside them, sliding her fingers through Claire’s brown hair to tuck it behind her ear. “Use the glitter on the last two. He needs to match.”

Claire nodded, completely serious.

Liam shifted slightly, careful not to wake Alexander. “You’re both conspiring against me.” “You love it.”

“I tolerate it,” he murmured, but the edge of his mouth was already pulling upward. “Besides, the last time I said no, she cried and told me I didn’t believe in her art.”

Claire huffed. “That was one time.”

“And she’s five,” Caroline added, kissing the top of her daughter’s head. “She’s supposed to be dramatic.”

Claire pointed a tiny painted brush at her mother. “Daddy says I get it from you.”

“Oh does he?” Caroline grinned, brushing a thumb along Liam’s cheek as she leaned in to kiss him. It was a soft, slow kiss—lingering in the way that said: I know you, I see you, I still want you. Even here, with a child drooling on your chest and a second one glitter-bombing your toes.

He kissed her back like it was nothing new. Like he’d never once stopped worshipping her. When she pulled back, Liam tilted his head. “You know this is the exact life I promised you.”
“You promised me power and ruin,” she teased. “Not Conca dei Marini and purple nail polish.”

“Same thing, in the end.”

She laughed, low and warm, the sound of it curling around his ribs. She traced her hand through Alexander’s curls before settling into the curve of Liam’s side. He adjusted, letting her rest against his shoulder, the boy’s soft weight pressing them all together.

Claire finished the last toe with a triumphant flourish and declared, “Daddy is a princess now.”

“King, sweetheart,” Liam said without missing a beat. “If you’re going to crown me, do it properly.”

Caroline pressed her face into his shoulder to hide her laugh, and Liam, despite the glitter on his feet and the child on his chest, felt—once again, unshakably—that he had already won.

Not the world. Just her. And everything they’d made.

That night, the villa quieted slowly, the way the sea did after dusk—soft, steady, the chaos of the day pulled under by moonlight. Somewhere down the hall, a white noise machine hummed in Claire’s room, and Alexander’s stuffed lion had finally surrendered to the foot of his bed after a prolonged bedtime negotiation that would’ve made the UN proud.

Caroline moved through the kitchen barefoot, a silk wrap tied loosely at her waist, her wine glass only half-full, though the night made her feel slightly drunk. Not from the wine—but from the stillness. The sweetness of peace. Of being wanted but not needed. For the next eight hours, no one would cry for her, no one would beg her to watch them somersault, or demand different toast because this one looked at them funny.

She stepped out onto the terrace where the breeze carried in the scent of lemon trees and the low hush of distant waves. Liam was already there—seated with a book open but untouched in his lap, gaze half-lidded as if he’d been waiting for her to reappear. He didn’t say anything when she walked over. He just held out a hand.

She gave him hers, letting herself be pulled into the space between his legs. His hands slid beneath her robe—warm palms against bare skin—and his mouth found the inside of her wrist, then lower, where her pulse beat slow and steady under his touch.

“We survived another day,” she murmured into his hair. “Barely.”

He smirked, kissing her collarbone. “You say that like you didn’t thrive on every tantrum.” “I thrive despite every tantrum.”
His eyes lifted to hers. “You were made for this.”

She smiled faintly, brushing her fingers through the back of his hair. “For bedtime battles and glitter in the grout?”

“For being a mother.” His voice dropped, more intimate now. “For being mine.”

She kissed him softly, unhurried, her breath catching a little when he deepened it. One hand gripped her waist, the other slid up her spine, and he didn’t stop until she sighed into him, her body melting against his with the kind of trust only years of love—and ruin—and rebuilding could shape.

He pulled back just enough to murmur, “You’re getting off birth control.”

Her brows lifted, more amused than surprised. “Oh? Decided that, have you?”

“I’ll call your doctor in the morning.” He kissed the corner of her mouth, a little too proud, a little too sure. “I’ve been patient.”

Caroline tilted her head. “And what if I’m perfectly happy with two?” “You’re not.”
“Liam—”

He cut her off with another kiss, more claiming this time, more final. Then: “You always said you were open to three.”

“That was before Alexander. Before Claire’s purple nail polish permanently stained your Birkenstocks.”

“And yet here you are,” he said, brushing his thumb across her lower lip, “still wanting more.”

Caroline stilled. Because he wasn’t wrong. Because somewhere deep down, she did want it— wanted another little life born of them, another heartbeat under her hand. Not out of need.
Not because they were incomplete. But because they were whole enough to stretch. To open wider.

“I hate how well you know me,” she whispered.

“No, you don’t.” His voice was quiet now. Reverent. “You love it.”

He leaned in again, this time slower, and kissed her like the promise was already growing inside her. Like the next child—whoever they would be—was already part of this quiet, sacred night. Part of the future they were still, even now, building with soft hands and fierce love.

And when she finally kissed him back, there was no trace of resistance—just the low, molten surrender of a woman who knew exactly who she belonged to, and what they’d make next.

